mers in the Murchison meteorite (https://www.nature.com/articles/28306)\n  45. The science from asteroid sample return missions (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.22182)\n  46. EXTRATERRESTRIAL AMINO ACIDS IDENTIFIED IN AN AGGREGATE SAMPLE RETURNED (https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2024/pdf/1640.pdf)\n  47. Enantiomeric excess of amino acids in interstellar ice analogues-asymmetric photolysis of precursors by circularly polarized UV light (https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/517/4/6112/6754748)\n  48. Photonenergy\u2010Controlled Symmetry Breaking with Circularly Polarized Light - Meinert - 2014 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.201307855)\n  49. ENANTIOMERIC EXCESSES INDUCED IN AMINO ACIDS BY ULTRAVIOLET CIRCULARLY POLARIZED LIGHT IRRADIATION OF EXTRATERRESTRIAL ICE ANALOGS: A POSSIBLE SOURCE OF ASYMMETRY FOR PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/788/1/79)\n  50. Anisotropy-Guided Enantiomeric Enhancement in AlanineUsing Far-UV Circularly Polarized Light (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-015-9413-x)\n  51. Homochirality: a prerequisite or consequence of life? (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.12850)\n  52. NASA study reproduces origins of life on ocean floor (https://phys.org/news/2019-02-nasa-life-ocean-floor.html)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct ten condition-specific pathways to alanine, explicitly distinguishing L-alanine from its D coproduct. These include Strecker chemistry, CO2-dependent cyanide chemistry of pyruvate, five mineral/metal-dependent alternatives including oxime reduction, and two thiol-dependent sugar routes. Several pathways are experimentally distinct catalyst variants, not independent reaction mechanisms. Atmospheric discharge supplies Strecker precursors; mineral-mediated CO2 reduction supplies a conditional, dilute pyruvate source. Glyceraldehyde, formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and hydroxylamine are explicitly external feedstocks where their production is not established here. All synthesis pathways produce racemic alanine. A separate, explicitly conditional circularly polarized light module represents possible modest enrichment, not demonstrated production of enantiopure L-alanine. Its photoproduct representation is marked speculative. The network is complete as a dependency graph, not as a demonstrated integrated geochemical process: concentration, mineral regeneration and terrestrial chiral selection remain gaps. Neutral formulas represent pH-dependent aqueous species; null environment_formed denotes external supply or formation in multiple environments. Mineral electron reservoirs are identified in agents, but their unresolved spent phases are not represented. Roles labeled intermediate also include side products because the schema has no byproduct role.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanimine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"(S)-alpha-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"(R)-alpha-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid; predominantly pyruvate under alkaline conditions\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid oxime; E/Z configuration unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde; feedstock stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid; lactate side-product pool, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"native iron; Fe0\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe^2+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proton; formal aqueous proton equivalent\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ethylamine; candidate alanine photodecarboxylation product\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H7N/c1-2-3/h2-3H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge production and aqueous capture of Strecker precursors\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K bulk gas-water apparatus; the discharge is nonthermal\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Electrical discharge through an anoxic, locally reducing gas mixture with water circulation and aqueous trapping. H2 may also be present initially. The node summarizes selected products of a complex discharge mixture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Energetic gas-phase fragmentation and recombination generate cyanide and carbonyl compounds, followed by dissolution in water. H2 is included as a possible net dehydrogenation product; this is not an elementary reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1953) and Johnson et al. (2008) support discharge-driven amino-acid precursor production and subsequent Strecker chemistry. The route requires a sufficiently reducing local atmosphere and does not establish a globally methane-rich early atmosphere. Product ratios and precursor concentrations cannot be inferred from this coarse-grained node.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible acetaldehyde-ammonia condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Moderately alkaline, ammonia-containing water; approximately pH 8-10 is a proposed environmental window rather than a claimed optimum. The imine is transient and need not accumulate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, followed by reversible carbinolamine dehydration to ethanimine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), consistent with Miller's interpretation of discharge-derived alanine (Miller, 1953). Water disfavors imine accumulation, but cyanide trapping can pull the coupled equilibrium forward. Low ammonia activity instead increases competing cyanohydrin chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Nonenantioselective cyanide capture of ethanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous HCN/CN- and NH3/NH4+ equilibria; no chiral catalyst or chiral irradiation is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the planar imine or iminium carbon from either face, generating equal amounts of the two aminonitrile enantiomers under achiral conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The aminonitrile pathway is supported by classical Strecker synthesis and its prebiotic applications (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1953). The separate stereoisomer nodes prevent racemic synthesis from being misrepresented as selective L-alanine production.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic aqueous fluid providing proton catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K proposed warm hydrolysis stage\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"A separate acidic stage, approximately pH 1-3, is proposed after aminonitrile formation. Residence time and natural conversion at these conditions are uncertain. Neutral formulas represent the corresponding protonated species in acidic solution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydration proceeds through an amide and then carboxylic-acid formation, releasing ammonia/ammonium. In the absence of racemization, each aminonitrile retains its alpha-carbon configuration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis completes established Strecker alanine synthesis (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1953). Laboratory hydrolysis or analytical workup must not be equated with spontaneous quantitative conversion in a natural pool. Joining alkaline precursor formation to sufficiently effective hydrolysis is an environmental bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-mediated CO2 reduction and C-C assembly to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a candidate Fe-Ni catalytic mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Pressurized H2/CO2 aqueous system; exact experimental pressure is not specified in the supplied report\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline, anoxic water-rock reaction. This node represents a multistep product network, not concerted reduction of three CO2 molecules. Cooling is required before the lower-temperature amination modules.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-assisted hydrogen activation, carbon-oxide reduction and surface C-C coupling produce a small pyruvate fraction within a broader mixture of reduced carbon compounds.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) reported pyruvate up to approximately 10 micromolar in an experimental Fe/Ni-mineral CO2-reduction study. That maximum is not assigned here as a guaranteed awaruite-specific yield. The mechanism and concentration bridge to millimolar amination experiments remain unresolved. No pore concentration or thermophoretic enrichment is assumed to have been demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of pyruvate to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K candidate surface window; stage-specific conditions require verification\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with dissolved inorganic carbon maintained\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanide, ammonia/ammonium and CO2/bicarbonate must coexist. This is a coarse-grained reaction sequence including hydrolysis, not an assertion that every intermediate hydrolyzes rapidly at room temperature.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CO2 enables the cyanide/ammonia reaction of the alpha-ketoacid through the hydantoin/N-carbamoyl-type chemistry described in the supplied research summary. Ring processing, hydrolysis and carbon loss are collapsed into the endpoint reaction. NH3 and CO2 occur on both sides to represent exchange and regeneration within the net sequence; no atom mapping is asserted.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support the alpha-ketoacid-to-alpha-amino-acid endpoint and the essential role of CO2. The supplied summary does not establish enough intermediate-specific detail to assign a unique hydantoin structure or an exact microscopic sequence here. This route is not modeled as simple cyanide reduction of pyruvate. Cyanide supply, pyruvate supply and hydrolytic processing remain separate constraints.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Fresh FeS-mediated reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Freshly precipitated FeS: consumable mineral reductant and reactive surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm laboratory benchmark; natural interfacial pressure is not established\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 9-10, substantial ammonia excess and fresh reactive FeS. Related Fe-based experiments use roughly 0.375-1.5 M ammonia. Protons denote buffered proton equivalents, not an acidic bulk solution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbonyl-ammonia condensation followed by reduction gives alanine. The organic transformation requires two reducing equivalents per alanine; FeS supplies a finite redox inventory and its spent solid composition is not uniquely assigned.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) demonstrated reductive amination using fresh iron minerals. The approximately 50% yield cited in the supplied report comes from a secondary summary and is not treated as a verified alanine-specific value. FeS is not assumed to be a indefinitely regenerating catalyst. High ammonia demand and the dilute upstream pyruvate source limit integration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Mixed-valence iron hydroxide-mediated reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide material with approximately 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm experimental benchmark\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 10, excess ammonia and incubation up to approximately 72 h. The mixed-valence solid is a finite reactive redox reservoir. Its natural preparation must be anoxic; molecular oxygen is not supplied.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The mixed-valence surface promotes pyruvate-ammonia conversion and electron transfer. An imine-like intermediate is plausible, but the detailed mineral-mediated mechanism is unresolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. (2019) reported the strongest alanine outcome near pH 10 and a 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III) ratio, with approximately 70% alanine and 30% lactate among detected dissolved organics. Pure ferrous and pure ferric end members did not reproduce this result. An anoxic source of the appropriate mixed-valence material is required and is not established by this node.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination by electrochemically pre-reduced FeS\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Electrochemically pre-reduced FeS containing reactive Fe0 domains; finite reducing reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm experimental benchmark\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported amination conditions include pH 9.6, 0.5 M ammonia, approximately 5 mM pyruvate and 48 h. Mineral preparation involved approximately -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days; this energy-intensive preparation is not hidden as spontaneous catalysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduced iron domains deliver electrons to the carbonyl-ammonia reaction system, producing racemic alanine and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) observed nearly complete pyruvate conversion under the reported laboratory conditions. Establishing and maintaining an equivalent natural reducing potential is an open geochemical requirement. The millimolar pyruvate and concentrated ammonia benchmark is not directly supplied by rxn_005.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni0/H2-catalyzed reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible Ni0 surface; supported nickel is the laboratory benchmark\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-8 and 24 h. Screening used approximately 30 mM pyruvate. Lower-ammonia experiments included approximately 6 mM NH3. Natural native-nickel surfaces need not reproduce the activity of engineered supported nickel.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel activates H2 and catalyzes hydrogenation of pyruvate-derived imine species. H2, rather than unaccounted mineral oxidation, supplies the net reducing equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated alanine formation under these conditions and useful conversion at lower ammonia concentrations than most Fe-mineral experiments. Their low-ammonia nickel yield was reported relative to ammonia, not pyruvate. Native Ni availability, passivation, H2 pressure and pyruvate concentration remain limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy/H2-mediated reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy; Aletai meteorite material is the experimental analogue\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Reactive Fe-Ni alloy surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral to mildly alkaline water containing pyruvate and ammonia. The reported meteorite material contained approximately 18 mol% Ni. Metal delivery is distinguished from delivery of preformed alanine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Accessible nickel-bearing alloy sites activate hydrogen and mediate the same net imine hydrogenation as the supported-Ni route, with different surface composition and competing reactions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reported approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate with Aletai meteorite material. This is a directly tested, geochemically distinct catalyst-source alternative, not a new fundamental reaction mechanism. Weathering and passivation of naturally delivered alloy may substantially reduce activity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Condensation of pyruvate with hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K candidate mild aqueous window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hydroxylamine-containing pyruvate solution. The stated environmental window is proposed, not an exact reconstruction of the published amino-acid experiment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine adds to the ketone and loses water to form a pyruvate oxime. E/Z oxime configurations are not resolved in this coarse-grained node.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support hydroxylamine/Fe0 conversion of ketoacids to amino acids through oxime chemistry. Hydroxylamine is explicitly an external starting material here because a sufficient prebiotic source is not established by the supplied evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Stoichiometric Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native Fe0, explicitly represented as a consumed reactant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K candidate mild aqueous window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous contact with reactive iron metal. Buffering supplies proton equivalents. Surface passivation and competing corrosion can reduce productive electron transfer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the C=N bond and cleavage of the N-O bond convert the oxime into alanine. The formal transformation requires four electrons per oxime; oxidation of Fe0 to Fe2+ provides the corresponding reducing equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) demonstrated amino-acid production after adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 to their ketoacid chemistry. Unlike two-electron imine reduction, oxime-to-amine conversion requires a four-electron budget. No chiral control is established, and natural hydroxylamine supply remains the principal route-specific gap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-enhanced alanine formation from glyceraldehyde and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally tested organic thiol promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Near ambient; approximately 300 K surface analogue\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Long-lived anoxic glyceraldehyde/ammonium solution; reaction times of approximately 60 days. The organic thiol is an externally supplied promoter whose prebiotic production is not included.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A proposed carbonyl-rearrangement pathway involving methylglyoxal-type, hemithioacetal and imine chemistry couples internal oxidation and reduction to amino-acid formation. Unverified individual transient structures are not promoted to molecule nodes.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1985) observed approximately 0.6% alanine after 60 days with thiol, compared with approximately 0.1% without thiol. This supports the net transformation, not a uniquely established elementary mechanism or selective L production. Low yield, long residence time, side reactions and thiol supply strongly constrain environmental implementation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-dependent alanine formation from formose substrates\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally tested organic thiol promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 5.2 and 35 days; formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and ammonia/ammonium present together. This is the observed mixed-substrate reaction, not an assumed isolated glyceraldehyde synthesis step.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"C1-C2 carbonyl chemistry builds reactive C3 intermediates, followed by thiol-assisted rearrangement and nitrogen incorporation. The overall transformation is internally redox-balanced; no external H2 requirement is introduced.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1998) reported approximately 1.8% alanine on a formaldehyde basis and essentially none without thiol. This is a distinct tested feedstock pathway from rxn_014, although their downstream chemistry may overlap. A separate, selectively producing formose aldol step is not invented because it is unnecessary to represent the experiment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing nickel-mediated pyruvate hydrogenation to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible Ni0 or meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy surface; alternative catalysts, not a required mixture\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni-bearing metal surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Conditions overlap rxn_010 and rxn_011. Ammonia activity and surface properties determine competition between ketone reduction and reductive amination.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the pyruvate ketone produces lactate without incorporating nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly observed lactate alongside alanine, including substantial lactate production with meteorite material. This branch prevents the graph from implying that all reduced pyruvate becomes alanine. Its relative flux is kinetic and cannot be inferred from reaction connectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing mixed-valence iron hydroxide reduction to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe(II)-bearing iron hydroxide redox reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm experimental benchmark\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Competition with rxn_008. Lower pH and strongly ferrous compositions can favor lactate; proton equivalents are supplied by the aqueous buffer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-derived reducing equivalents reduce the keto group directly rather than a nitrogen-containing intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. (2019) observed pronounced lactate formation at lower pH and with ferrous-rich materials, including conditions that produced no alanine. This experimental result argues against treating arbitrary Fe(II) minerals as interchangeable alanine catalysts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing FeS-mediated pyruvate reduction to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh reactive FeS as a consumable electron donor\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm benchmark\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"A competing branch within the FeS/pyruvate reaction system. Its importance depends on ammonia activity, pH, surface state and mineral aging.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct two-electron reduction of the pyruvate carbonyl yields lactate instead of amino-acid formation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied research identifies lactate as the recurring competing sink in mineral reductive amination, including the Fe-mineral systems discussed by Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003). An FeS-specific quantitative rate or product ratio is not asserted because it is not supplied. This branch represents the chemically supported competition rather than an additional productive pathway.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional L-favoring partial CPL photolysis with a speculative decarboxylation subchannel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 300-320 K proposed dry-film analogue\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Ambient pressure is a proposed terrestrial analogue, not a validated experimental equivalence\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Partial irradiation of a thin alanine film with circularly polarized UV of a helicity and wavelength that preferentially destroys D-alanine. Both enantiomers are also listed as outputs because some survive. Natural polarized flux, far-UV transmission and a persistent L-favoring sign are unestablished.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Different photodestruction rates can enrich the surviving mixture in one enantiomer; there is no D-to-L conversion. Ethylamine plus CO2 represents an explicitly speculative, atom-balanced photodecarboxylation subchannel, not a verified complete CPL photoproduct inventory. Relative survival and enantiomeric excess cannot be encoded by stoichiometric edges alone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Meinert et al. (2014) support wavelength- and helicity-dependent asymmetric photolysis of alanine, while Modica et al. (2014) illustrate small irradiation-induced excesses in ice-analogue chemistry. These studies do not establish reliable terrestrial L selection or enantiopure alanine. The particular ethylamine/CO2 product assignment is not established by the supplied research and is a speculative mechanistic surrogate. This module is therefore a candidate modest-enrichment process, not a solved final purification step.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-fed Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Methane/ammonia/water discharge supplies cyanide and acetaldehyde; imine formation, nonselective cyanide addition and nitrile hydrolysis yield L- and D-alanine. The appended CPL module is conditional and does not establish enantiopurity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate followed by cyanide/ammonia chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate enters the CO2-dependent alpha-ketoacid amination sequence. Cyanide can be supplied by rxn_001. Environmental transfer, concentration and hydrolytic completion are unresolved; the product before conditional CPL processing is racemic.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with fresh FeS reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Mineral CO2 reduction is followed by cooling and FeS-mediated amination in ammonia-rich water. Mineral consumption and the micromolar-to-millimolar concentration gap prevent this from being a demonstrated integrated process. Conditional CPL processing follows racemic synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with mixed-valence iron hydroxides\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate is aminated at an alkaline mixed-valence iron interface. Appropriate Fe(II):Fe(III), ammonia availability and competition with lactate are essential. An anoxic origin for the active material is required. CPL enrichment remains conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with electrochemically activated FeS\",\n      \"description\": \"Pre-reduced FeS provides a different experimentally tested electron reservoir for pyruvate amination. Establishing the required preactivation potential and concentrated substrates naturally is unresolved. The amination itself is nonenantioselective.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with Ni0/H2 catalysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate undergoes nickel-catalyzed reductive amination under H2. This route has lower-ammonia experimental support than most Fe-mineral variants, but substrate concentration, native-metal activity and pressure remain conditional. CPL does not provide established homochirality.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy/H2\",\n      \"description\": \"Delivered Fe-Ni metal replaces engineered supported nickel as the tested amination surface. This is a distinct catalyst-source pathway, not an independent reaction mechanism. Lactate is a major competing sink, and the synthesized alanine is racemic before conditional CPL treatment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate through oxime formation and Fe0 reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied hydroxylamine traps pyruvate as an oxime, which is reduced by consumed iron metal. Hydroxylamine availability is an explicit upstream gap. The route produces both alanine enantiomers and shares the unresolved enrichment problem.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde-ammonia-thiol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A supplied triose reacts slowly with ammonia in the presence of the experimentally tested organic thiol. The net alanine endpoint is demonstrated at low yield, while detailed transient chemistry and thiol provenance remain unresolved. CPL processing is only a conditional extension.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-ammonia-thiol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied C1 and C2 aldehydes produce alanine in the tested thiol-containing mixed-substrate system. This experimentally distinct input route does not assume an isolated glyceraldehyde intermediate. The observed synthesis is low-yield and nonenantioselective; the appended CPL module remains conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_019\",\n    \"mol_022\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten condition-specific pathways to alanine, explicitly distinguishing L-alanine from its D coproduct. These include Strecker chemistry, CO2-dependent cyanide chemistry of pyruvate, five mineral/metal-dependent alternatives including oxime reduction, and two thiol-dependent sugar routes. Several pathways are experimentally distinct catalyst variants, not independent reaction mechanisms. Atmospheric discharge supplies Strecker precursors; mineral-mediated CO2 reduction supplies a conditional, dilute pyruvate source. Glyceraldehyde, formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and hydroxylamine are explicitly external feedstocks where their production is not established here. All synthesis pathways produce racemic alanine. A separate, explicitly conditional circularly polarized light module represents possible modest enrichment, not demonstrated production of enantiopure L-alanine. Its photoproduct representation is marked speculative. The network is complete as a dependency graph, not as a demonstrated integrated geochemical process: concentration, mineral regeneration and terrestrial chiral selection remain gaps. Neutral formulas represent pH-dependent aqueous species; null environment_formed denotes external supply or formation in multiple environments. Mineral electron reservoirs are identified in agents, but their unresolved spent phases are not represented. Roles labeled intermediate also include side products because the schema has no byproduct role.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H5N", "iupac_name": "ethanimine", "common_name": "acetaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "(S)-alpha-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "(R)-alpha-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid; predominantly pyruvate under alkaline conditions", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid oxime; E/Z configuration unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "glyceraldehyde; feedstock stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "lactic acid; lactate side-product pool, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "native iron; Fe0", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "Fe^2+", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton; formal aqueous proton equivalent", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C2H7N", "iupac_name": "ethanamine", "common_name": "ethylamine; candidate alanine photodecarboxylation product", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H7N/c1-2-3/h2-3H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Discharge production and aqueous capture of Strecker precursors", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K bulk gas-water apparatus; the discharge is nonthermal", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Electrical discharge through an anoxic, locally reducing gas mixture with water circulation and aqueous trapping. H2 may also be present initially. The node summarizes selected products of a complex discharge mixture."}, "mechanism": "Energetic gas-phase fragmentation and recombination generate cyanide and carbonyl compounds, followed by dissolution in water. H2 is included as a possible net dehydrogenation product; this is not an elementary reaction.", "reasoning": "Miller (1953) and Johnson et al. (2008) support discharge-driven amino-acid precursor production and subsequent Strecker chemistry. The route requires a sufficiently reducing local atmosphere and does not establish a globally methane-rich early atmosphere. Product ratios and precursor concentrations cannot be inferred from this coarse-grained node."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Reversible acetaldehyde-ammonia condensation", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Moderately alkaline, ammonia-containing water; approximately pH 8-10 is a proposed environmental window rather than a claimed optimum. The imine is transient and need not accumulate."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, followed by reversible carbinolamine dehydration to ethanimine.", "reasoning": "This is established Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), consistent with Miller's interpretation of discharge-derived alanine (Miller, 1953). Water disfavors imine accumulation, but cyanide trapping can pull the coupled equilibrium forward. Low ammonia activity instead increases competing cyanohydrin chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Nonenantioselective cyanide capture of ethanimine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Aqueous HCN/CN- and NH3/NH4+ equilibria; no chiral catalyst or chiral irradiation is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the planar imine or iminium carbon from either face, generating equal amounts of the two aminonitrile enantiomers under achiral conditions.", "reasoning": "The aminonitrile pathway is supported by classical Strecker synthesis and its prebiotic applications (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1953). The separate stereoisomer nodes prevent racemic synthesis from being misrepresented as selective L-alanine production."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic aqueous fluid providing proton catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K proposed warm hydrolysis stage", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Aqueous acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "A separate acidic stage, approximately pH 1-3, is proposed after aminonitrile formation. Residence time and natural conversion at these conditions are uncertain. Neutral formulas represent the corresponding protonated species in acidic solution."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydration proceeds through an amide and then carboxylic-acid formation, releasing ammonia/ammonium. In the absence of racemization, each aminonitrile retains its alpha-carbon configuration.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis completes established Strecker alanine synthesis (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1953). Laboratory hydrolysis or analytical workup must not be equated with spontaneous quantitative conversion in a natural pool. Joining alkaline precursor formation to sufficiently effective hydrolysis is an environmental bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Mineral-mediated CO2 reduction and C-C assembly to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Awaruite, Ni3Fe, as a candidate Fe-Ni catalytic mineral"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 373 K", "pressure": "Pressurized H2/CO2 aqueous system; exact experimental pressure is not specified in the supplied report", "catalyst": "Awaruite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline, anoxic water-rock reaction. This node represents a multistep product network, not concerted reduction of three CO2 molecules. Cooling is required before the lower-temperature amination modules."}, "mechanism": "Mineral-assisted hydrogen activation, carbon-oxide reduction and surface C-C coupling produce a small pyruvate fraction within a broader mixture of reduced carbon compounds.", "reasoning": "Preiner et al. (2020) reported pyruvate up to approximately 10 micromolar in an experimental Fe/Ni-mineral CO2-reduction study. That maximum is not assigned here as a guaranteed awaruite-specific yield. The mechanism and concentration bridge to millimolar amination experiments remain unresolved. No pore concentration or thermophoretic enrichment is assumed to have been demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of pyruvate to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K candidate surface window; stage-specific conditions require verification", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm with dissolved inorganic carbon maintained", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cyanide, ammonia/ammonium and CO2/bicarbonate must coexist. This is a coarse-grained reaction sequence including hydrolysis, not an assertion that every intermediate hydrolyzes rapidly at room temperature."}, "mechanism": "CO2 enables the cyanide/ammonia reaction of the alpha-ketoacid through the hydantoin/N-carbamoyl-type chemistry described in the supplied research summary. Ring processing, hydrolysis and carbon loss are collapsed into the endpoint reaction. NH3 and CO2 occur on both sides to represent exchange and regeneration within the net sequence; no atom mapping is asserted.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support the alpha-ketoacid-to-alpha-amino-acid endpoint and the essential role of CO2. The supplied summary does not establish enough intermediate-specific detail to assign a unique hydantoin structure or an exact microscopic sequence here. This route is not modeled as simple cyanide reduction of pyruvate. Cyanide supply, pyruvate supply and hydrolytic processing remain separate constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Fresh FeS-mediated reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated FeS: consumable mineral reductant and reactive surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm laboratory benchmark; natural interfacial pressure is not established", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 9-10, substantial ammonia excess and fresh reactive FeS. Related Fe-based experiments use roughly 0.375-1.5 M ammonia. Protons denote buffered proton equivalents, not an acidic bulk solution."}, "mechanism": "Carbonyl-ammonia condensation followed by reduction gives alanine. The organic transformation requires two reducing equivalents per alanine; FeS supplies a finite redox inventory and its spent solid composition is not uniquely assigned.", "reasoning": "Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) demonstrated reductive amination using fresh iron minerals. The approximately 50% yield cited in the supplied report comes from a secondary summary and is not treated as a verified alanine-specific value. FeS is not assumed to be a indefinitely regenerating catalyst. High ammonia demand and the dilute upstream pyruvate source limit integration."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Mixed-valence iron hydroxide-mediated reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide material with approximately 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm experimental benchmark", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 10, excess ammonia and incubation up to approximately 72 h. The mixed-valence solid is a finite reactive redox reservoir. Its natural preparation must be anoxic; molecular oxygen is not supplied."}, "mechanism": "The mixed-valence surface promotes pyruvate-ammonia conversion and electron transfer. An imine-like intermediate is plausible, but the detailed mineral-mediated mechanism is unresolved.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. (2019) reported the strongest alanine outcome near pH 10 and a 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III) ratio, with approximately 70% alanine and 30% lactate among detected dissolved organics. Pure ferrous and pure ferric end members did not reproduce this result. An anoxic source of the appropriate mixed-valence material is required and is not established by this node."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Reductive amination by electrochemically pre-reduced FeS", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Electrochemically pre-reduced FeS containing reactive Fe0 domains; finite reducing reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm experimental benchmark", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported amination conditions include pH 9.6, 0.5 M ammonia, approximately 5 mM pyruvate and 48 h. Mineral preparation involved approximately -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days; this energy-intensive preparation is not hidden as spontaneous catalysis."}, "mechanism": "Reduced iron domains deliver electrons to the carbonyl-ammonia reaction system, producing racemic alanine and water.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) observed nearly complete pyruvate conversion under the reported laboratory conditions. Establishing and maintaining an equivalent natural reducing potential is an open geochemical requirement. The millimolar pyruvate and concentrated ammonia benchmark is not directly supplied by rxn_005."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Ni0/H2-catalyzed reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Accessible Ni0 surface; supported nickel is the laboratory benchmark"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 295 K", "pressure": "Approximately 5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni0", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-8 and 24 h. Screening used approximately 30 mM pyruvate. Lower-ammonia experiments included approximately 6 mM NH3. Natural native-nickel surfaces need not reproduce the activity of engineered supported nickel."}, "mechanism": "Nickel activates H2 and catalyzes hydrogenation of pyruvate-derived imine species. H2, rather than unaccounted mineral oxidation, supplies the net reducing equivalents.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated alanine formation under these conditions and useful conversion at lower ammonia concentrations than most Fe-mineral experiments. Their low-ammonia nickel yield was reported relative to ammonia, not pyruvate. Native Ni availability, passivation, H2 pressure and pyruvate concentration remain limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy/H2-mediated reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy; Aletai meteorite material is the experimental analogue"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 295 K", "pressure": "Approximately 5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Reactive Fe-Ni alloy surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral to mildly alkaline water containing pyruvate and ammonia. The reported meteorite material contained approximately 18 mol% Ni. Metal delivery is distinguished from delivery of preformed alanine."}, "mechanism": "Accessible nickel-bearing alloy sites activate hydrogen and mediate the same net imine hydrogenation as the supported-Ni route, with different surface composition and competing reactions.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate with Aletai meteorite material. This is a directly tested, geochemically distinct catalyst-source alternative, not a new fundamental reaction mechanism. Weathering and passivation of naturally delivered alloy may substantially reduce activity."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Condensation of pyruvate with hydroxylamine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K candidate mild aqueous window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Hydroxylamine-containing pyruvate solution. The stated environmental window is proposed, not an exact reconstruction of the published amino-acid experiment."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine adds to the ketone and loses water to form a pyruvate oxime. E/Z oxime configurations are not resolved in this coarse-grained node.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support hydroxylamine/Fe0 conversion of ketoacids to amino acids through oxime chemistry. Hydroxylamine is explicitly an external starting material here because a sufficient prebiotic source is not established by the supplied evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Stoichiometric Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_020", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_021", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Native Fe0, explicitly represented as a consumed reactant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K candidate mild aqueous window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous contact with reactive iron metal. Buffering supplies proton equivalents. Surface passivation and competing corrosion can reduce productive electron transfer."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the C=N bond and cleavage of the N-O bond convert the oxime into alanine. The formal transformation requires four electrons per oxime; oxidation of Fe0 to Fe2+ provides the corresponding reducing equivalents.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) demonstrated amino-acid production after adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 to their ketoacid chemistry. Unlike two-electron imine reduction, oxime-to-amine conversion requires a four-electron budget. No chiral control is established, and natural hydroxylamine supply remains the principal route-specific gap."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Thiol-enhanced alanine formation from glyceraldehyde and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally tested organic thiol promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Near ambient; approximately 300 K surface analogue", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "3-Mercaptopropanoic acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Long-lived anoxic glyceraldehyde/ammonium solution; reaction times of approximately 60 days. The organic thiol is an externally supplied promoter whose prebiotic production is not included."}, "mechanism": "A proposed carbonyl-rearrangement pathway involving methylglyoxal-type, hemithioacetal and imine chemistry couples internal oxidation and reduction to amino-acid formation. Unverified individual transient structures are not promoted to molecule nodes.", "reasoning": "Weber (1985) observed approximately 0.6% alanine after 60 days with thiol