anism": "Direct two-electron reduction of the pyruvate carbonyl yields lactate instead of amino-acid formation.", "reasoning": "The supplied research identifies lactate as the recurring competing sink in mineral reductive amination, including the Fe-mineral systems discussed by Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003). An FeS-specific quantitative rate or product ratio is not asserted because it is not supplied. This branch represents the chemically supported competition rather than an additional productive pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Conditional L-favoring partial CPL photolysis with a speculative decarboxylation subchannel", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_023", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 300-320 K proposed dry-film analogue", "pressure": "Ambient pressure is a proposed terrestrial analogue, not a validated experimental equivalence", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Partial irradiation of a thin alanine film with circularly polarized UV of a helicity and wavelength that preferentially destroys D-alanine. Both enantiomers are also listed as outputs because some survive. Natural polarized flux, far-UV transmission and a persistent L-favoring sign are unestablished."}, "mechanism": "Different photodestruction rates can enrich the surviving mixture in one enantiomer; there is no D-to-L conversion. Ethylamine plus CO2 represents an explicitly speculative, atom-balanced photodecarboxylation subchannel, not a verified complete CPL photoproduct inventory. Relative survival and enantiomeric excess cannot be encoded by stoichiometric edges alone.", "reasoning": "Meinert et al. (2014) support wavelength- and helicity-dependent asymmetric photolysis of alanine, while Modica et al. (2014) illustrate small irradiation-induced excesses in ice-analogue chemistry. These studies do not establish reliable terrestrial L selection or enantiopure alanine. The particular ethylamine/CO2 product assignment is not established by the supplied research and is a speculative mechanistic surrogate. This module is therefore a candidate modest-enrichment process, not a solved final purification step."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Discharge-fed Strecker pathway", "description": "Methane/ammonia/water discharge supplies cyanide and acetaldehyde; imine formation, nonselective cyanide addition and nitrile hydrolysis yield L- and D-alanine. The appended CPL module is conditional and does not establish enantiopurity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate followed by cyanide/ammonia chemistry", "description": "Hydrothermal pyruvate enters the CO2-dependent alpha-ketoacid amination sequence. Cyanide can be supplied by rxn_001. Environmental transfer, concentration and hydrolytic completion are unresolved; the product before conditional CPL processing is racemic.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate with fresh FeS reductive amination", "description": "Mineral CO2 reduction is followed by cooling and FeS-mediated amination in ammonia-rich water. Mineral consumption and the micromolar-to-millimolar concentration gap prevent this from being a demonstrated integrated process. Conditional CPL processing follows racemic synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate with mixed-valence iron hydroxides", "description": "Pyruvate is aminated at an alkaline mixed-valence iron interface. Appropriate Fe(II):Fe(III), ammonia availability and competition with lactate are essential. An anoxic origin for the active material is r