olysis conditions are not specified quantitatively. Approval concerns production of L-alanine within a racemate, not demonstrated enrichment. The balanced rxn_019 neither establishes differential D destruction nor validates its proposed ethylamine photoproduct.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify discharge gas composition, energy input, precursor capture efficiency, and aqueous concentrations; treat rxn_001 as a net inventory rather than a fixed experimental product ratio.\",\n        \"Provide pH, temperature, and residence time for aminonitrile hydrolysis, including compatibility with the preceding aqueous chemistry.\",\n        \"Keep CPL processing optional. Model L- and D-alanine photodestruction separately using wavelength- and helicity-dependent rates, and report both surviving alanine and enantiomeric excess.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.74,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.72,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.53,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.79,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.7,\n      \"feedback\": \"The supplied research supports both dilute mineral-mediated pyruvate production and CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of ketoacids to amino acids: Preiner et al. (2020) and Pulletikurti et al. (2022), respectively. Accepting rxn_006 as balanceable does not establish its mechanism or actual net NH3/CO2 fluxes. The main network-level weakness is connecting micromolar hydrothermal pyruvate to an appropriately concentrated surface reaction mixture without demonstrated recovery, transport, or concentration. The route produces racemic alanine; conditional CPL processing does not establish selective L production.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Assign rxn_005 a specific experimentally tested mineral, temperature, gas pressures, pH, residence time, and measured pyruvate yield.\",\n        \"For rxn_006, publish explicit stoichiometric coefficients and annotate the literature-supported hydantoin/N-carbamoyl chemistry, including cyanide-derived carbon and nitrogen fates.\",\n        \"Quantify pyruvate recovery, cooling, transport, and any concentration step before contact with cyanide, ammonia, and CO2; do not extrapolate concentrated amination yields directly to micromolar feed.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.36,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.46,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.5,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.4,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.43,\n      \"feedback\": \"Fresh FeS-mediated reductive amination has experimental support from Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), but the submitted rxn_007 violates conservation. Naming FeS as an agent does not supply its reducing equivalents in the reaction equation. The competing lactate branch rxn_018 is also UNBALANCED. Both omit electron-donor oxidation and its products. Thus this pathway cannot be accepted as chemically closed despite its plausible underlying transformation. The literature additionally requires controlled mineral freshness, alkaline conditions, and substantial ammonia concentrations.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Repair rxn_007 using the reduction half-reaction pyruvic acid + NH3 + 2 H+ + 2 e- -> alanine + H2O, doubled for one L and one D product. Couple it to an explicitly balanced oxidation of the actual Fe/S reductant.\",\n        \"Repair rxn_018 using pyruvic acid + 2 H+ + 2 e- -> lactic acid, again coupled to the identified mineral oxidation products. Electrons alone are not a complete network-level source.\",\n        \"Specify FeS preparation and electron capacity, pH near 9-10, experimentally relevant temperature and ammonia activity, and a finite reductant budget or justified resupply mechanism.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.36,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.46,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.5,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.4,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.43,\n      \"feedback\": \"Barge et al. (2019, DOI: 10.1073/pnas.1812098116) support mixed-valence iron hydroxide-mediated alanine formation and competing lactate production. Nevertheless, rxn_008 and rxn_017 are conservation violations in this network: the mineral electron reservoir and its oxidized endpoint are omitted from the stoichiometry. The experimental activity depends strongly on Fe(II):Fe(III), pH, and temperature; a generic mixed-valence mineral label is insufficient to establish those conditions. The reported high alanine fraction also should not be interpreted automatically as isolated yield from all input carbon.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add the actual mineral reactant and spent-phase compositions to rxn_008 and rxn_017. Each alanine or lactate formation requires two reducing equivalents; proton coefficients must also be corrected.\",\n        \"Use explicitly coupled half-reactions: pyruvic acid + NH3 + 2 H+ + 2 e- -> alanine + H2O, and pyruvic acid + 2 H+ + 2 e- -> lactic acid.\",\n        \"Specify mineral Fe(II):Fe(III), ammonia concentration, and reaction conditions. Benchmark against the approximately 1:1 mineral ratio, pH 10, and 70 \u00b0C experiment, while preserving the reported yield basis and lactate competition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.34,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.43,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.42,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.38,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.39,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.aav7848) provide strong experimental support for amination using electrochemically pre-reduced FeS containing partially generated Fe0. However, rxn_009 is UNBALANCED: it contains proton equivalents but no represented electron donor or oxidation products. Electrochemical pretreatment is a preparation requirement and finite source of stored reducing power, not evidence of an indefinitely regenerative catalyst. Neither that preparation nor its geochemical implementation is sufficiently specified here.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Repair rxn_009 by representing the material's actual oxidizable components and products. If Fe0 is the relevant donor, include its conversion to the appropriate iron endpoint rather than leaving it solely in an agent description.\",\n        \"Account for two electrons per alanine and couple the balanced organic reduction half-reaction to mineral oxidation or a fully specified electrochemical circuit.\",\n        \"Document the pretreatment potential and duration, including the experimental approximately -0.7 V versus SHE 