al_feasibility\": 0.75,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.75,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.56,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.72,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Fe-Ni alloy/H2 reaction is balanceable and supported by Kaur et al. (2024), including experiments using the Aletai meteorite. This is a legitimate experimentally distinct catalyst implementation, not an additional independent mechanism. Its applicability depends on alloy composition, exposed reactive surface, corrosion history, and H2 availability. Substantial lactate production in the cited experiment limits alanine selectivity. As elsewhere, a dilute pyruvate source and conditional CPL module do not establish an integrated enriched-L production process.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Define alloy composition, surface preparation or weathering state, loading, and a physically justified supply of accessible metal.\",\n        \"Specify H2 pressure, ammonia concentration, pH, and temperature rather than assuming the broad environment label reproduces the experiment.\",\n        \"Represent alloy-specific lactate competition quantitatively and preserve the distinction between the reported approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate outcomes.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.8,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.76,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.56,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.82,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"Oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction is chemically coherent and supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Unlike the defective mineral-amination equations, rxn_0