ot a high-flux route or selective L synthesis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Identify the thiol used and its concentration; distinguish the experimentally used 3-mercaptopropionic acid from untested generic thiol substitutions.\",\n        \"Specify pH, temperature, glyceraldehyde and ammonium concentrations, and residence time, and quantify competing lactate and condensation products.\",\n        \"Annotate the proposed dehydration, hemithioacetal, and imine sequence as a mechanistic interpretation rather than treating rxn_014 as an elementary event.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.76,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.75,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.58,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.72,\n      \"feedback\": \"Weber (1998) supports thiol-dependent alanine formation from formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, and ammonia. The net equation passes the supplied balance test; no additional external reductant should be demanded solely because this is a sugar-derived route. Nevertheless, the demonstrated approximately 1.8% yield on a formaldehyde basis required prolonged incubation and a particular thiol-containing mixture. The supplied network does not establish those operating conditions or catalyst availability. It produces L-alanine as a component of racemic alanine, while rxn_019 remains conditional.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the thiol identity and loading, reagent ratios, and conditions corresponding to the approximately pH 5.2, 40 \u00b0C, 35-day experiment.\",\n        \"Preserve the formaldehyde-based yield denominator and account for competing sugar reactions and product losses rather than implying stoichiometric alanine conversion.\",\n        \"Provide reaction-level literature support and a realistic incubation and retention scenario; independently test any proposed CPL enrichment and photoproduct assignment.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"NOT APPROVED at the network threshold of 0.9. The listed 23 molecule nodes and 19 reaction nodes form a connected graph with consistent identifiers, and several routes genuinely produce L-alanine within a racemic mixture. However, graph connectivity is not chemical closure. The deterministic check identifies five mandatory conservation failures: rxn_007, rxn_008, rxn_009, rxn_017, and rxn_018. These require explicit reducing equivalents, corrected proton stoichiometry, and the corresponding electron-donor oxidation products; agent annotations and acknowledged unresolved spent phases do not repair them. The other 14 reactions are accepted as balanced or balanceable, including rxn_006 and rxn_019, without treating balanceability as proof of mechanism or selectivity. Criterion assessments are network validity 0.70, chemical feasibility 0.63, environmental consistency 0.54, pathway coherence 0.68, target achievement 0.83, and literature alignment 0.78. Experimental support is substantial, and the submission appropriately acknowledges racemic production, dilute pyruvate, external feedstocks, catalyst variants, and integration gaps. Nevertheless, numerical reaction conditions, physical transfer justifications, agent availability, and reaction-level citations are largely absent from the supplied network. Literature conditions cannot simply be presumed implemented. CPL-induced small excesses are supported by the cited Meinert and Modica studies, but this does not establish a terrestrial L-favoring irradiation source, differential photolysis rates, or ethyla