mers in the Murchison meteorite (https://www.nature.com/articles/28306)\n  45. The science from asteroid sample return missions (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.22182)\n  46. EXTRATERRESTRIAL AMINO ACIDS IDENTIFIED IN AN AGGREGATE SAMPLE RETURNED (https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2024/pdf/1640.pdf)\n  47. Enantiomeric excess of amino acids in interstellar ice analogues-asymmetric photolysis of precursors by circularly polarized UV light (https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/517/4/6112/6754748)\n  48. Photonenergy\u2010Controlled Symmetry Breaking with Circularly Polarized Light - Meinert - 2014 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.201307855)\n  49. ENANTIOMERIC EXCESSES INDUCED IN AMINO ACIDS BY ULTRAVIOLET CIRCULARLY POLARIZED LIGHT IRRADIATION OF EXTRATERRESTRIAL ICE ANALOGS: A POSSIBLE SOURCE OF ASYMMETRY FOR PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/788/1/79)\n  50. Anisotropy-Guided Enantiomeric Enhancement in AlanineUsing Far-UV Circularly Polarized Light (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-015-9413-x)\n  51. Homochirality: a prerequisite or consequence of life? (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.12850)\n  52. NASA study reproduces origins of life on ocean floor (https://phys.org/news/2019-02-nasa-life-ocean-floor.html)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.69\n- Feedback: NOT APPROVED at the network threshold of 0.9. The listed 23 molecule nodes and 19 reaction nodes form a connected graph with consistent identifiers, and several routes genuinely produce L-alanine within a racemic mixture. However, graph connectivity is not chemical closure. The deterministic check identifies five mandatory conservation failures: rxn_007, rxn_008, rxn_009, rxn_017, and rxn_018. These require explicit reducing equivalents, corrected proton stoichiometry, and the corresponding electron-donor oxidation products; agent annotations and acknowledged unresolved spent phases do not repair them. The other 14 reactions are accepted as balanced or balanceable, including rxn_006 and rxn_019, without treating balanceability as proof of mechanism or selectivity. Criterion assessments are network validity 0.70, chemical feasibility 0.63, environmental consistency 0.54, pathway coherence 0.68, target achievement 0.83, and literature alignment 0.78. Experimental support is substantial, and the submission appropriately acknowledges racemic production, dilute pyruvate, external feedstocks, catalyst variants, and integration gaps. Nevertheless, numerical reaction conditions, physical transfer justifications, agent availability, and reaction-level citations are largely absent from the supplied network. Literature conditions cannot simply be presumed implemented. CPL-induced small excesses are supported by the cited Meinert and Modica studies, but this does not establish a terrestrial L-favoring irradiation source, differential photolysis rates, or ethylamine as the relevant product. Enantiopure L-alanine is not demonstrated, although purity was not imposed as a prerequisite for crediting actual L-alanine formation. Pathway membership was inferred from route names and step counts; rxn_019 was treated as optional post-processing, and rxn_016-rxn_018 were scored as side branches where applicable. P1's pathway approval does not imply network approval. Neither the number of catalyst variants, shared molecules, convergence points, nor the number or ordering of environments affected the assessment.\n- Issues: ['The supplied research supports both dilute mineral-mediated pyruvate production and CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of ketoacids to amino acids: Preiner et al. (2020) and Pulletikurti et al. (2022), respectively. Accepting rxn_006 as balanceable does not establish its mechanism or actual net NH3/CO2 fluxes. The main network-level weakness is connecting micromolar hydrothermal pyruvate to an appropriately concentrated surface reaction mixture without demonstrated recovery, transport, or concentration. The route produces racemic alanine; conditional CPL processing does not establish selective L production.', 'Fresh FeS-mediated reductive amination has experimental support from Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), but the submitted rxn_007 violates conservation. Naming FeS as an agent does not supply its reducing equivalents in the reaction equation. The competing lactate branch rxn_018 is also UNBALANCED. Both omit electron-donor oxidation and its products. Thus this pathway cannot be accepted as chemically closed despite its plausible underlying transformation. The literature additionally requires controlled mineral freshness, alkaline conditions, and substantial ammonia concentrations.', 'Barge et al. (2019, DOI: 10.1073/pnas.1812098116) support mixed-valence iron hydroxide-mediated alanine formation and competing lactate production. Nevertheless, rxn_008 and rxn_017 are conservation violations in this network: the mineral electron reservoir and its oxidized endpoint are omitted from the stoichiometry. The experimental activity depends strongly on Fe(II):Fe(III), pH, and temperature; a generic mixed-valence mineral label is insufficient to establish those conditions. The reported high alanine fraction also should not be interpreted automatically as isolated yield from all input carbon.', 'Kitadai et al. (2019, DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.aav7848) provide strong experimental support for amination using electrochemically pre-reduced FeS containing partially generated Fe0. However, rxn_009 is UNBALANCED: it contains proton equivalents but no represented electron donor or oxidation products. Electrochemical pretreatment is a preparation requirement and finite source of stored reducing power, not evidence of an indefinitely regenerative catalyst. Neither that preparation nor its geochemical implementation is sufficiently specified here.', 'The organic reduction and hydrogen source are correctly represented in rxn_010, and rxn_016 is a valid competing lactate sink. Kaur et al. (2024) experimentally support Ni/H2 amination under mild temperatures, including useful outcomes at lower ammonia concentrations than many iron-mineral systems. The principal gaps are upstream pyruvate productivity, catalyst identity and availability, maintenance of dissolved H2, and transfer into the cooler amination setting. The label Biochemical does not itself specify a physical environment. L-alanine is produced within the racemate without needing rxn_019.', 'The Fe-Ni alloy/H2 reaction is balanceable and supported by Kaur et al. (2024), including experiments using the Aletai meteorite. This is a legitimate experimentally distinct catalyst implementation, not an additional independent mechanism. Its applicability depends on alloy composition, exposed reactive surface, corrosion history, and H2 availability. Substantial lactate production in the cited experiment limits alanine selectivity. As elsewhere, a dilute pyruvate source and conditional CPL module do not establish an integrated enriched-L production process.', 'Oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction is chemically coherent and supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Unlike the defective mineral-amination equations, rxn_013 explicitly includes Fe0 consumption and Fe2+ production and passes the supplied balance check. Its stoichiometry corresponds to two Fe0 atoms per alanine when iron ends as Fe2+. Declaring hydroxylamine an external feedstock is legitimate; the remaining questions concern supplied concentration, compatibility, and persistence, not a requirement to synthesize every starting material internally. Pyruvate supply and environmental implementation remain conditional.', \"Weber's glyceraldehyde/ammonium experiments support this balanceable net conversion, including thiol enhancement and low alanine yields over long reaction times. The lumped transformation is not intrinsically invalid merely because its mechanistic intermediates are omitted. However, the network does not identify the thiol agent or specify its loading and environmental availability. External glyceraldehyde is an acceptable stated boundary condition. The approximately 0.6% alanine outcome after 60 days supports formation, not a high-flux route or selective L synthesis.\", 'Weber (1998) supports thiol-dependent alanine formation from formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, and ammonia. The net equation passes the supplied balance test; no additional external reductant should be demanded solely because this is a sugar-derived route. Nevertheless, the demonstrated approximately 1.8% yield on a formaldehyde basis required prolonged incubation and a particular thiol-containing mixture. The supplied network does not establish those operating conditions or catalyst availability. It produces L-alanine as a component of racemic alanine, while rxn_019 remains conditional.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct ten distinct reaction sequences that produce L-alanine as one component of racemic alanine. These include experimentally distinguishable catalyst implementations, not ten independent mechanisms. The network combines Strecker chemistry, five implementations of pyruvate amination, hydroxylamine chemistry, and two thiol-assisted sugar routes. Hydrothermal CO2 reduction supplies a modeled pyruvate source, but its reported micromolar productivity is not silently converted into the millimolar substrate concentrations used in downstream experiments. Cooling into adjacent anoxic porewaters is a conditional transport link; recovery, dilution and reaction rates at those low concentrations remain unresolved. Acetaldehyde, HCN, ammonia, sugar feedstocks, hydroxylamine, reactive minerals and organic thiols are explicit external boundary stocks. Every represented reduction includes its electron donor and an oxidation product. Where mineral oxidation products were not established experimentally, a specific, atom-balanced endpoint is identified as a conditional closure rather than a measured result. Neutral formulas are used for acid-base families; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Both alanine enantiomers are represented separately, and achiral synthesis nodes produce them equally. Enantioenrichment is a separate unresolved module: no reaction is invented that converts D-alanine into L-alanine or produces enantiopure material. CPL can create small excesses, but the supplied evidence does not establish a terrestrial L-favoring irradiation field or sufficiently characterized photoproducts for a closed enrichment reaction (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). Thus the target is reached molecularly, not as an isolated homochiral product. The environment_formed field records a representative formation setting, not an exclusive location. The intermediate role also covers terminal coproducts because the schema has no byproduct category.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\