\"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeHO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(III) oxide hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferric oxyhydroxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"zero-valent iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde, racemic feedstock\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-assisted hydrothermal CO2 reduction to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Awaruite, Ni3Fe, as an H2-activating mineral catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar total as a modeled hydrothermal boundary condition, not a reproduced experimental pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic alkaline rock-water pores, approximately pH 9-11, with sustained H2 supply and dissolved inorganic carbon. The node represents the pyruvate-producing channel, not the entire product distribution. Retain a source concentration ceiling of approximately 10 micromolar before transport losses; do not assume millimolar pyruvate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-mediated activation of H2 and CO2 supports reduction and carbon-carbon bond formation. The balanced overall pyruvate channel consumes reducing hydrogen and releases water; its elementary surface sequence is not established.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) demonstrated H2-dependent inorganic-carbon reduction on relevant minerals and reported pyruvate concentrations up to approximately 10 micromolar across the investigated systems. That upper envelope is not a guaranteed awaruite-specific yield under the modeled pressure. Dilute C3 production is a major network bottleneck. Subsequent chemistry requires cooling into nearby anoxic porewaters, with dilution and adsorption losses retained rather than replaced by an assumed concentration mechanism. Short residence at elevated temperature avoids importing black-smoker conditions into the amination steps (Miller and Bada, 1988).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sheltered aqueous pool; proposed pH 8-9 with ammonia in excess over acetaldehyde. Millimolar aldehyde and cyanide are explicit local feedstock assumptions, not predicted atmospheric concentrations. Limit volatilization and direct UV exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde and ammonia reversibly form an imine or iminium species; cyanide addition generates racemic 2-aminopropanenitrile. Water is released in the net condensation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acetaldehyde-based Strecker chemistry is experimentally established (Strecker, 1850), and Miller's discharge experiments demonstrate formation of alanine from simpler gases through compatible precursor chemistry (Miller, 1953). This node does not assume that a globally reducing atmosphere supplied the required local concentrations. The temperature and pH window is a proposed pool implementation rather than a reproduction of a cited recipe. Insufficient ammonia favors competing cyanohydrin chemistry rather than alanine formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminonitrile hydration to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium as a regenerated general-acid catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic hydrolysis stage, pH 1-3, in an anoxic chloride-rich geothermal pool. Requires transfer or acidification of the preceding alkaline mixture. Days-to-weeks residence is a scenario assumption; dilute-solution kinetics are not supplied by the research summary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proton-assisted water addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers, gives the corresponding amide without changing the carbon skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis through an amide is the established chemical completion of Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The specific mild-temperature terrestrial residence time remains an implementation gap; it is not inferred from the mere existence of alanine in discharge experiments (Miller, 1953). Acidic volcanogenic chloride waters provide an oxygen-independent source of acidity. No redox reagent is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Alan\u093f\u0928amide hydrolysis to both alanine enantiomers\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium as a regenerated general-acid catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue acidic aqueous hydrolysis at proposed pH 1-3 until the amide is consumed, then cool. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium at this pH; the graph uses neutral-form bookkeeping. L- and D-alanine are produced equally.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis replaces the terminal amide nitrogen with hydroxyl, releasing ammonia and retaining the aminonitrile-derived stereocenter.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the classical Strecker route (Strecker, 1850). Water and liberated ammonia are explicit, so hydrolysis is atom-balanced rather than represented as unexplained nitrile-to-acid conversion. The original achiral addition produces both stereoisomers; hydrolysis does not select L-alanine. The selected geothermal hydrolysis window is conditional, not a measured rate claim from Miller (1953).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS-mediated reductive amination with explicit pyrite-forming sulfur oxidation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh mackinawite as a reactive surface and consumed reagent\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; the cited aqueous benchmark does not establish this pressure dependence\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool anoxic vent-fed porewater, pH 9-10. Iron-route laboratory ammonia benchmarks are approximately 0.375-1.5 M; such concentrations are an unresolved local requirement. Supply at least one external sulfide equivalent per FeS equivalent converted to pyrite. The sulfide node represents the H2S/HS- acid-base pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An ammonia-derived imine is reduced while sulfide sulfur is oxidized to disulfide sulfur in pyrite. One FeS plus one external sulfide can supply the two-electron budget per alanine formed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) support reductive amination on freshly precipitated FeS. The specific quantitative pyrite endpoint used here is a conditional, speculative closure because the supplied research does not establish the complete spent-phase inventory. Unlike an agent-only FeS annotation, this node explicitly consumes sulfur reductant and produces FeS2. Its implementation requires verifying pyrite formation and excluding alternative sulfur products. The reported high-ammonia experiments cannot establish the yield from the micromolar pyruvate supplied by rxn_001.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide-oxyhydroxide assemblage\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; laboratory benchmark approximately ambient pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sediment porewater downstream of the hotter source; pH 10; initial Fe(II):Fe(III) approximately 1:1; 72-hour reference duration. A substantial ammonia reservoir is required; the supplied review places earlier iron systems in the approximately 0.1-1.5 M range. Initial ferric material is supplied without invoking molecular oxygen.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-associated pyruvate and ammonia undergo reductive amination. Two ferrous equivalents are oxidized per alanine. Fe(OH)2 and FeOOH provide explicit idealized reduced and oxidized solid inventories; pre-existing FeOOH is retained while additional FeOOH is formed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. (2019) found strong pH and mixed-valence dependence, with the highest reported alanine fraction at pH 10, 343 K and approximately equal Fe(II)/Fe(III). Their approximately 70% value describes detected liquid-phase organics, not automatically isolated yield from all starting carbon. The mechanism and final mineral assemblage were not established. Consequently, FeOOH is a specified, atom-balanced oxidation endpoint for this conditional model, not an experimentally identified unique spent phase. Pure ferrous hydroxide is not assumed to reproduce the mixed-valence result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Stored Fe0-driven amination on pre-reduced FeS particles\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS-supported metallic iron inventory supplied as pre-reduced particles\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; laboratory amination benchmark approximately ambient pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool anoxic mineral pore; pH 9.6; 0.5 M total ammonia; 48 hours. The cited experiment used 5 mM keto acid and FeS pretreated at -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days. This network instead declares the resulting Fe0 inventory an external, finite feedstock and does not claim to generate it electrochemically.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An imine-derived intermediate accepts two electrons from Fe0 oxidation to Fe(II). Ferrous hydroxide is the modeled alkaline corrosion product, with water explicitly supplying its oxygen and hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) demonstrated efficient amination using electrochemically pre-reduced FeS containing metallic iron. The reducing inventory is not a perpetually regenerating catalyst: Fe0 appears as an input and an Fe(II) product appears as an output. Formation of the starting pre-reduced particles under natural conditions remains unresolved and is not hidden inside the agents field. Fe(OH)2 is a conditional alkaline corrosion endpoint, not a claim that it was the only measured product. Near-complete laboratory conversion cannot be transferred to a micromolar hydrothermal feed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed H2 reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Metallic nickel on a silicate support\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 fugacity; total pore pressure may be higher\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0; laboratory benchmark Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool, sealed anoxic mineral pore; pH 7-8; 24-hour laboratory benchmark. Low-ammonia comparison uses approximately 6 mM total ammonia. Published screening used 30 mM pyruvate, whereas the internally supplied stream remains at most approximately 10 micromolar before dilution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel dissociates H2 and hydrogenates an ammonia-derived imine. H2 supplies the reducing equivalents, and water is the net oxygen-containing coproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) support Ni/H2 amination under mild conditions, including measurable conversion at approximately 6 mM ammonia. This relaxes, but does not eliminate, the nitrogen-concentration problem. Their low-ammonia percentage is referenced to ammonia and must not be compared directly with percentages referenced to pyruvate. Natural exposed Ni0, resistance to sulfide passivation, and maintained dissolved H2 are necessary boundary conditions. The laboratory supported catalyst is not presumed identical to an indigenous mineral surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-Ni meteoritic-alloy H2 reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed Fe-Ni alloy comparable to the tested Aletai meteorite, approximately 18 mol% Ni\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 fugacity; total pore pressure may be higher\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Reactive Fe-Ni alloy surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic H2-bearing porewater, pH 7-8, approximately 24-hour laboratory comparison. Alloy composition, available surface area and corrosion history must be characterized. An accessible meteoritic metal fragment is an external geological boundary stock, not newly synthesized here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alloy-associated nickel activates H2 for imine hydrogenation. H2, rather than unrecorded iron corrosion, supplies the reducing equivalents assigned to this reaction node.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) observed alanine formation using the Aletai meteorite and substantial accompanying lactate, reported as approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate in the cited experiment. This is a distinct experimentally tested catalyst implementation, not an independent amination mechanism. Sulfidation, hydroxide coatings and changing alloy exposure may reduce activity. The graph does not borrow additional electrons from alloy corrosion without representing an oxidation product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Condensation of pyruvate with hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K, proposed implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; no pressure-enhancement claim\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous compartment; proposed pH 5-7 for oxime formation. Hydroxylamine is an explicit external stock, initially at least comparable to available pyruvate. Its source, lifetime and compatibility with dissolved metals are unresolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine adds to the ketone carbonyl and eliminates water, forming pyruvate oxime with unspecified E/Z composition.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) demonstrated amino-acid formation following hydroxylamine and Fe0 addition to an iron-promoted network. Oxime formation provides the chemically coherent intermediate connecting pyruvate to that reduction. The stated pH and temperature window is a proposed staging choice, not a verified transcription of the primary recipe. Prebiotic hydroxylamine availability is the principal additional feedstock bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Four-electron Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Reactive zero-valent iron surface, consumed during reduction\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K, proposed implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; no pressure-enhancement claim\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic water; proposed pH 8-9 after oxime formation, allowing ferrous-hydroxide precipitation. Supply at least two reactive Fe0 equivalents per oxime reduced. Carbonate or sulfide capture of Fe(II) would require a different explicitly balanced endpoint.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the C=N functionality and cleavage of the N-O bond produce the amino group. Two Fe0 atoms oxidized to Fe(II) supply the four electrons required per alanine; water and Fe(OH)2 close the oxygen and hydrogen inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The hydroxylamine/Fe0 amino-acid route is supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Unlike two-electron imine hydrogenation, oxime-to-amine conversion requires four reducing equivalents; the represented iron inventory supplies them explicitly. The chosen Fe(OH)2 product is a conditional speciation model rather than a verified unique spent phase. No stereochemical selection follows from the achiral reduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-dependent cyanide-ammonia conversion of pyruvate through a Bucherer-Bergs-type network\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K, proposed mild aqueous implementation\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; cited mild aqueous chemistry does not establish this pressure dependence\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic carbonate-bearing porewater; proposed pH 8-9. Ammonia, free cyanide and dissolved inorganic carbon must coexist. Trial boundary stocks of 0.1 M total ammonia and 1-10 mM total cyanide are explicit assumptions, not a verified primary-paper recipe. Separate the fluid from strongly cyanide-binding mineral surfaces. Internally supplied pyruvate remains micromolar or lower.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped formation-and-hydrolysis node represents the reported CO2-dependent hydantoin/carbamoyl reaction network. NH3 and CO2 are listed on both sides because their participation and release cannot be represented correctly by treating them as absent reagents. The fully hydrolyzed neutral-form net is balanceable with cyanide consumption and net CO2 release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support conversion of alpha-ketoacids to corresponding amino acids using cyanide, ammonia and essential CO2. This node does not claim that an atom-balanced sum determines the elementary mechanism or isotopic provenance. Its complete-hydrolysis accounting allows ammonia released from nitrogen-containing intermediates and CO2 released from the combined carbon pools; exact experimental NH3/CO2 fluxes are not established by the supplied summary. Co-location of cyanide with dilute pyruvate, protection from metal complexation, and completion of hydrolysis remain integration gaps. This route is not treated as direct cyanide reduction of pyruvate without CO2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as an organic thiol catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300 K, near-ambient scenario\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, persistently wet sheltered pool; approximately 60 days. Explicit trial stocks: 10 mM glyceraldehyde, 0.1 M total ammonia and 10 mM thiol at pH 5.2. These concentrations and pH are scenario assumptions, not verified values from Weber's 1985 recipe. Shield the long incubation from direct UV.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped low-yield channel involves triose dehydration, thiol-associated carbonyl intermediates and amination with internal redox redistribution. The net conversion releases water and requires no additional external electron donor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1985) observed alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium, with thiol-enhanced formation of approximately 0.6% after 60 days versus approximately 0.1% without thiol. Those yields are evidence of formation, not predictions for the trial loadings specified here. The net equation is already atom- and redox-balanced; adding an arbitrary reductant would misrepresent the chemistry. Availability and persistence of 3-mercaptopropanoic acid and month-long substrate survival are unresolved prebiotic requirements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as an organic thiol catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-Mercaptopropanoic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sheltered geothermal pool, pH 5.2, approximately 35 days. Explicit trial stocks: 10 mM formaldehyde, 10 mM glycolaldehyde, 0.1 M total ammonia and 10 mM thiol. The temperature, pH and duration follow the supplied report; the concentrations are unvalidated trial assumptions because catalyst loading was not supplied.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbon-carbon bond formation accesses C3 carbonyl chemistry, followed by thiol-assisted rearrangement and amination. This lumped experimental channel has the same internally balanced overall oxidation-state redistribution as the triose route.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1998) reported approximately 1.8% alanine on a formaldehyde basis after 35 days at 313 K and pH 5.2, with little formation without thiol. This is a distinct experimentally demonstrated feedstock entry route, although downstream chemistry overlaps rxn_013. Exact catalyst loading must be checked before quantitative reproduction; the stated trial stocks are not assigned the published yield. Low selectivity, competing sugar chemistry, thiol availability and UV protection remain significant limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing H2 reduction of pyruvate to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Metallic nickel or reactive Fe-Ni alloy\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 fugacity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0 or Fe-Ni alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic porewater, pH 7-8, approximately 24-hour comparison. This channel competes directly with rxn_008 and rxn_009, particularly when imine formation is limited by ammonia availability.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"H2 activation on the metal is followed by ketone hydrogenation to the secondary alcohol. Hydrogen is the explicit electron donor; no oxygen-containing coproduct is needed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) measured competing lactate in Ni/H2 and meteoritic-alloy experiments. The reported product distributions demonstrate why alanine formation cannot be equated with complete pyruvate conversion. This atom-balanced sink uses H2 explicitly rather than treating the metal catalyst as an unspecified source of reducing power.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing lactate formation with oxidation of ferrous hydroxide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Mixed-valence iron hydroxide-oxyhydroxide assemblage\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; laboratory benchmark approximately ambient pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic porewater; pH 7 comparison; initial Fe(II):Fe(III) approximately 3:1; 72 hours. Ferric material is an initial mineral stock, not produced using molecular oxygen.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two ferrous equivalents reduce the pyruvate carbonyl to lactate and oxidize to ferric oxyhydroxide. Initial FeOOH is retained, and additional FeOOH records the consumed reducing inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. (2019) found no alanine under the cited pH 7, 75% Fe(II) condition and approximately 30-50% lactate after 72 hours. This provides a direct selectivity constraint on rxn_006. As in that node, the FeOOH endpoint is a conditional solid-speciation closure rather than an identified unique spent phase; nevertheless, both electron-donor oxidation and all atoms are explicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional pyrite-coupled lactate side channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh mackinawite, consumed together with external sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfidic porewater, proposed pH 8-10. Relevant when ammonia-derived imine formation is slow. Requires the same verified pyrite-forming redox inventory as rxn_005.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbonyl reduction competes with imine reduction while sulfide sulfur is oxidized into pyrite disulfide. FeS, external sulfide and FeS2 explicitly close the two-electron budget.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This catalyst-specific side channel is speculative: the supplied research establishes iron-mediated amination and widespread lactate competition, but not a quantitative pyrite-coupled lactate yield under these exact conditions (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Barge et al., 2019). It is included as a chemically plausible competing sink, not an experimentally calibrated reaction. Its sulfur endpoint must be tested together with rxn_005.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing Fe0-driven reduction of pyruvate to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Reactive zero-valent iron surface, consumed during reduction\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic mineral pore; proposed pH 8-9 for the represented Fe(OH)2 endpoint. Competes with amination where ammonia activity or imine coverage is insufficient. No numerical yield is assigned.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxidation of Fe0 to Fe(II) supplies the two electrons needed for ketone reduction. Water provides the hydrogen and the oxygen incorporated into the modeled ferrous-hydroxide corrosion product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) demonstrate the importance of metal identity and surface preparation for reductive selectivity. The exact rate and Fe(OH)2 endpoint of this standalone side channel are conditional, not quantitatively established by the supplied summary. It is explicitly balanced and does not use unrepresented proton or electron reservoirs.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia form a racemic aminonitrile, followed by staged hydrolysis. Produces L-alanine and an equal amount of D-alanine; hydrolysis conditions and precursor concentrations remain environmental constraints.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate with fresh FeS and sulfide\",\n      \"description\": \"Dilute mineral-derived pyruvate undergoes FeS-associated reductive amination. The represented sulfur-to-pyrite electron closure is conditional and must be verified; no high downstream yield is inferred from the dilute source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate with mixed-valence iron hydroxides\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate enters a cooler alkaline mineral pore containing both ferrous and ferric components. Ferrous oxidation supports racemic alanine formation, while a shift toward neutral pH favors the lactate side channel.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate with a finite pre-reduced FeS-associated Fe0 inventory\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied pre-reduced mineral particles provide explicitly consumed metallic iron. The route does not assume natural regeneration of the laboratory-prepared reducing inventory or transfer near-complete millimolar conversion to micromolar feed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate with Ni-catalyzed H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Cooling delivers pyruvate to an H2-bearing nickel interface. This implementation has experimental support at lower ammonia concentrations than many iron routes, but still requires accessible Ni0, sustained H2 and competition control.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate with meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy and H2\",\n      \"description\": \"A reactive Fe-Ni fragment substitutes for the supported nickel catalyst. This experimentally distinct implementation shares the H2 reductive-amination mechanism and can produce substantial lactate alongside both alanine enantiomers.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"An external hydroxylamine stock converts pyruvate to its oxime. Explicit four-electron reduction by metallic iron yields racemic alanine. Hydroxylamine availability and the required staging of aqueous conditions are additional bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-dependent cyanide-ammonia pathway from pyruvate\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate enters a separate carbonate-, cyanide- and ammonia-bearing reaction compartment. A lumped Bucherer-Bergs-type formation-and-hydrolysis node reaches racemic alanine; co-location, mineral compatibility and complete hydrolysis are not assumed demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde-thiol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied racemic glyceraldehyde and ammonium undergo a slow thiol-assisted net conversion. The reported low-yield route produces L-alanine within a racemate without cyanide or an added external reductant.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-thiol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied C1 and C2 aldehydes enter a demonstrated thiol-assisted C3 assembly and amination channel. It is distinct from direct glyceraldehyde feeding, although downstream chemistry overlaps that route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_023\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten distinct reaction sequences that produce L-alanine as one component of racemic alanine. These include experimentally distinguishable catalyst implementations, not ten independent mechanisms. The network combines Strecker chemistry, five implementations of pyruvate amination, hydroxylamine chemistry, and two thiol-assisted sugar routes. Hydrothermal CO2 reduction supplies a modeled pyruvate source, but its reported micromolar productivity is not silently converted into the millimolar substrate concentrations used in downstream experiments. Cooling into adjacent anoxic porewaters is a conditional transport link; recovery, dilution and reaction rates at those low concentrations remain unresolved. Acetaldehyde, HCN, ammonia, sugar feedstocks, hydroxylamine, reactive minerals and organic thiols are explicit external boundary stocks. Every represented reduction includes its electron donor and an oxidation product. Where mineral oxidation products were not established experimentally, a specific, atom-balanced endpoint is identified as a conditional closure rather than a measured result. Neutral formulas are used for acid-base families; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Both alanine enantiomers are represented separately, and achiral synthesis nodes produce them equally. Enantioenrichment is a separate unresolved module: no reaction is invented that converts D-alanine into L-alanine or produces enantiopure material. CPL can create small excesses, but the supplied evidence does not establish a terrestrial L-favoring irradiation field or sufficiently characterized photoproducts for a closed enrichment reaction (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). Thus the target is reached molecularly, not as an isolated homochiral product. The environment_formed field records a representative formation setting, not an exclusive location. The intermediate role also covers terminal coproducts because the schema has no byproduct category.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alpha-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanamide", "common_name": "alaninamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "FeS", "iupac_name": "iron(II) sulfide", "common_name": "mackinawite", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "FeS2", "iupac_name": "iron disulfide", "common_name": "pyrite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "FeH2O2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferrous hydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "ferric oxyhydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "zero-valent iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "glyceraldehyde, racemic feedstock", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Mineral-assisted hydrothermal CO2 reduction to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Awaruite, Ni3Fe, as an H2-activating mineral catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373 K", "pressure": "100 bar total as a modeled hydrothermal boundary condition, not a reproduced experimental pressure", "catalyst": "Awaruite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic alkaline rock-water pores, approximately pH 9-11, with sustained H2 supply and dissolved inorganic carbon. The node represents the pyruvate-producing channel, not the entire product distribution. Retain a source concentration ceiling of approximately 10 micromolar before transport losses; do not assume millimolar pyruvate."}, "mechanism": "Mineral-mediated activation of H2 and CO2 supports reduction and carbon-carbon bond formation. The balanced overall pyruvate channel consumes reducing hydrogen and releases water; its elementary surface sequence is not established.", "reasoning": "Preiner et al. (2020) demonstrated H2-dependent inorganic-carbon reduction on relevant minerals and reported pyruvate concentrations up to approximately 10 micromolar across the investigated systems. That upper envelope is not a guaranteed awaruite-specific yield under the modeled pressure. Dilute C3 production is a major network bottleneck. Subsequent chemistry requires cooling into nearby anoxic porewaters, with dilution and adsorption losses retained rather than replaced by an assumed concentration mechanism. Short residence at elevated temperature avoids importing black-smoker conditions into the amination steps (Miller and Bada, 1988)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sheltered aqueous pool; proposed pH 8-9 with ammonia in excess over acetaldehyde. Millimolar aldehyde and cyanide are explicit local feedstock assumptions, not predicted atmospheric concentrations. Limit volatilization and direct UV exposure."}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde and ammonia reversibly form an imine or iminium species; cyanide addition generates racemic 2-aminopropanenitrile. Water is released in the net condensation.", "reasoning": "Acetaldehyde-based Strecker chemistry is experimentally established (Strecker, 1850), and Miller's discharge experiments demonstrate formation of alanine from simpler gases through compatible precursor chemistry (Miller, 1953). This node does not assume that a globally reducing atmosphere supplied the required local concentrations. The temperature and pH window is a proposed pool implementation rather than a reproduction of a cited recipe. Insufficient ammonia favors competing cyanohydrin chemistry rather than alanine formation."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Aminonitrile hydration to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium as a regenerated general-acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed acidic hydrolysis stage, pH 1-3, in an anoxic chloride-rich geothermal pool. Requires transfer or acidification of the preceding alkaline mixture. Days-to-weeks residence is a scenario assumption; dilute-solution kinetics are not supplied by the research summary."}, "mechanism": "Proton-assisted water addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers, gives the corresponding amide without changing the carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydrolysis through an amide is the established chemical completion of Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The specific mild-temperature terrestrial residence time remains an implementation gap; it is not inferred from the mere existence of alanine in discharge experiments (Miller, 1953). Acidic volcanogenic chloride waters provide an oxygen-independent source of acidity. No redox reagent is required."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Alan\u093f\u0928amide hydrolysis to both alanine enantiomers", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium as a regenerated general-acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue acidic aqueous hydrolysis at proposed pH 1-3 until the amide is consumed, then cool. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium at this pH; the graph uses neutral-form bookkeeping. L- and D-alanine are produced equally."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis replaces the terminal amide nitrogen with hydroxyl, releasing ammonia and retaining the aminonitrile-derived stereocenter.", "reasoning": "This completes the classical Strecker route (Strecker, 1850). Water and liberated ammonia are explicit, so hydrolysis is atom-balanced rather than represented as unexplained nitrile-to-acid conversion. The original achiral addition produces both stereoisomers; hydrolysis does not select L-alanine. The selected geothermal hydrolysis window is conditional, not a measured rate claim from Miller (1953)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "FeS-mediated reductive amination with explicit pyrite-forming sulfur oxidation", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_003", "mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Fresh mackinawite as a reactive surface and consumed reagent"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348 K", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; the cited aqueous benchmark does not establish this pressure dependence", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool anoxic vent-fed porewater, pH 9-10. Iron-route laboratory ammonia benchmarks are approximately 0.375-1.5 M; such concentrations are an unresolved local requirement. Supply at least one external sulfide equivalent per FeS equivalent converted to pyrite. The sulfide node represents the H2S/HS- acid-base pool."}, "mechanism": "An ammonia-derived imine is reduced while sulfide sulfur is oxidized to disulfide sulfur in pyrite. One FeS plus one external sulfide can supply the two-electron budget per alanine formed.", "reasoning": "Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) support reductive amination on freshly precipitated FeS. The specific quantitative pyrite endpoint used here is a conditional, speculative closure because the supplied research does not establish the complete spent-phase inventory. Unlike an agent-only FeS annotation, this node explicitly consumes sulfur reductant and produces FeS2. Its implementation requires verifying pyrite formation and excluding alternative sulfur products. The reported high-ammonia experiments cannot establish the yield from the micromolar pyruvate supplied by rxn_001."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_015", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_016", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide-oxyhydroxide assemblage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; laboratory benchmark approximately ambient pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sediment porewater downstream of the hotter source; pH 10; initial Fe(II):Fe(III) approximately 1:1; 72-hour reference duration. A substantial ammonia reservoir is required; the supplied review places earlier iron systems in the approximately 0.1-1.5 M range. Initial ferric material is supplied without invoking molecular oxygen."}, "mechanism": "Surface-associated pyruvate and ammonia undergo reductive amination. Two ferrous equivalents are oxidized per alanine. Fe(OH)2 and FeOOH provide explicit idealized reduced and oxidized solid inventories; pre-existing FeOOH is retained while additional FeOOH is formed.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. (2019) found strong pH and mixed-valence dependence, with the highest reported alanine fraction at pH 10, 343 K and approximately equal Fe(II)/Fe(III). Their approximately 70% value describes detected liquid-phase organics, not automatically isolated yield from all starting carbon. The mechanism and final mineral assemblage were not established. Consequently, FeOOH is a specified, atom-balanced oxidation endpoint for this conditional model, not an experimentally identified unique spent phase. Pure ferrous hydroxide is not assumed to reproduce the mixed-valence result."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Stored Fe0-driven amination on pre-reduced FeS particles", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["FeS-supported metallic iron inventory supplied as pre-reduced particles"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298 K", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; laboratory amination benchmark approximately ambient pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool anoxic mineral pore; pH 9.6; 0.5 M total ammonia; 48 hours. The cited experiment used 5 mM keto acid and FeS pretreated at -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days. This network instead declares the resulting Fe0 inventory an external, finite feedstock and does not claim to generate it electrochemically."}, "mechanism": "An imine-derived intermediate accepts two electrons from Fe0 oxidation to Fe(II). Ferrous hydroxide is the modeled alkaline corrosion product, with water explicitly supplying its oxygen and hydrogen.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) demonstrated efficient amination using electrochemically pre-reduced FeS containing metallic iron. The reducing inventory is not a perpetually regenerating catalyst: Fe0 appears as an input and an Fe(II) product appears as an output. Formation of the starting pre-reduced particles under natural conditions remains unresolved and is not hidden inside the agents field. Fe(OH)2 is a conditional alkaline corrosion endpoint, not a claim that it was the only measured product. Near-complete laboratory conversion cannot be transferred to a micromolar hydrothermal feed."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed H2 reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Metallic nickel on a silicate support"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 fugacity; total pore pressure may be higher", "catalyst": "Ni0; laboratory benchmark Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool, sealed anoxic mineral pore; pH 7-8; 24-hour laboratory benchmark. Low-ammonia comparison uses approximately 6 mM total ammonia. Published screening used 30 mM pyruvate, whereas the internally supplied stream remains at most approximately 10 micromolar before dilution."}, "mechanism": "Nickel dissociates H2 and hydrogenates an ammonia-derived imine. H2 supplies the reducing equivalents, and water is the net oxygen-containing coproduct.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) support Ni/H2 amination under mild conditions, including measurable conversion at approximately 6 mM ammonia. This relaxes, but does not eliminate, the nitrogen-concentration problem. Their low-ammonia percentage is referenced to ammonia and must not be compared directly with percentages referenced to pyruvate. Natural exposed Ni0, resistance to sulfide passivation, and maintained dissolved H2 are necessary boundary conditions. The laboratory supported catalyst is not presumed identical to an indigenous mineral surface."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Fe-Ni meteoritic-alloy H2 reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Exposed Fe-Ni alloy comparable to the tested Aletai meteorite, approximately 18 mol% Ni"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 fugacity; total pore pressure may be higher", "catalyst": "Reactive Fe-Ni alloy surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic H2-bearing porewater, pH 7-8, approximately 24-hour laboratory comparison. Alloy composition, available surface area and corrosion history must be characterized. An accessible meteoritic metal fragment is an external geological boundary stock, not newly synthesized here."}, "mechanism": "Alloy-associated nickel activates H2 for imine hydrogenation. H2, rather than unrecorded iron corrosion, supplies the reducing equivalents assigned to this reaction node.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) observed alanine formation using the Aletai meteorite and substantial accompanying lactate, reported as approximately 24% alanine and 43% lactate in the cited experiment. This is a distinct experimentally tested catalyst implementation, not an independent amination mechanism. Sulfidation, hydroxide coatings and changing alloy exposure may reduce activity. The graph does not borrow additional electrons from alloy corrosion without representing an oxidation product."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Condensation of pyruvate with hydroxylamine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K, proposed implementation window", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; no pressure-enhancement claim", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous compartment; proposed pH 5-7 for oxime formation. Hydroxylamine is an explicit external stock, initially at least comparable to available pyruvate. Its source, lifetime and compatibility with dissolved metals are unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine adds to the ketone carbonyl and eliminates water, forming pyruvate oxime with unspecified E/Z composition.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) demonstrated amino-acid formation following hydroxylamine and Fe0 addition to an iron-promoted network. Oxime formation provides the chemically coherent intermediate connecting pyruvate to that reduction. The stated pH and temperature window is a proposed staging choice, not a verified transcription of the primary recipe. Prebiotic hydroxylamine availability is the principal additional feedstock bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Four-electron Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Reactive zero-valent iron surface, consumed during reduction"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K, proposed implementation window", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; no pressure-enhancement claim", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic water; proposed pH 8-9 after oxime formation, allowing ferrous-hydroxide precipitation. Supply at least two reactive Fe0 equivalents per oxime reduced. Carbonate or sulfide capture of Fe(II) would require a different explicitly balanced endpoint."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the C=N functionality and cleavage of the N-O bond produce the amino group. Two Fe0 atoms oxidized to Fe(II) supply the four electrons required per alanine; water and Fe(OH)2 close the oxygen and hydrogen inventory.", "reasoning": "The hydroxylamine/Fe0 amino-acid route is supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Unlike two-electron imine hydrogenation, oxime-to-amine conversion requires four reducing equivalents; the represented iron inventory supplies them explicitly. The chosen Fe(OH)2 product is a conditional speciation model rather than a verified unique spent phase. No stereochemical selection follows from the achiral reduction."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "CO2-dependent cyanide-ammonia conversion of pyruvate through a Bucherer-Bergs-type network", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_004", "mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K, proposed mild aqueous implementation", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; cited mild aqueous chemistry does not establish this pressure dependence", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic carbonate-bearing porewater; proposed pH 8-9. Ammonia, free cyanide and dissolved inorganic carbon must coexist. Trial boundary stocks of 0.1 M total ammonia and 1-10 mM total cyanide are explicit assumptions, not a verified primary-paper recipe. Separate the fluid from strongly cyanide-binding mineral surfaces. Internally supplied pyruvate remains micromolar or lower."}, "mechanism": "A lumped formation-and-hydrolysis node represents the reported CO2-dependent hydantoin/carbamoyl reaction network. NH3 and CO2 are listed on both sides because their participation and release cannot be represented correctly by treating them as absent reagents. The fully hydrolyzed neutral-form net is balanceable with cyanide consumption and net CO2 release.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support conversion of alpha-ketoacids to corresponding amino acids using cyanide, ammonia and essential CO2. This node does not claim that an atom-balanced sum determines the elementary mechanism or isotopic provenance. Its complete-hydrolysis accounting allows ammonia released from nitrogen-containing intermediates and CO2 released from the combined carbon pools; exact experimental NH3/CO2 fluxes are not established by the supplied summary. Co-location of cyanide with dilute pyruvate, protection from metal complexation, and completion of hydrolysis remain integration gaps. This route is not treated as direct cyanide reduction of pyruvate without CO2."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as an organic thiol catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300 K, near-ambient scenario", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "3-Mercaptopropanoic acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, persistently wet sheltered pool; approximately 60 days. Explicit trial stocks: 10 mM glyceraldehyde, 0.1 M total ammonia and 10 mM thiol at pH 5.2. These concentrations and pH are scenario assumptions, not verified values from Weber's 1985 recipe. Shield the long incubation from direct UV."}, "mechanism": "A lumped low-yield channel involves triose dehydration, thiol-associated carbonyl intermediates and amination with internal redox redistribution. The net conversion releases water and requires no additional external electron donor.", "reasoning": "Weber (1985) observed alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium, with thiol-enhanced formation of approximately 0.6% after 60 days versus approximately 0.1% without thiol. Those yields are evidence of formation, not predictions for the trial loadings specified here. The net equation is already atom- and redox-balanced; adding an arbitrary reductant would misrepresent the chemistry. Availability and persistence of 3-mercaptopropanoic acid and month-long substrate survival are unresolved prebiotic requirements."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-Mercaptopropanoic acid as an organic thiol catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "313 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "3-Mercaptopropanoic acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sheltered geothermal pool, pH 5.2, approximately 35 days. Explicit trial stocks: 10 mM formaldehyde, 10 mM glycolaldehyde, 0.1 M total ammonia and 10 mM thiol. The temperature, pH and duration follow the supplied report; the concentrations are unvalidated trial assumptions because catalyst loading was not supplied."}, "mechanism": "Carbon-carbon bond formation accesses C3 carbonyl chemistry, followed by thiol-assisted rearrangement and amination. This lumped experimental channel has the same internally balanced overall oxidation-state redistribution as the triose route.", "reasoning": "Weber (1998) reported approximately 1.8% alanine on a formaldehyde basis after 35 days at 313 K and pH 5.2, with little formation without thiol. This is a distinct experimentally demonstrated feedstock entry route, although downstream chemistry overlaps rxn_013. Exact catalyst loading must be checked before quantitative reproduction; the stated trial stocks are not assigned the published yield. Low selectivity, competing sugar chemistry, thiol availability and UV protection remain significant limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Competing H2 reduction of pyruvate to lactate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_023"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Metallic nickel or reactive Fe-Ni alloy"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 fugacity", "catalyst": "Ni0 or Fe-Ni alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic porewater, pH 7-8, approximately 24-hour comparison. This channel competes directly with rxn_008 and rxn_009, particularly when imine formation is limited by ammonia availability."}, "mechanism": "H2 activation on the metal is followed by ketone hydrogenation to the secondary alcohol. Hydrogen is the explicit electron donor; no oxygen-containing coproduct is needed.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) measured competing lactate in Ni/H2 and meteoritic-alloy experiments. The reported product distributions demonstrate why alanine formation cannot be equated with complete pyruvate conversion. This atom-balanced sink uses H2 explicitly rather than treating the metal catalyst as an unspecified source of reducing power."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Competing lactate formation with oxidation of ferrous hydroxide", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_015", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_016"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Mixed-valence iron hydroxide-oxyhydroxide assemblage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343 K", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario; laboratory benchmark approximately ambient pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic porewater; pH 7 comparison; initial Fe(II):Fe(III) approximately 3:1; 72 hours. Ferric material is an initial mineral stock, not produced using molecular oxygen."}, "mechanism": "Two ferrous equivalents reduce the pyruvate carbonyl to lactate and oxidize to ferric oxyhydroxide. Initial FeOOH is retained, and additional FeOOH records the consumed reducing inventory.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. (2019) found no alanine under the cited pH 7, 75% Fe(II) condition and approximately 30-50% lactate after 72 hours. This provides a direct selectivity constraint on rxn_006. As in that node, the FeOOH endpoint is a conditional solid-speciation closure rather than an identified unique spent phase; nevertheless, both electron-donor oxidation and all atoms are explicit."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Conditional pyrite-coupled lactate side channel", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_012", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Fresh mackinawite, consumed together with external sulfide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348 K", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfidic porewater, proposed pH 8-10. Relevant when ammonia-derived imine formation is slow. Requires the same verified pyrite-forming redox inventory as rxn_005."}, "mechanism": "Carbonyl reduction competes with imine reduction while sulfide sulfur is oxidized into pyrite disulfide. FeS, external sulfide and FeS2 explicitly close the two-electron budget.", "reasoning": "This catalyst-specific side channel is speculative: the supplied research establishes iron-mediated amination and widespread lactate competition, but not a quantitative pyrite-coupled lactate yield under these exact conditions (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Barge et al., 2019). It is included as a chemically plausible competing sink, not an experimentally calibrated reaction. Its sulfur endpoint must be tested together with rxn_005."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Competing Fe0-driven reduction of pyruvate to lactate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Reactive zero-valent iron surface, consumed during reduction"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-323 K", "pressure": "100 bar hydrostatic pore scenario", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic mineral pore; proposed pH 8-9 for the represented Fe(OH)2 endpoint. Competes with amination where ammonia activity or imine coverage is insufficient. No numerical yield is assigned."}, "mechanism": "Oxidation of Fe0 to Fe(II) supplies the two electrons needed for ketone reduction. Water provides the hydrogen and the oxygen incorporated into the modeled ferrous-hydroxide corrosion product.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) demonstrate the importance of metal identity and surface preparation for reductive selectivity. The exact rate and Fe(OH)2 endpoint of this standalone side channel are conditional, not quantitatively established by the supplied summary. It is explicitly balanced and does not use unrepresented proton or electron reservoirs."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker pathway", "description": "Externally supplied acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia form a racemic aminonitrile, followed by staged hydrolysis. Produces L-alanine and an equal amount of D-alanine; hydrolysis conditions and precursor concentrations remain environmental constraints.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Hydrothermal pyruvate with fresh FeS and sulfide", "description": "Dilute mineral-derived pyruvate undergoes FeS-associated reductive amination. The represented sulfur-to-pyrite electron closure is conditional and must be verified; no high downstream yield is inferred from the dilute source.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Hydrothermal pyruvate with mixed-valence iron hydroxides", "description": "Pyruvate enters a cooler alkaline mineral pore containing both ferrous and ferric components. Ferrous oxidation supports racemic alanine formation, while a shift toward neutral pH favors the lactate side channel.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Hydrothermal pyruvate with a finite pre-reduced FeS-associated Fe0 inventory", "description": "Externally supplied pre-reduced mineral particles provide explicitly consumed metallic iron. The route does not assume natural regeneration of the laboratory-prepared reducing inventory or transfer near-complete millimolar conversion to micromolar feed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Hydrothermal pyruvate with Ni-catalyzed H2 amination", "description": "Cooling delivers pyruvate to an H2-bearing nickel interface. This implementation has experimental support at lower ammonia concentrations than many iron routes, but still requires accessible Ni0, sustained H2 and competition control.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Hydrothermal pyruvate with meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy and H2", "description": "A reactive Fe-Ni fragment substitutes for the supported nickel catalyst. This experimentally distinct implementation shares the H2 reductive-amination mechanism and can produce substantial lactate alongside both alanine enantiomers.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Pyruvate oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction", "description": "An external hydroxylamine stock converts pyruvate to its oxime. Explicit four-electron reduction by metallic iron yields racemic alanine. Hydroxylamine availability and the required staging of aqueous conditions are additional bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "CO2-dependent cyanide-ammonia pathway from pyruvate", "description": "Pyruvate enters a separate carbonate-, cyanide- and ammonia-bearing reaction compartment. A lumped Bucherer-Bergs-type formation-and-hydrolysis node reaches racemic alanine; co-location, mineral compatibility and complete hydrolysis are not assumed demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Glyceraldehyde-thiol pathway", "description": "Externally supplied racemic glyceraldehyde and ammonium undergo a slow thiol-assisted net conversion. The reported low-yield route produces L-alanine within a racemate without cyanide or an added external reductant.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-thiol pathway", "description": "Externally supplied C1 and C2 aldehydes enter a demonstrated thiol-assisted C3 assembly and amination channel. It is distinct from direct glyceraldehyde feeding, although downstream chemistry overlaps that route.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_005", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_015", "mol_023"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_005", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017", "rxn_018"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_011", "rxn_018"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_016"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_023", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017", "rxn_018"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 623453.5140991211, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T03:35:46.897600", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct ten distinct reaction sequences that produce L-alanine as one component of racemic alanine. These include experimentally distinguishable catalyst implementations, not ten independent mechanisms. The network combines Strecker chemistry, five implementations of pyruvate amination, hydroxylamine chemistry, and two thiol-assisted sugar routes. Hydrothermal CO2 reduction supplies a modeled pyruvate source, but its reported micromolar productivity is not silently converted into the millimolar substrate concentrations used in downstream experiments. Cooling into adjacent anoxic porewaters is a conditional transport link; recovery, dilution and reaction rates at those low concentrations remain unresolved. Acetaldehyde, HCN, ammonia, sugar feedstocks, hydroxylamine, reactive minerals and organic thiols are explicit external boundary stocks. Every represented reduction includes its electron donor and an oxidation product. Where mineral oxidation products were not established experimentally, a specific, atom-balanced endpoint is identified as a conditional closure rather than a measured result. Neutral formulas are used for acid-base families; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Both alanine enantiomers are represented separately, and achiral synthesis nodes produce them equally. Enantioenrichment is a separate unresolved module: no reaction is invented that converts D-alanine into L-alanine or produces enantiopure material. CPL can create small excesses, but the supplied evidence does not establish a terrestrial L-favoring irradiation field or sufficiently characterized photoproducts for a closed enrichment reaction (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). Thus the target is reached molecularly, not as an isolated homochiral product. The environment_formed field records a representative formation setting, not an exclusive location. The intermediate role also covers terminal coproducts because the schema has no byproduct category.\n\nMOLECULES (23 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_007: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C3H6N2 (alpha-aminopropionitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H8N2O (alaninamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_011: C3H7NO2 (D-alanine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: FeS (mackinawite) [starting]\n  - mol_013: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_014: FeS2 (pyrite) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: FeH2O2 (ferrous hydroxide) [starting]\n  - mol_016: FeHO2 (ferric oxyhydroxide) [starting]\n  - mol_017: Fe (zero-valent iron) [starting]\n  - mol_018: H3NO (hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_019: C3H5NO3 (pyruvic acid oxime) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C3H6O3 (glyceraldehyde, racemic feedstock) [starting]\n  - mol_021: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_022: C2H4O2 (glycolaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_023: C3H6O3 (lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified) [hub_intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (18 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_005, mol_003 (Mineral-assisted hydrothermal CO2 reduction to pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_002: mol_006, mol_004, mol_007 -> mol_008, mol_003 (Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_008, mol_003 -> mol_009 (Aminonitrile hydration to alaninamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_004 (Alan\u093f\u0928amide hydrolysis to both alanine enantiomers) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_005, mol_004, mol_012, mol_013 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_003, mol_014 (FeS-mediated reductive amination with explicit pyrite-forming sulfur oxidation) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_006: mol_005, mol_004, mol_015, mol_016 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_016, mol_003 (Mixed-valence iron-hydroxide reductive amination) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_007: mol_005, mol_004, mol_017, mol_003 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_015 (Stored Fe0-driven amination on pre-reduced FeS particles) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_008: mol_005, mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_003 (Nickel-catalyzed H2 reductive amination) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_009: mol_005, mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_003 (Fe-Ni meteoritic-alloy H2 reductive amination) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_005, mol_018 -> mol_019, mol_003 (Condensation of pyruvate with hydroxylamine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_019, mol_017, mol_003 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_015 (Four-electron Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_012: mol_005, mol_007, mol_004, mol_001, mol_003 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_004, mol_001 (CO2-dependent cyanide-ammonia conversion of pyruvate through a Bucherer-Bergs-type network) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_013: mol_020, mol_004 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_003 (Thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_014: mol_021, mol_022, mol_004 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_003 (Thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_015: mol_005, mol_002 -> mol_023 (Competing H2 reduction of pyruvate to lactate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_016: mol_005, mol_015, mol_016 -> mol_023, mol_016 (Competing lactate formation with oxidation of ferrous hydroxide) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_017: mol_005, mol_012, mol_013 -> mol_023, mol_014 (Conditional pyrite-coupled lactate side channel) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_018: mol_005, mol_017, mol_003 -> mol_023, mol_015 (Competing Fe0-driven reduction of pyruvate to lactate) [Biochemical]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Acetaldehyde Strecker pathway (3 steps)\n  - P2: Hydrothermal pyruvate with fresh FeS and sulfide (2 steps)\n  - P3: Hydrothermal pyruvate with mixed-valence iron hydroxides (2 steps)\n  - P4: Hydrothermal pyruvate with a finite pre-reduced FeS-associated Fe0 inventory (2 steps)\n  - P5: Hydrothermal pyruvate with Ni-catalyzed H2 amination (2 steps)\n  - P6: Hydrothermal pyruvate with meteoritic Fe-Ni alloy and H2 (2 steps)\n  - P7: Pyruvate oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction (3 steps)\n  - P8: CO2-dependent cyanide-ammonia pathway from pyruvate (2 steps)\n  - P9: Glyceraldehyde-thiol pathway (1 steps)\n  - P10: Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-thiol pathway (1 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 18 reactions | 5 balanced as written | 13 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 balanceable: 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> C3H4O3 + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_004 balanceable: 2 C3H8N2O + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_005 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 FeS + 2 H2S -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O + 2 FeS2\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 4 FeH2O2 + 5 FeHO2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 5 FeHO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_007 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 Fe + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 FeH2O2\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: 2 C3H5NO3 + 4 Fe + 6 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 4 FeH2O2\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 CHN + NH3 + 3 CO2 + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + NH3 + 3 CO2\n  - rxn_013 balanceable: 2 C3H6O3 + 2 NH3 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_014 balanceable: 2 CH2O + 2 C2H4O2 + 2 NH3 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_016 balanceable: C3H4O3 + 2 FeH2O2 + 3 FeHO2 -> C3H6O3 + 3 FeHO2\n  - rxn_018 balanceable: C3H4O3 + Fe + 2 H2O -> C3H6O3 + FeH2O2\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Alanine: Routes, Evidence, Conditions and Contested Points\n\nRacemic alanine is one of the easiest proteinogenic amino acids to make abiotically. It has been synthesized from several unrelated feedstocks: HCN with acetaldehyde and NH3 (Strecker), pyruvate with NH3 and a reductant (reductive amination), pyruvate with cyanide, NH3 and CO2 (Bucherer\u2013Bergs), and aldehyde/sugar chemistry with NH3. It also arrives in meteorites. No experiment, however, makes the **L** enantiomer selectively. The real bottleneck for \"L-alanine\" is enantioenrichment, plus the problem of joining each route's feedstock synthesis to its amination step under one set of conditions. Which route is \"best\" depends on which framework you accept, and each is contested on yield, concentration, or geochemical plausibility.\n\n## TL;DR\n- **Two families of routes dominate, and both work in the lab.** (1) Aldehyde/cyanide chemistry: Strecker from acetaldehyde + HCN + NH3, starting with Miller's 1953 spark discharge, and today Sutherland's cyanosulfidic network. (2) Keto-acid chemistry: reductive amination of pyruvate on FeS, green rust, electro-reduced FeS, Fe0 or Ni0/H2. Pyruvate amination gives the highest single-step yields (~50\u201370%, \"nearly complete\" in some runs), but needs high NH3, alkaline pH and a pyruvate source that is itself low-yield (\u226410 \u00b5M from CO2 + H2).\n- **Every demonstrated route gives racemic alanine.** The only claimed natural L-excess, ~33% L-alanine in Murchison, was reported by Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature*) on the basis of \"nearly equal, extraterrestrial \u03b415N values measured for each alanine enantiomer\". It was disputed (Pizzarello & Cronin 1998). Later Murchison and Bennu analyses found alanine racemic. Asymmetric photolysis by circularly polarized light gives only percent-level excesses. Getting from racemic to L is unsolved for every framework.\n- **Environment is unresolved.** Alkaline hydrothermal/serpentinizing settings best fit the keto-acid routes, but face the \"hot-vent decomposition\" critique (Miller & Bada 1988). Surface UV/sulfidic or atmospheric-discharge settings fit the HCN routes, but depend on reducing gases or locally concentrated HCN/sulfide. For network design, treat pyruvate\u21c4alanine as a robust, multi-catalyst node. Treat the upstream C3 source and the chirality step as the open problems.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Route summary (evidence-weighted)**\n\n| Route | Feedstocks | Key intermediate(s) | Representative conditions | Best reported alanine outcome | Main critique |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark-discharge / Strecker | CH4, NH3, H2, H2O (or CO2/N2 with buffering) | HCN, acetaldehyde, \u03b1-aminopropionitrile | Electric discharge, then aqueous Strecker | Glycine 2.1% of CH4-carbon; alanine \u22480.54 mol per mol glycine (Miller) | Needs reducing atmosphere; neutral CO2/N2 atmospheres give much less unless nitrite is quenched |\n| Cyanosulfidic (Sutherland) | HCN, H2S/HS\u207b, Cu(I), UV, phosphate | Aldehydes from reductive HCN homologation; aminonitriles | 254 nm UV, sulfidic water | 12 amino acids accessed via Strecker | Local high HCN/sulfide; UV wavelength; stepwise \"one-pot\" claims |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs from \u03b1-ketoacids | Pyruvate, cyanide, NH3, CO2 | Hydantoin / N-carbamoyl amino acid | Aqueous, mild; CO2 essential | \u03b1-Amino acids from \u03b1-ketoacids | Still needs cyanide plus a pyruvate source |\n| Reductive amination on Fe minerals | Pyruvate, NH3, Fe(II)/Fe(III) minerals | Iminopyruvate | FeS/Fe(OH)2 at 50\u201375 \u00b0C; green rust 50\u201370 \u00b0C, alkaline | Up to ~50% (FeS); ~70% (green rust, pH 10, 70 \u00b0C, 72 h) | Requires 0.1\u20131.5 M NH3; lactate competes; stoichiometric mineral reductant |\n| Electro-reduced FeS / Fe0 | Pyruvate, NH3, H2 / electrochemical potential | Iminopyruvate | pH 9.6, 0.5 M NH3, 23\u201325 \u00b0C, 48 h | \"Nearly complete\" conversion | Requires a sustained negative potential in vents |\n| Ni0 / H2 | Pyruvate, NH3, H2 | Iminopyruvate | 22 \u00b0C, pH 7\u20138, 5 bar H2 | 18\u201340% (Ni or Pd/C); 17% at only 6 mM NH3 (Ni) | Native Ni availability; high H2 pressure |\n| Hydroxylamine + Fe0 | Pyruvate, NH2OH, Fe0 | Pyruvate oxime | Aqueous, mild | Alanine among 4 amino acids | NH2OH prebiotic availability |\n| Transamination | Pyruvate + another amino acid, pyridoxal, metal ions | Pyridoxamine / aldimine | Aqueous; Fe(III)/Al(III) cooperativity | Aspartate\u2192alanine works; pyridoxal raises Ni/H2 alanine from 7.6% to 34% | Pyridoxal's own prebiotic origin |\n| Thiol-catalyzed sugar route (Weber) | Glyceraldehyde, or HCHO + glycolaldehyde, NH3, thiol | Methylglyoxal-type dehydration products, hemithioacetal, imine | 40 \u00b0C, pH 5.2, 35 days | 1.8% (HCHO basis); 0.6% after 60 days (glyceraldehyde) | Low yield, slow; mercaptopropionic acid as catalyst |\n| Formose-type parent-body chemistry | HCHO, glycolaldehyde, acetaldehyde, NH3 | Aldimines / aminonitrile-free routes | 60\u2013250 \u00b0C aqueous | \u03b1-, \u03b2-, \u03b3-amino acids including alanine | Asteroidal rather than terrestrial; racemic |\n| Delivery / interstellar ice | CH3OH, NH3, H2O, CO ices + UV; parent-body chemistry | Radicals; Strecker precursors | Cryogenic UV; meteorite parent bodies | Racemic alanine (Bennu, ice residues) | Delivered amounts; survival on impact |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Strecker chemistry (HCN + acetaldehyde + NH3)\n\n**Chemistry.** CH3CHO + NH3 \u2192 imine. Adding HCN gives \u03b1-aminopropionitrile, and hydrolysis (via the amide) gives alanine. Strecker made alanine this way from acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN in 1850, the first laboratory amino acid synthesis.\\[1\\] In Strecker chemistry the imine/aminonitrile and the cyanohydrin are in equilibrium until hydrolysis begins. Where NH3 is low or pH is low, aminonitriles partly convert to cyanohydrins, which hydrolyze to lactate rather than alanine (as noted in the 2021 *Science Advances* paper \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\").\\[2\\]\n\n**Miller\u2013Urey evidence.** Miller (1953, *Science*) sparked CH4/NH3/H2/H2O and obtained glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine and \u03b1-aminobutyric acid.\\[3\\] Miller attributed this to Strecker chemistry: HCN and aldehydes formed in the gas phase reacted in the water.\\[3\\] Bada's retrospective (M\u00e8tode, citing Miller & Urey 1959) gives these yields relative to glycine = 1: alanine \u2248 0.54 mol, \u03b2-alanine \u2248 0.24 mol. From 710 mg of methane carbon, 4.8 mg glycine formed.\\[3\\]\\[4\\] Johnson et al. (2008, *Science*) give glycine at 2.1% of methane carbon for the classic apparatus, versus 1.8% and 0.4% for the other two.\\[5\\] Miller estimated that 50\u201360% of the methane carbon became organic compounds, mostly polymeric material.\\[3\\]\n\n**Variants.**\n- **H2S-rich atmosphere.** Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS*) reanalyzed a 1958 Miller experiment run with CH4, NH3, H2S and CO2. It produced 23 amino acids and 4 amines, with high relative abundances of alkyl amino acids including alanine.\\[6\\]\\[7\\]\n- **Neutral atmosphere.** Cleaves et al. (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) showed that CO2/N2 atmospheres give far more amino acids than previously thought, including alanine, once nitrite/nitrate (which destroy amino acids during workup) are controlled and the solution is buffered. The alanine was racemic within experimental error.\\[8\\]\\[9\\]\n\n**Cyanosulfidic protometabolism (Sutherland school).** Patel et al. (2015, *Nature Chemistry*) showed that UV-driven (254 nm), HS\u207b/Cu-mediated reductive homologation of HCN produces precursors of ribonucleotides, lipids and amino acids. In that network 12 amino acids are reached by Strecker synthesis.\\[2\\]\\[10\\]\\[11\\] Alanine is fairly photostable in these systems. At high pH it photolyzes slowly (~6% products after 2.5 h), and NaSH suppresses its photodegradation. Slow oxidation of alanine to pyruvate was also observed (2021 *Sci. Adv.*).\\[2\\] This links the cyanide and keto-acid worlds.\n\n**Isotopic fingerprint.** Murchison alanine has a \u00b9\u00b3C-rich \u03b1-carbon, consistent with Strecker synthesis from acetaldehyde with a \u00b9\u00b3C-rich carbonyl carbon (Chimiak et al. 2020). Experiments then measured Strecker-specific isotope effects for alanine, e.g. a 55.1\u2030 nitrogen equilibrium isotope effect between NH3 and the aminonitrile (*Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*, 2022).\\[12\\] This is the strongest natural-sample evidence that Strecker chemistry actually made alanine somewhere in the early Solar System.\n\n**Critiques.**\n- The early atmosphere was probably weakly reducing or neutral (CO2/N2), which sharply lowers HCN and aldehyde production.\\[5\\]\n- The cyanosulfidic network needs locally high HCN and sulfide. Ranjan et al. (arXiv 1801.07725) analyzed sulfidic anion concentrations as an extra local constraint.\\[11\\]\n- Strecker chemistry is aldehyde-based. Extant biochemistry makes alanine from pyruvate. This \"disconnect\" is the main congruence critique raised by Pulletikurti et al. (2022).\\[13\\]\n\n### 2. Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from pyruvate (cyanide + NH3 + CO2)\n\nPulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142\u20131150) showed that \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources to give the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids by the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway (hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates).\\[14\\] The same chemistry gives dihydroorotate from oxaloacetate.\\[15\\] CO2 is mechanistically essential. Krishnamurthy: \"If you mix only the keto acid, cyanide and ammonia, it just sits there. As soon as you add carbon dioxide, even trace amounts, the reaction picks up speed.\"\\[16\\] This route is a bridge: it uses a cyanide feedstock but a metabolic (keto-acid) carbon skeleton.\\[13\\] Its weakness is that it still needs both a pyruvate source and cyanide in the same place. A related paper (Yadav et al. 2022, *Nature Chemistry*) showed cyanide acting as a reductant on TCA intermediates, but pyruvate itself was not reduced.\\[17\\]\\[18\\]\n\n### 3. Reductive amination of pyruvate (metabolism-first / hydrothermal frameworks)\n\n**Chemistry.** Pyruvate + NH3 + 2H\u207a + 2e\u207b \u2192 alanine + H2O, via iminopyruvate.\\[19\\] This is the same transformation alanine dehydrogenase catalyzes with NADH.\\[20\\] The universal competing reaction is reduction of pyruvate to lactate. Product selectivity depends on pH, NH3 concentration and the reductant.\n\n**Iron sulfide / ferrous hydroxide (W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user school).**\n- Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695\u20131697) reported that \"amino acids are formed efficiently by reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids under aqueous conditions with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 and with NH3\u2026 at pH values near their pKa\" (pH ~9\u201310 for NH3).\\[21\\]\n- A secondary summary (Milner-White & Russell 2008, *Biology Direct*) quotes alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine yields up to ~50% at 50 and 75 \u00b0C.\\[22\\] The primary table was not verified here.\n- These experiments used large NH3 excesses. Kaur et al. (2024) characterize earlier Fe-based reductive aminations as using \"375 mM to 1.5 M\" ammonia.\\[23\\]\n- FeS acts as a stoichiometric reductant (oxidized toward pyrite/Fe(III)), not as a true catalyst.\\[19\\]\n\n**Mixed-valence iron hydroxides (green rust; Russell/Barge alkaline-vent school).** Barge et al. (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828\u20134833) reacted pyruvate + NH3 with Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxides:\\[24\\]\n- Alanine formed only when the mineral was partially oxidized. The maximum was at 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III).\\[24\\]\n- Alkaline pH was required. At 75% Fe(II) and pH 7, \"no alanine was produced\", and lactate rose to 30\u201350% after 72 h. Lactate is favored at low pH.\n- The highest yield was at pH 10, Fe(II):Fe(III) = 1:1 and 70 \u00b0C: \"\u223c70%\" alanine with \"\u223c30% lactate\", relative to all organics detected in the liquid phase. At 50 \u00b0C \"the same reactions proceeded but more slowly.\"\n- Neither end member worked: \"Purely ferrous hydroxides did not drive reductive amination but instead promoted pyruvate reduction to lactate, and ferric hydroxides did not result in any reaction.\"\n- The authors place this in iron-rich sediments near alkaline vents, where redox and pH gradients would set alanine/lactate selectivity. They state that \"the precise mechanism of pyruvate reductive amination to alanine in these systems was not established.\"\\[24\\]\\[25\\]\n\n**Electro-reduced FeS and native metals (Kitadai/Nakamura).** Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances* 5:eaav7848):\n- FeS was electrolyzed at \u22120.7 V (vs SHE) for 7 days, producing partial Fe0 (\"FeS_PERM\"). The authors argue such potentials arise naturally in H2-rich alkaline vents.\\[26\\]\n- At pH 9.6 (0.5 M NH3), 5 mM pyruvate and \u03b1-ketoglutarate \"reacted nearly completely\" to alanine and glutamate within 48 h at 23\u201325 \u00b0C.\\[26\\]\n- In a competitive one-pot run (four keto acids, 1.25 mM each), all amino acids formed in >50% yield, with alanine and glutamate favored.\\[27\\]\n- Pure Fe0 was less selective: it also made pyruvate and alanine by oxaloacetate decarboxylation.\\[27\\]\n\n**Ni0/H2 (Moran/Martin school).** Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem* 10:1528\u20131540):\n- Screening conditions: 30 mM pyruvate, 5 bar H2, 22 \u00b0C, pH 7\u20138, 24 h. Ni/SiO2\u2013Al2O3 or Pd/C gave 18\u201340% alanine with 5\u201315% lactate.\\[23\\]\n- At only 6 mM NH3, alanine reached up to 17% with Ni and 44% with Pd (relative to ammonia). This substantially relaxes the ammonia-concentration critique of the Fe-based work.\\[23\\]\n- The Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni) gave 24% alanine and 43% lactate.\\[23\\]\n- The system also runs a reverse-Krebs-cycle analog, making keto acids and amino acids in one pot.\\[28\\]\n- Follow-up work without H2 (Ni at 100 \u00b0C, pH 11, 18 h) aminated 6.4% of pyruvate to alanine.\\[29\\]\n\n**Hydroxylamine + Fe0.** Muchowska, Varma & Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104\u2013107) showed an Fe\u00b2\u207a-promoted network in which pyruvate and glyoxylate build 9 of 11 TCA-cycle intermediates. Adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 gave four biological amino acids, including alanine, by oxime reduction \"in a manner that parallels biosynthesis\".\\[30\\] The prebiotic source of NH2OH is the weak link.\\[26\\]\n\n**Where does the pyruvate come from?** This is the upstream bottleneck for every keto-acid route.\n- Cody et al. (2000, *Science*) made pyruvate from formic acid with nonyl thiol and FeS at 250 \u00b0C and 200 MPa. W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user himself noted the paradox: pyruvate is thermally fragile (it decomposes near 165 \u00b0C).\\[31\\]\n- Preiner et al. (2020, *Nature Ecology & Evolution* 4:534\u2013542) showed greigite (Fe3S4), magnetite (Fe3O4) and awaruite (Ni3Fe) fixing CO2 with H2 at 100 \u00b0C under alkaline conditions. Their abstract reports \"formate (up to 200 mM), acetate (up to 100 \u00b5M), pyruvate (up to 10 \u00b5M), methanol (up to 100 \u00b5M) and methane\". Higher figures quoted in a 2023 *Frontiers in Microbiology* review (560 \u00b5M acetate, 0.3 M formate) do not match this primary abstract.\n- Huber, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user and co-workers made pyruvate from acetylene + CO on NiS above 100 \u00b0C (2020, *Life*/PMC).\\[32\\]\n\nA tens-of-percent amination step fed by micromolar pyruvate gives micromolar alanine at best. Concentrating mechanisms (pores, thermophoresis, mineral adsorption) are invoked but not demonstrated for this specific pair.\n\n### 4. Transamination (pyridoxal and metal ions)\n\nPyridoxal (PL) transaminates amino acids without enzymes, and metal ions strongly modulate this.\\[33\\] Dherbassy, Mayer, Muchowska & Moran (2023, *JACS* 145:13357\u201313370) found cooperative metal\u2013pyridoxal catalysis, strongest with Fe(III) and Al(III) (roughly 180-fold over Fe(III) alone and 90-fold over pyridoxal alone, as quoted in a PMC review). They also found that the mechanism converges on the biological one.\\[34\\] Amino-group transfer from aspartate to pyruvate (giving alanine) proceeds readily, but alanine\u2192aspartate is poor (*Life* 2023, \"Prebiotic Synthesis of Aspartate Using Life's Metabolism as a Guide\").\\[35\\] In Ni/H2 systems (FEBS J), adding pyridoxal raised pyruvate\u2192alanine conversion from 7.6% to 34% at pH 11 and 80 \u00b0C, and up to 48% at 80\u2013100 \u00b0C. Pyridoxamine acts as the nitrogen shuttle and favors amination over lactate formation.\\[36\\] Counterpoint: Zabinski & Toney (2001, *JACS*) found that metal ions inhibit some PLP-catalyzed transaminations, so metal effects depend on the system.\\[37\\]\n\n### 5. Sugar/aldehyde routes without cyanide (Weber's \"triose model\")\n\nWeber (1985, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) made alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium at ambient temperature. A thiol (3-mercaptopropionic acid) made the reaction about six times faster: 0.6% vs 0.1% after 60 days. The proposed path runs through dehydration of glyceraldehyde to a methylglyoxal-type species, a hemithioacetal and an imine, with lactate as a parallel product.\\[38\\] Weber (1998, *OLEB* 28:259\u2013270) then showed that formaldehyde + glycolaldehyde (formose substrates) + NH3 + thiol at pH 5.2 and 40 \u00b0C for 35 days gave 1.8% alanine (on formaldehyde), and essentially none without thiol.\\[39\\] Attractions: no HCN, a link to formose sugars, and thioester (activated) intermediates. Critiques: low yield, month-long timescales, and an organic thiol catalyst whose own prebiotic source must be explained.\n\n### 6. Parent-body and interstellar synthesis (delivery framework)\n\n- **Parent-body aqueous chemistry.** Kebukawa et al. (2017, *Science Advances*) heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and ammonia (90\u2013250 \u00b0C) and made \u03b1-, \u03b2- and \u03b3-amino acids together with insoluble organic matter.\\[40\\] Koga & Naraoka (2017, *Scientific Reports*) made glycine, alanine and hydroxy amino acids from formaldehyde/acetaldehyde/NH3 at 60 \u00b0C and pH 11.5.\\[41\\] In their Murchison sample, \"the second most abundant amino acid was alanine (1.7 ppm) with a relative enrichment of L-alanine\". That L-enrichment conflicts with the racemic results discussed below.\n- **Interstellar ice photolysis.** UV-irradiated ice analogues give racemic amino acids, including alanine (Bernstein et al. 2002 and Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, *Nature* 416).\n- **Returned samples.** Bennu samples (Glavin, Dworkin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199\u2013210) contain 33 amino acids, 14 of them proteinogenic. The total is ~70 nmol/g, ~3.5\u00d7 less than Murchison, and alanine is racemic.\\[42\\]\\[43\\] The authors interpret the high glycine and racemic \u03b1-amino acids as consistent with HCN-polymerization/Strecker-type chemistry in an ammonia-rich parent body.\\[42\\]\n\nDelivery supplies alanine without terrestrial geochemical constraints. It is racemic, though, and quantitative impact survival and delivery fluxes are uncertain.\n\n### 7. Thermodynamics and stability (hydrothermal frameworks)\n\nAmend & Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659\u20131662) calculated that net alanine synthesis from CO2, NH4\u207a and H2 in a 100 \u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution releases 12.12 kJ/mol. Synthesis is exergonic there but endergonic in cold oxidized surface seawater.\\[44\\] This supports the idea that H2-rich hydrothermal mixing zones thermodynamically favor alanine. The kinetic counterpoint: Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009, *OLEB*) found that at 175 \u00b0C glycine and alanine peak after ~75 h and then decompose by deamination/decarboxylation.\\[45\\] Miller & Bada (1988, *Nature* 334:609\u2013611) argued that vent temperatures \"would not allow synthesis of organic compounds, but would decompose them, unless the exposure time at vent temperatures was short.\"\\[46\\] Note that the most productive keto-acid experiments (Barge, Kitadai, Kaur) run at 22\u201370 \u00b0C, well below black-smoker temperatures. That partly answers the critique for off-axis alkaline systems, but not for high-temperature (>150 \u00b0C) pyruvate formation steps like Cody et al.\n\n### 8. The \"L\" problem\n\nAll routes above give racemic alanine. The evidence on natural enantioenrichment:\n- **Murchison.** Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature* 389:265\u2013268) reported an L-alanine excess ([L \u2212 D]/[L + D]) of ~33%. They argued it was extraterrestrial on the basis of \"nearly equal, extraterrestrial \u03b415N values measured for each alanine enantiomer\". Pizzarello & Cronin (1998, *Nature* 394:236) found interior Murchison alanine racemic. They attributed the excess to exterior contamination or incomplete chromatographic resolution.\\[47\\] Glavin et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) found Murchison alanine \"both racemic (D \u2248 L) and heavily enriched in 13C, indicating no measurable terrestrial alanine contamination\" (16.5 nmol of racemic alanine in a 1 g extract). In the same samples glutamic acid showed ~15\u201339% L-excess.\n- **Bennu.** Alanine is racemic (2025).\\[48\\]\\[49\\]\n- **Circularly polarized light.** Asymmetric photolysis of racemic alanine films (Meinert et al. 2014, *Angewandte Chemie*) and CPL irradiation of ice analogues (Modica et al. 2014, *ApJ* 788:79; up to |1.34|% ee) induce only small, helicity- and wavelength-dependent excesses.\\[50\\]\\[51\\]\\[52\\]\\[53\\]\n\nSo no published route produces L-alanine selectively. An amplification mechanism (crystallization, autocatalysis, chiral templating, or later selection by chiral catalysts) has to be added to any route.\\[54\\] This is common to all frameworks and is not specific to alanine.\n\n## Catalysts and surfaces: what is actually shown\n\n- **No catalyst needed:** the Strecker steps (imine formation, cyanide addition, nitrile hydrolysis under acid or base) and Bucherer\u2013Bergs (needs CO2/bicarbonate as reagent, not a mineral catalyst). Spark discharge supplies the energy for HCN and aldehyde formation.\n- **Mineral or metal reductants/catalysts for pyruvate \u2192 alanine:**\n  - freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)2 (stoichiometric)\n  - mixed-valence green rust (Fe(II):Fe(III) \u2248 1:1)\n  - electro-reduced FeS / Fe0\n  - native Fe0 with NH2OH\n  - Ni0 or Pd with H2 (catalytic in metal, H2 as reductant)\n  - Ni-bearing meteorite metal\n- **Upstream C\u2013C bond formation (CO2 \u2192 pyruvate):** greigite, magnetite, awaruite (with H2); FeS with thiols at high T/P; NiS with CO/acetylene.\n- **Organocatalysts/cofactors:** thiols (Weber); pyridoxal with Fe(III)/Al(III) (transamination); Cu(I)/HS\u207b photoredox (cyanosulfidic).\n\n## Recommendations for synthesis-network design\n\n1. **Model pyruvate \u21c4 alanine as a reversible, multi-catalyst hub.** It is the single best-supported step across frameworks: Fe minerals, Ni/H2, NH2OH/Fe0, pyridoxal transamination, and Bucherer\u2013Bergs with cyanide. Always include lactate as the competing sink. Make selectivity a function of pH (alkaline favors alanine), NH3 activity and reductant redox state.\n2. **Treat the C3 source as the rate-limiting, framework-defining choice.** Use either acetaldehyde + HCN (Strecker; needs a reducing atmosphere or local HCN/sulfide) or CO2/H2 \u2192 pyruvate (\u00b5M yields on Fe/Ni minerals). Do not assume high pyruvate concentrations without an explicit concentrating mechanism.\n3. **Use realistic NH3.** Fe-mineral yields come from 0.1\u20131.5 M NH3. Only the Ni/H2 (Kaur 2024) and pyridoxal-shuttle data show useful yields near 5\u20136 mM. Flag any pathway that needs molar NH3.\n4. **Keep amination steps \u2264~100 \u00b0C.** The best amination data are at 22\u201375 \u00b0C. Above ~150 \u00b0C, alanine decomposes on timescales of days.\n5. **Add an explicit, separate enantioenrichment module.** No feedstock route provides it, and claims of natural L-alanine excess are not reliable.\n\n## Caveats and contested points\n\n- **Hydrothermal vs surface.** Contested. Vent chemistry has thermodynamic support (Amend & Shock) and mild-temperature experimental support (Barge, Kitadai, Kaur). It is still criticized for dilution, decomposition at high T, and reliance on lab-prepared minerals, electrochemical potentials or 5 bar H2. Surface cyanide chemistry is criticized for needing reducing atmospheres or local HCN/sulfide/UV, and for incongruence with keto-acid biochemistry.\n- **Yields not verified against primary tables.** The Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) ~50% figure is from a secondary review. Kitadai's alanine is described as \"nearly complete\" (exact percentage in their Table S1). Miller's alanine figure is given relative to glycine; the commonly quoted absolute value (~1.7% of carbon) was not confirmed from the 1955 *JACS* table.\n- **Yield definitions differ** (percent of keto acid, of NH3, of carbon), so cross-route comparisons in the table are indicative only.\n- **The NASA/JPL press release** on Barge et al. described the chemistry as \"reproducing origins of life on the ocean floor\"; that framing outruns the data, which show one reaction step.\\[55\\]\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [The 1953 Stanley L. Miller Experiment: Fifty Years of Prebiotic ...](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1023/A:1024807125069.pdf)\n2. [A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.abh3981)\n3. [One of the foremost experiments of the 20th century - Revista M\u00e8tode](https://metode.org/issues/monographs/one-of-the-foremost-experiments-of-the-20th-century.html)\n4. [M\u00e8tode Science Studies Journal ISSN: 2174-3487 metodessj@uv.es](https://www.redalyc.org/pdf/5117/511754471026.pdf)\n5. <https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Johnsonetal2008.pdf>\n6. [Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 ...](https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/691/analytical/PDF/Parker2011.pdf)\n7. [PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY Enhanced Synthesis of Alkyl Amino Acids in Miller\u2019s](https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Parker2011b.pdf)\n8. [PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY A Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral](https://www.bio.miami.edu/dana/dox/Cleaves2008.pdf)\n9. [The first peptides: the evolutionary transition between prebiotic amino acids and early proteins](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.2832)\n10. [Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism.](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Common-origins-of-RNA,-protein-and-lipid-precursors-Patel-Percivalle/3b482858daca31713acd680830b446c96e0