d attach reaction-specific experimental citations.\",\n        \"Account for cyanohydrin formation and competing lactate production when estimating alanine yield.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.65\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.78,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.66,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"The supplied Preiner et al. (2020) findings support mineral-assisted CO2/H2 production of trace pyruvate, while Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) support FeS-associated reductive amination. These do not establish the combined pathway at transported micromolar pyruvate concentrations. The explicit FeS/H2S-to-pyrite accounting is a plausible conditional redox closure, not a demonstrated mineral endpoint for this implementation. The corresponding pyrite-coupled lactate channel, rxn_017, is less directly supported than the general observation of competing lactate formation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Identify the actual upstream catalyst and conditions; generic mineral assistance does not establish equivalence to greigite, magnetite or awaruite experiments.\",\n        \"Specify fresh FeS preparation or supply, sulfide activity, ammonia concentration, pH and temperature for rxn_005.\",\n        \"Measure mineral oxidation products and substrate selectivity before treating pyrite formation as experimentally established.\",\n        \"Test amination after realistic cooling and dilution, rather than importing yields measured with millimolar pyruvate.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.86\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.92,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"Barge et al. (2019) directly support alanine and lactate formation in mixed-valence iron hydroxide systems. Including both products is appropriate. However, activity depends strongly on pH and mineral oxidation state; the results cannot be generalized to arbitrary mixtures of Fe(OH)2 and FeOOH. The stated ferric oxyhydroxide endpoint is a conditional representation of mineral oxidation, not a resolved experimental mechanism. Cooling is physically plausible, but substrate recovery and downstream performance at micromolar pyruvate remain unverified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify mineral phase, Fe(II):Fe(III) ratio, ammonia concentration and reaction time; distinguish the alkaline alanine-producing conditions from lower-pH lactate-favoring conditions.\",\n        \"Track initial Fe(III) separately from Fe(III) generated by oxidation of the ferrous reductant, using explicit stoichiometric coefficients.\",\n        \"Validate the cooled-feed experiment without equating the reported liquid-phase product proportions with overall carbon recovery.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.9\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.83,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.92,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"The supplied Kitadai et al. (2019) findings support reductive amination using pre-reduced FeS containing Fe0. Treating the reducing capacity as a finite supplied inventory avoids implying an unexplained sustained electrochemical input. Nevertheless, isolated Fe0 in the reaction equation does not fully specify the experimentally active FeS-associated material. Fe(OH)2 is an acceptable conditional accounting endpoint, but its actual formation and persistence depend on porewater composition. Upstream pyruvate delivery and the high-ammonia reaction environment remain conditional.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the Fe0 loading, accessible reducing capacity, FeS support and passivation behavior of the supplied material.\",\n        \"Define an aqueous environment consistent with the reported approximately room-temperature, pH 9.6, ammonia-rich experiments, or justify departures experimentally.\",\n        \"Measure iron oxidation products and alanine/lactate partitioning at the actual transported pyruvate concentration.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.89,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.83,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.74,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) provide strong experimental support for nickel-catalyzed H2 reductive amination and competing lactate formation. The explicit hydrogen donor and water product correctly describe the net amination. The environmental case still requires a specified nickel catalyst, maintained hydrogen activity and appropriate ammonia availability. Results obtained with a supported laboratory catalyst at 5 bar H2 do not automatically establish performance on an unspecified natural nickel surface. The hydrothermal-to-porewater concentration mismatch is acknowledged but unresolved.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify nickel oxidation state, support, loading and accessible surface area, distinguishing demonstrated catalyst formulations from proposed natural equivalents.\",\n        \"Define how the cooled compartment maintains the reported near-neutral pH, approximately 22 degrees C and sufficient H2 pressure or dissolved activity.\",\n        \"Measure selectivity and rate at micromolar pyruvate, retaining rxn_015 as a competing sink.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n      \"feedback\": \"The meteoritic-alloy implementation has direct support from the Aletai experiment reported by Kaur et al. (2024), making it more than a speculative substitution for nickel. That experiment also produced substantial lactate, so the included competing sink is important. A supplied alloy stock is a legitimate boundary condition, but its composition, corrosion state and catalytic accessibility must be specified. The network does not yet establish coexistence of an active alloy surface, sufficient H2 and recoverable hydrothermal pyruvate under the proposed porewater conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify an experimentally comparable alloy composition, exposed metal area and weathering state.\",\n        \"Retain the reported alanine/lactate competition rather than transferring selectivity from a different nickel catalyst.\",\n     