ersion.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the demonstrated thiol identity, such as 3-mercaptopropionic acid, its loading and its availability as a boundary stock.\",\n        \"Define pH, temperature and a physically justified residence time consistent with the glyceraldehyde experiment.\",\n        \"Annotate the lumped mechanism and quantify lactate and other competing products without presenting the net equation as a single elementary step.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.89,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"Weber (1998) directly supports thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and ammonia. The supplied literature reports approximately 1.8% alanine on a formaldehyde basis after 35 days at pH 5.2 and 40 degrees C. Those results support molecular target production, while the low yield does not itself make the route invalid. The Surface designation remains insufficient to verify sustained experimental conditions, thiol persistence or protection from competing aldehyde chemistry.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the thiol identity and loading, substrate concentrations and conditions corresponding to the cited experiment.\",\n        \"Provide a credible aqueous residence-time and replenishment model for the month-long reaction.\",\n        \"Track competing products and preserve the reported formaldehyde-based yield definition when estimating L-alanine output.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n  \"network_