"common_name\": \"oxaloacetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous hydroxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed local-stock implementation: 10-50 mM acetaldehyde, 10-100 mM total HCN/CN-, and 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia; pH 8.5-9.5; hours to several days. Use a shaded, poorly ventilated water pocket with replenished volatile feedstocks. These concentrations are boundary assumptions, not demonstrated concentrations in an early-Earth pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to acetaldehyde, followed by dehydration and cyanide addition to the resulting imine or iminium species. The net reaction produces aminonitrile and water. Addition to either face gives an achiral-environment racemic inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established Strecker chemistry, and atmospheric-discharge production of its precursors is consistent with Miller (1953). The network starts from the precursors rather than assigning a fictitious balanced equation to an entire discharge mixture. Cyanohydrin formation competes, especially when effective ammonia activity is low. Volatile-feedstock retention and the specified ammonia inventory are environmental limitations, not assumed consequences of a global reducing atmosphere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminonitrile hydration to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"aqueous acid providing catalytic hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed hydrolysis compartment: pH 1-2, approximately 1-20 mM aminonitrile after dilution, and residence for hours to days. A short drainage path into an adjacent volcanic-acid water pocket provides the necessary pH change. Acid capacity must exceed the carried-over ammonia alkalinity; quantitative transfer recovery is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed water addition to the nitrile gives the corresponding primary amide. One water molecule is incorporated per aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis through an amide is the established Strecker workup sequence (Strecker, 1850). The stated natural compartment and operating window are proposed implementations, not a reproduced geological experiment. Hydrolysis is a kinetic bottleneck at milder temperatures, and loss of aminonitrile before hydrolysis must be measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Alaninamide hydrolysis to racemic alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"aqueous acid providing catalytic hydronium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue in the acid hydrolysis compartment at pH 1-2 for hours to days, monitoring conversion rather than presuming complete hydrolysis. Ammonia is present predominantly as ammonium under these conditions. Cool the product solution after hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the carboxamide releases ammonia and produces alanine. The equation balances by processing equal amounts of the two alaninamide enantiomers to the separately represented L- and D-alanine products.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This closes the established Strecker sequence (Strecker, 1850). It produces actual L-alanine, but only alongside D-alanine. Moderate hydrolysis temperatures avoid invoking prolonged black-smoker exposure, which would increase amino-acid destruction risks (Miller and Bada, 1988; Aubrey et al., 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-333 K for the proposed formation stage; hydrolysis may require a subsequent 343-370 K stage\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar in a CO2-retaining water pocket\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed stocks: 5-30 mM pyruvate, 10-100 mM total cyanide, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia, and 10-100 mM dissolved inorganic carbon; initially pH 8-9.5. Follow conversion over days. If hydantoin or carbamoyl intermediates accumulate, a separate aqueous hydrolysis stage is required. These implementation concentrations and staging are not claimed to reproduce the exact published protocol.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry through hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates, with decarboxylation and hydrolysis. The net component balance per alanine is pyruvic acid + HCN + water -> alanine + CO2. Ammonia and additional CO2 participate in intermediate formation and are included on both sides as turnover inventories. This component balance does not establish carbon or nitrogen atom mapping.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) experimentally support CO2-dependent conversion of alpha-ketoacids using cyanide and ammonia. CO2 is mechanistically necessary and is not replaced by an arbitrary mineral catalyst. A lumped node avoids asserting an unverified ordering or isolation of every intermediate. Cancellation of ammonia in a net component balance does not mean ammonia is dispensable or prove that alanine nitrogen comes directly from cyanide. Pyruvate/cyanide colocation, concentrations, and the hydrolysis schedule remain implementation gaps; this route is not linked to dilute hydrothermal pyruvate without evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Native Fe0-driven reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"fresh exposed Fe0 surface; iron is also an explicitly consumed reactant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar in a sealed anoxic mineral-water pocket\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Benchmark window based on Kitadai et al.: 5 mM pyruvate, 0.5 M total ammonia, pH 9.6, and 48 h. Proposed accessible Fe0 inventory: 10-50 mM iron equivalents, with fresh surface exposed and corrosion measured. Proton entries are buffered acid equivalents, not a claim of acidic bulk pH. Dissolved Fe2+ is an immediate redox-accounting species; subsequent precipitation is represented separately.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine-like pyruvate adduct, followed by two-electron reduction. Per alanine, Fe0 -> Fe2+ supplies two electrons: pyruvic acid + ammonia + Fe0 + two proton equivalents -> alanine + water + Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) support native-metal reductive amination and show that material identity affects selectivity. Their near-complete conversion reported for pre-reduced FeS must not be assigned to pure Fe0. Iron is a finite reductant, not an indefinitely regenerative catalyst. The specified Fe0 stock and fresh surface are proposed operating requirements; abundant early-Earth Fe2+ does not by itself establish abundant native Fe0. Lactate competition is explicitly represented in rxn_015.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Stored-Fe0 reductive amination on pre-reduced FeS\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS_PERM: electrochemically pre-reduced FeS hosting accessible Fe0\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar in a sealed anoxic mineral-interface compartment\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported benchmark: pH 9.6, 0.5 M total ammonia, 5 mM pyruvate, and 48 h. Material preparation in the cited experiment used approximately -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days. Proposed application requires an independently assayed accessible Fe0 inventory of 10-50 mM equivalents; the exact necessary bulk FeS loading depends on that assay. No continuing electrical regeneration is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Interfacial imine formation and reduction draw on the finite Fe0 inventory created during pretreatment. Fe0 consumption, proton equivalents, water formation and Fe2+ release are explicitly included; no reducing equivalents are assigned to an unspecified catalyst annotation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The preparation and mild-temperature amination are directly supported by Kitadai et al. (2019). Their nearly complete pyruvate conversion applies to the prepared material and benchmark conditions, not arbitrary natural FeS. This is a distinct reagent preparation of the reductive-amination family, not a new independent mechanism. Natural production of an equivalent reduced surface and its accessible electron inventory remain unproven; the network treats it as a declared starting material rather than hiding an electrochemical energy source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni0/H2-catalyzed reductive amination at low ammonia concentration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni0 on silica-alumina, corresponding to the experimentally tested supported-nickel class\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 partial pressure; total pressure at least 5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"supported Ni0\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported screening framework: 30 mM pyruvate, pH 7-8, and 24 h; use the reported low-ammonia case of approximately 6 mM total ammonia. Proposed catalyst inventory for environmental-interface screening: 1-10 mM Ni equivalents, not a loading recovered from the supplied paper summary. A cool, pressurized, H2-fed rock cavity is required; this is not an ambient-pressure open pool. Maintain H2 supply and measure catalyst passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel activates H2 and hydrogenates an imine-like pyruvate/ammonia intermediate. Per alanine, pyruvic acid + ammonia + H2 -> alanine + water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) support Ni/H2 reductive amination at 22 degrees C and 5 bar H2, including useful conversion at approximately 6 mM ammonia. The approximately 17% low-ammonia result is normalized to ammonia, not to all pyruvate carbon. Supported laboratory nickel is not automatically equivalent to a naturally occurring mineral surface. The main environmental requirements are the external pyruvate stock, accessible Ni0, and sustained H2 fugacity. Competing lactate formation is represented in rxn_014.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"H2-fed Fe-Ni alloy reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"freshly exposed Fe-Ni alloy comparable to the tested Aletai meteorite, approximately 18 mol% Ni\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 partial pressure; total pressure at least 5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni alloy surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed application: 30 mM pyruvate, 30 mM total ammonia, pH 7-8, and 24 h in a cool pressurized water-filled cavity. The ammonia concentration and 1-10 g/L freshly exposed alloy loading are design assumptions, not claimed exact values from the Aletai experiment. Maintain dissolved H2 and quantify surface corrosion and passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ni-bearing alloy sites promote H2 activation and imine hydrogenation. The represented redox channel uses H2 as reductant; ancillary iron corrosion is not silently substituted for the hydrogen input.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) tested Aletai meteoritic metal and reported alanine together with substantial lactate, approximately 24% and 43% respectively in that experiment. These outcomes are not projected onto the proposed loading or ammonia stock. The alloy is a geochemically distinct catalyst implementation of the same hydrogenation mechanism as rxn_007. Its relevance depends on exposed Ni content, weathering history and H2 supply, not merely on the presence of a meteorite.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate oxime formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed mild downstream window: 5-20 mM pyruvate, approximately 1-2 molar equivalents of hydroxylamine, pH 4.5-6.5, and hours to days in a shaded anoxic pool. These concentrations, pH and residence times are application assumptions rather than a reproduced protocol from the supplied summary. Hydroxylamine must be delivered as a fresh local stock.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the pyruvate ketone to form an oxime and water. No external reductant is consumed during this condensation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support hydroxylamine/Fe0 conversion of ketoacids to amino acids via oxime chemistry. Resolving condensation from reduction makes the nitrogen source and electron demand explicit. Hydroxylamine supply and persistence are the principal prebiotic weaknesses; this network does not claim that an anoxic Fe2+-rich environment automatically generates it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"fresh Fe0 surface; iron is explicitly consumed\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar in an anoxic mineral-water compartment\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed continuation of rxn_009: pH 4.5-6.5, 5-20 mM oxime, and at least two accessible Fe0 equivalents per oxime, with additional iron allowed for nonproductive corrosion. Screen hours to days and assay dissolved and precipitated iron. The exact optimal operating window is not established by the supplied summary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of both the oxime C=N functionality and its N-O bond produces alanine and water. Per alanine, the closed balance is oxime + two Fe0 + four proton equivalents -> alanine + water + two Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime reduction with hydroxylamine and Fe0 is supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Four electrons, rather than the two required for ordinary imine reduction, are necessary; two Fe0 atoms supply them when the formal oxidation endpoint is Fe2+. This explicit accounting prevents the missing-reductant problem. The method does not establish enantioselection, and the specified local stock conditions still require experimental validation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally identified organic thiol promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-303 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-mercaptopropanoic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported timescale: approximately 60 days near ambient temperature. Proposed local stocks: 10-100 mM glyceraldehyde, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia, and 1-10 mM thiol; proposed pH 5-6. These loadings and pH bracket are not exact experimental values recovered from the supplied summary. Keep a continuously wet, shaded micro-pool and replenish unstable sugar only if quantified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped sugar dehydration, thiol-adduct chemistry, nitrogen incorporation and internal redox rearrangement. The closed net component transformation is glyceraldehyde + ammonia -> alanine + water; no additional external reducing agent is required by this net balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1985) reported alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium, with approximately 0.6% alanine after 60 days with thiol versus approximately 0.1% without it. Those results support low-flux formation, not efficient conversion under the proposed stock conditions. The availability of 3-mercaptopropanoic acid and survival of glyceraldehyde over long residence times are unresolved. Mechanistic intermediates are not promoted to separately validated nodes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally identified organic thiol promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-mercaptopropanoic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported temperature, pH and duration: 40 degrees C, pH 5.2, approximately 35 days. Proposed application stocks: 10-100 mM formaldehyde equivalents, 10-100 mM glycolaldehyde, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia, and 1-10 mM thiol; these loadings are design assumptions. Use a shaded, persistently wet pool pocket. Formaldehyde hydration and oligomerization are included in its analytical component inventory.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbon-carbon bond formation and subsequent thiol-assisted sugar/ammonia chemistry give alanine through a lumped internal-redox sequence. The balanced net transformation is formaldehyde + glycolaldehyde + ammonia -> alanine + water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1998) reported approximately 1.8% alanine on a formaldehyde basis after 35 days, with little or none without thiol. This is distinct from supplying glyceraldehyde directly, although downstream chemistry can overlap. The observed low yield and long incubation do not justify predicting efficient synthesis from arbitrary formose mixtures. External aldehyde and thiol stocks are explicitly required rather than assigned an unsupported upstream origin.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyridoxal-metal-mediated amino-group transfer from aspartate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"pyridoxal as an amino-group-transfer cofactor\",\n        \"Al(III) coordinated to organic ligands as a Lewis-acid co-catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyridoxal-Al(III) coordination system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed testable implementation: 5-20 mM pyruvate, 10-50 mM racemic aspartate, 0.1-1 mM pyridoxal, and 0.01-0.1 mM ligand-accessible Al(III), pH 5.5-7, hours to days. These are design windows, not exact published optimum conditions. Free Al3+ must not be assumed soluble at circumneutral pH; measure complexed versus precipitated aluminum. Pyridoxal and aspartate are declared external stocks.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aspartate transfers its amino group through pyridoxal/pyridoxamine intermediates to pyruvate. The net balanced reaction is aspartic acid + pyruvic acid -> oxaloacetic acid + alanine. No net H2 or metal oxidation is required.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Dherbassy et al. (2023) support cooperative metal-pyridoxal nonenzymatic transamination, with strong effects from Fe(III) and Al(III). Al(III) is chosen here to avoid presuming a large ferric reservoir in a reduced environment; its soluble coordination chemistry still requires verification. This is amino-nitrogen redistribution, not de novo nitrogen fixation. Pyridoxal's prebiotic origin and the stated operating window remain unresolved, and metal effects cannot be generalized to every cofactor system (Zabinski and Toney, 2001). Racemic donor and achiral catalyst inventories do not provide an L-alanine bias.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing Ni/H2 reduction of pyruvate to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni0-containing surface, including supported Ni0 or the Fe-Ni alloy implementation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 partial pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0-containing surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Occurs alongside rxn_007 or rxn_008 in the same compartment, not in an additional transport stage. Use their pyruvate stock, catalyst loading and 24 h benchmark. Low effective ammonia activity or insufficient imine formation favors direct carbonyl reduction. Quantify lactate together with both alanine enantiomers.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the pyruvate carbonyl gives lactate. The complete net reaction is pyruvic acid + H2 -> lactic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly observed lactate competition in Ni/H2 and meteoritic-metal experiments. This branch closes the organic and hydrogen balance without assigning an unknown mineral oxidation product. It prevents alanine formation from being mistaken for quantitative selectivity, especially for the alloy implementation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing Fe0-driven reduction of pyruvate to lactate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"accessible Fe0 surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use the same 5 mM pyruvate and finite accessible Fe0 inventory as rxn_005 or rxn_006. Evaluate pH 7-9.6 and effective ammonia activity; do not import selectivity percentages from green-rust experiments. Fe2+ may subsequently enter rxn_016 if the solution is supersaturated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two-electron reduction of the keto group competes with imine reduction. The explicit balance is pyruvic acid + Fe0 + two proton equivalents -> lactic acid + Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct ketoacid reduction is an established competitor in the metal/mineral experiments summarized by Kitadai et al. (2019) and Barge et al. (2019). The exact branching rate for a particular native-Fe0 surface is not specified by the supplied findings and must be measured. Unlike a proposed FeS/H2S-to-pyrite lactate branch, this reaction has an explicit electron donor and oxidation product and makes no claim about sulfur-mineral endpoints.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional precipitation of released ferrous iron\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Apply only to anoxic solutions supersaturated with respect to Fe(OH)2, most relevant to the alkaline pH 9.6 compartments. The component balance consumes two waters and releases two proton equivalents per Fe2+. Carbonate, sulfide and strong ligands can instead control ferrous speciation; this branch is conditional, not compulsory.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ferrous-ion hydrolysis and precipitation: Fe2+ + two waters -> Fe(OH)2 + two proton equivalents. This is a speciation reaction, not an additional source of reducing power.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ferrous hydroxide is chemically relevant in the supplied iron-mineral literature (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Barge et al., 2019). Its assignment as the actual spent phase of the Fe0 or FeS_PERM reactions is speculative and is not established by those citations or by Kitadai et al. (2019). The conditional branch is included to avoid implying that all released Fe2+ remains freely dissolved at alkaline pH; no pathway yield depends on this particular precipitate forming.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Declared acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia stocks form aminopropionitrile, followed by staged aqueous hydrolysis through alaninamide. L-alanine is produced together with D-alanine. Volatile-stock retention, transfer into the acid compartment and hydrolysis kinetics are explicit limitations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-dependent pyruvate/cyanide pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied pyruvate undergoes the experimentally supported CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia transformation. The reaction is represented as a closed lumped sequence, not as an asserted series of isolated intermediates. L-alanine occurs in the racemic product.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reductive-amination pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate and ammonia react using finite native Fe0 reducing equivalents. Fe2+ is explicitly produced. This pathway shares its reaction family with P4 but uses a different starting material preparation. Lactate formation competes through rxn_015.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Pre-reduced FeS stored-reductant pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate is aminated on a declared FeS_PERM stock containing accessible Fe0. Pretreatment and electron inventory are explicit boundary requirements. No natural electrochemical regeneration or indefinite catalytic turnover is assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Supported-Ni/H2 low-ammonia pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A cool pressurized mineral-water compartment supplies H2 for Ni-catalyzed reductive amination at approximately 6 mM total ammonia. This relaxes, but does not eliminate, the concentration problem. The alanine product is racemic and rxn_014 competes.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-Ni alloy/H2 pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"An exposed Ni-bearing alloy implements the same hydrogenation family using a mineral or meteoritic material analogue. It is distinguished from P5 by catalyst composition and surface history, not by an independent reaction mechanism. Substantial lactate formation is a documented limitation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxylamine-oxime reduction pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate and a declared hydroxylamine stock form the oxime, which consumes four electrons per alanine during Fe0 reduction. The explicit two-iron-per-alanine redox requirement distinguishes this route from ordinary reductive amination. Hydroxylamine availability is unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct glyceraldehyde-thiol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A glyceraldehyde/ammonium inventory undergoes slow thiol-assisted chemistry to alanine without cyanide or an external reductant in the net equation. The demonstrated low yield, long residence time and external thiol stock prevent treating it as a high-flux source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-thiol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Two smaller aldehyde stocks enter thiol-assisted carbon-carbon bond formation and amino-acid chemistry. This is a distinct feedstock entry from P8, with overlapping possible downstream intermediates. L-alanine is produced within a racemate at low reported yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Aspartate-to-pyruvate transamination pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A supplied aspartate pool transfers amino nitrogen to pyruvate through pyridoxal-metal chemistry. Oxaloacetate is the explicit coproduct. This redistributes existing amino nitrogen and requires an external organic cofactor; it is not claimed to solve pyridoxal synthesis or alanine homochirality.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_020\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_022\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten experimentally motivated pathways that produce L-alanine as a component of racemic alanine. L- and D-alanine are separate molecule nodes; no reaction is represented as enantioselective. The pathways comprise Strecker synthesis, CO2-dependent cyanide chemistry of pyruvate, four reductive-amination implementations, oxime reduction, two thiol-assisted sugar routes, and transamination. Catalyst variants are identified as implementations, not independent mechanisms. This is a complete stoichiometric network at declared feedstock boundaries, not a demonstrated autonomous environmental system. External acetaldehyde, cyanide, pyruvate, sugars, hydroxylamine and aspartate stocks are legitimate boundary conditions; their proposed concentrations and persistence limitations are specified below. In particular, millimolar pyruvate is not claimed to follow from the micromolar CO2/H2 experiments of Preiner et al. (2020), and no unsupported hydrothermal-to-surface concentration step is inserted. All settings are anoxic. Surface reactions occur in shaded or optically screened water pockets unless stated otherwise. Biochemical denotes enzyme-free prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces, including cool pressurized cavities, not living metabolism. Neutral formulas represent analytical component inventories; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Explicit H+, Fe0 and Fe2+ nodes close the metal-reduction balances. Ferrous hydroxide precipitation is a conditional speciation branch, not an asserted measured spent-mineral endpoint. Reported experimental conditions are distinguished from proposed environmental implementation parameters. No unsupported enantioenrichment reaction is inserted: CPL studies show small, irradiation-dependent excesses but do not establish an available terrestrial L-selecting source or a closed photoproduct inventory here (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). Enantiopure or isolated L-alanine therefore remains outside this network's demonstrated scope. Null environment_formed values indicate external stocks or species formed in multiple environments; reaction-level labels resolve the latter. Terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role because it provides no coproduct category.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydrogen(1+)", "common_name": "proton equivalent", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "zero-valent iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alpha-aminopropionitrile, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanamide", "common_name": "alaninamide, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H8N2O/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H2,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "NH3O", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid oxime, E/Z unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c1-2-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "glyceraldehyde, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h1,3,5-6H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C4H7NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "aspartic acid, supplied as a racemic inventory", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NO4/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h2H,1,5H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "oxaloacetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "FeH2O2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferrous hydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed local-stock implementation: 10-50 mM acetaldehyde, 10-100 mM total HCN/CN-, and 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia; pH 8.5-9.5; hours to several days. Use a shaded, poorly ventilated water pocket with replenished volatile feedstocks. These concentrations are boundary assumptions, not demonstrated concentrations in an early-Earth pool."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to acetaldehyde, followed by dehydration and cyanide addition to the resulting imine or iminium species. The net reaction produces aminonitrile and water. Addition to either face gives an achiral-environment racemic inventory.", "reasoning": "This is established Strecker chemistry, and atmospheric-discharge production of its precursors is consistent with Miller (1953). The network starts from the precursors rather than assigning a fictitious balanced equation to an entire discharge mixture. Cyanohydrin formation competes, especially when effective ammonia activity is low. Volatile-feedstock retention and the specified ammonia inventory are environmental limitations, not assumed consequences of a global reducing atmosphere."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Aminonitrile hydration to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid providing catalytic hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed hydrolysis compartment: pH 1-2, approximately 1-20 mM aminonitrile after dilution, and residence for hours to days. A short drainage path into an adjacent volcanic-acid water pocket provides the necessary pH change. Acid capacity must exceed the carried-over ammonia alkalinity; quantitative transfer recovery is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed water addition to the nitrile gives the corresponding primary amide. One water molecule is incorporated per aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydrolysis through an amide is the established Strecker workup sequence (Strecker, 1850). The stated natural compartment and operating window are proposed implementations, not a reproduced geological experiment. Hydrolysis is a kinetic bottleneck at milder temperatures, and loss of aminonitrile before hydrolysis must be measured."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Alaninamide hydrolysis to racemic alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["aqueous acid providing catalytic hydronium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue in the acid hydrolysis compartment at pH 1-2 for hours to days, monitoring conversion rather than presuming complete hydrolysis. Ammonia is present predominantly as ammonium under these conditions. Cool the product solution after hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the carboxamide releases ammonia and produces alanine. The equation balances by processing equal amounts of the two alaninamide enantiomers to the separately represented L- and D-alanine products.", "reasoning": "This closes the established Strecker sequence (Strecker, 1850). It produces actual L-alanine, but only alongside D-alanine. Moderate hydrolysis temperatures avoid invoking prolonged black-smoker exposure, which would increase amino-acid destruction risks (Miller and Bada, 1988; Aubrey et al., 2009)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_002", "mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_002", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K for the proposed formation stage; hydrolysis may require a subsequent 343-370 K stage", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar in a CO2-retaining water pocket", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed stocks: 5-30 mM pyruvate, 10-100 mM total cyanide, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia, and 10-100 mM dissolved inorganic carbon; initially pH 8-9.5. Follow conversion over days. If hydantoin or carbamoyl intermediates accumulate, a separate aqueous hydrolysis stage is required. These implementation concentrations and staging are not claimed to reproduce the exact published protocol."}, "mechanism": "Lumped Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry through hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates, with decarboxylation and hydrolysis. The net component balance per alanine is pyruvic acid + HCN + water -> alanine + CO2. Ammonia and additional CO2 participate in intermediate formation and are included on both sides as turnover inventories. This component balance does not establish carbon or nitrogen atom mapping.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) experimentally support CO2-dependent conversion of alpha-ketoacids using cyanide and ammonia. CO2 is mechanistically necessary and is not replaced by an arbitrary mineral catalyst. A lumped node avoids asserting an unverified ordering or isolation of every intermediate. Cancellation of ammonia in a net component balance does not mean ammonia is dispensable or prove that alanine nitrogen comes directly from cyanide. Pyruvate/cyanide colocation, concentrations, and the hydrolysis schedule remain implementation gaps; this route is not linked to dilute hydrothermal pyruvate without evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Native Fe0-driven reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001", "mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["fresh exposed Fe0 surface; iron is also an explicitly consumed reactant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar in a sealed anoxic mineral-water pocket", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Benchmark window based on Kitadai et al.: 5 mM pyruvate, 0.5 M total ammonia, pH 9.6, and 48 h. Proposed accessible Fe0 inventory: 10-50 mM iron equivalents, with fresh surface exposed and corrosion measured. Proton entries are buffered acid equivalents, not a claim of acidic bulk pH. Dissolved Fe2+ is an immediate redox-accounting species; subsequent precipitation is represented separately."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine-like pyruvate adduct, followed by two-electron reduction. Per alanine, Fe0 -> Fe2+ supplies two electrons: pyruvic acid + ammonia + Fe0 + two proton equivalents -> alanine + water + Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) support native-metal reductive amination and show that material identity affects selectivity. Their near-complete conversion reported for pre-reduced FeS must not be assigned to pure Fe0. Iron is a finite reductant, not an indefinitely regenerative catalyst. The specified Fe0 stock and fresh surface are proposed operating requirements; abundant early-Earth Fe2+ does not by itself establish abundant native Fe0. Lactate competition is explicitly represented in rxn_015."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Stored-Fe0 reductive amination on pre-reduced FeS", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001", "mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["FeS_PERM: electrochemically pre-reduced FeS hosting accessible Fe0"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar in a sealed anoxic mineral-interface compartment", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported benchmark: pH 9.6, 0.5 M total ammonia, 5 mM pyruvate, and 48 h. Material preparation in the cited experiment used approximately -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days. Proposed application requires an independently assayed accessible Fe0 inventory of 10-50 mM equivalents; the exact necessary bulk FeS loading depends on that assay. No continuing electrical regeneration is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Interfacial imine formation and reduction draw on the finite Fe0 inventory created during pretreatment. Fe0 consumption, proton equivalents, water formation and Fe2+ release are explicitly included; no reducing equivalents are assigned to an unspecified catalyst annotation.", "reasoning": "The preparation and mild-temperature amination are directly supported by Kitadai et al. (2019). Their nearly complete pyruvate conversion applies to the prepared material and benchmark conditions, not arbitrary natural FeS. This is a distinct reagent preparation of the reductive-amination family, not a new independent mechanism. Natural production of an equivalent reduced surface and its accessible electron inventory remain unproven; the network treats it as a declared starting material rather than hiding an electrochemical energy source."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ni0/H2-catalyzed reductive amination at low ammonia concentration", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni0 on silica-alumina, corresponding to the experimentally tested supported-nickel class"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 partial pressure; total pressure at least 5 bar", "catalyst": "supported Ni0", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported screening framework: 30 mM pyruvate, pH 7-8, and 24 h; use the reported low-ammonia case of approximately 6 mM total ammonia. Proposed catalyst inventory for environmental-interface screening: 1-10 mM Ni equivalents, not a loading recovered from the supplied paper summary. A cool, pressurized, H2-fed rock cavity is required; this is not an ambient-pressure open pool. Maintain H2 supply and measure catalyst passivation."}, "mechanism": "Nickel activates H2 and hydrogenates an imine-like pyruvate/ammonia intermediate. Per alanine, pyruvic acid + ammonia + H2 -> alanine + water.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) support Ni/H2 reductive amination at 22 degrees C and 5 bar H2, including useful conversion at approximately 6 mM ammonia. The approximately 17% low-ammonia result is normalized to ammonia, not to all pyruvate carbon. Supported laboratory nickel is not automatically equivalent to a naturally occurring mineral surface. The main environmental requirements are the external pyruvate stock, accessible Ni0, and sustained H2 fugacity. Competing lactate formation is represented in rxn_014."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "H2-fed Fe-Ni alloy reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["freshly exposed Fe-Ni alloy comparable to the tested Aletai meteorite, approximately 18 mol% Ni"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 partial pressure; total pressure at least 5 bar", "catalyst": "Fe-Ni alloy surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed application: 30 mM pyruvate, 30 mM total ammonia, pH 7-8, and 24 h in a cool pressurized water-filled cavity. The ammonia concentration and 1-10 g/L freshly exposed alloy loading are design assumptions, not claimed exact values from the Aletai experiment. Maintain dissolved H2 and quantify surface corrosion and passivation."}, "mechanism": "Ni-bearing alloy sites promote H2 activation and imine hydrogenation. The represented redox channel uses H2 as reductant; ancillary iron corrosion is not silently substituted for the hydrogen input.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) tested Aletai meteoritic metal and reported alanine together with substantial lactate, approximately 24% and 43% respectively in that experiment. These outcomes are not projected onto the proposed loading or ammonia stock. The alloy is a geochemically distinct catalyst implementation of the same hydrogenation mechanism as rxn_007. Its relevance depends on exposed Ni content, weathering history and H2 supply, not merely on the presence of a meteorite."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Pyruvate oxime formation", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed mild downstream window: 5-20 mM pyruvate, approximately 1-2 molar equivalents of hydroxylamine, pH 4.5-6.5, and hours to days in a shaded anoxic pool. These concentrations, pH and residence times are application assumptions rather than a reproduced protocol from the supplied summary. Hydroxylamine must be delivered as a fresh local stock."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with the pyruvate ketone to form an oxime and water. No external reductant is consumed during this condensation.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support hydroxylamine/Fe0 conversion of ketoacids to amino acids via oxime chemistry. Resolving condensation from reduction makes the nitrogen source and electron demand explicit. Hydroxylamine supply and persistence are the principal prebiotic weaknesses; this network does not claim that an anoxic Fe2+-rich environment automatically generates it."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001", "mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["fresh Fe0 surface; iron is explicitly consumed"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar in an anoxic mineral-water compartment", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed continuation of rxn_009: pH 4.5-6.5, 5-20 mM oxime, and at least two accessible Fe0 equivalents per oxime, with additional iron allowed for nonproductive corrosion. Screen hours to days and assay dissolved and precipitated iron. The exact optimal operating window is not established by the supplied summary."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of both the oxime C=N functionality and its N-O bond produces alanine and water. Per alanine, the closed balance is oxime + two Fe0 + four proton equivalents -> alanine + water + two Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Oxime reduction with hydroxylamine and Fe0 is supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Four electrons, rather than the two required for ordinary imine reduction, are necessary; two Fe0 atoms supply them when the formal oxidation endpoint is Fe2+. This explicit accounting prevents the missing-reductant problem. The method does not establish enantioselection, and the specified local stock conditions still require experimental validation."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally identified organic thiol promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-303 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "3-mercaptopropanoic acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported timescale: approximately 60 days near ambient temperature. Proposed local stocks: 10-100 mM glyceraldehyde, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia, and 1-10 mM thiol; proposed pH 5-6. These loadings and pH bracket are not exact experimental values recovered from the supplied summary. Keep a continuously wet, shaded micro-pool and replenish unstable sugar only if quantified."}, "mechanism": "Lumped sugar dehydration, thiol-adduct chemistry, nitrogen incorporation and internal redox rearrangement. The closed net component transformation is glyceraldehyde + ammonia -> alanine + water; no additional external reducing agent is required by this net balance.", "reasoning": "Weber (1985) reported alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium, with approximately 0.6% alanine after 60 days with thiol versus approximately 0.1% without it. Those results support low-flux formation, not efficient conversion under the proposed stock conditions. The availability of 3-mercaptopropanoic acid and survival of glyceraldehyde over long residence times are unresolved. Mechanistic intermediates are not promoted to separately validated nodes."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-mercaptopropanoic acid as the experimentally identified organic thiol promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "313 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "3-mercaptopropanoic acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported temperature, pH and duration: 40 degrees C, pH 5.2, approximately 35 days. Proposed application stocks: 10-100 mM formaldehyde equivalents, 10-100 mM glycolaldehyde, 0.1-0.5 M total ammonia, and 1-10 mM thiol; these loadings are design assumptions. Use a shaded, persistently wet pool pocket. Formaldehyde hydration and oligomerization are included in its analytical component inventory."}, "mechanism": "Carbon-carbon bond formation and subsequent thiol-assisted sugar/ammonia chemistry give alanine through a lumped internal-redox sequence. The balanced net transformation is formaldehyde + glycolaldehyde + ammonia -> alanine + water.", "reasoning": "Weber (1998) reported approximately 1.8% alanine on a formaldehyde basis after 35 days, with little or none without thiol. This is distinct from supplying glyceraldehyde directly, although downstream chemistry can overlap. The observed low yield and long incubation do not justify predicting efficient synthesis from arbitrary formose mixtures. External aldehyde and thiol stocks are explicitly required rather than assigned an unsupported upstream origin."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Pyridoxal-metal-mediated amino-group transfer from aspartate", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_021", "mol_022"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["pyridoxal as an amino-group-transfer cofactor", "Al(III) coordinated to organic ligands as a Lewis-acid co-catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "pyridoxal-Al(III) coordination system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed testable implementation: 5-20 mM pyruvate, 10-50 mM racemic aspartate, 0.1-1 mM pyridoxal, and 0.01-0.1 mM ligand-accessible Al(III), pH 5.5-7, hours to days. These are design windows, not exact published optimum conditions. Free Al3+ must not be assumed soluble at circumneutral pH; measure complexed versus precipitated aluminum. Pyridoxal and aspartate are declared external stocks."}, "mechanism": "Aspartate transfers its amino group through pyridoxal/pyridoxamine intermediates to pyruvate. The net balanced reaction is aspartic acid + pyruvic acid -> oxaloacetic acid + alanine. No net H2 or metal oxidation is required.", "reasoning": "Dherbassy et al. (2023) support cooperative metal-pyridoxal nonenzymatic transamination, with strong effects from Fe(III) and Al(III). Al(III) is chosen here to avoid presuming a large ferric reservoir in a reduced environment; its soluble coordination chemistry still requires verification. This is amino-nitrogen redistribution, not de novo nitrogen fixation. Pyridoxal's prebiotic origin and the stated operating window remain unresolved, and metal effects cannot be generalized to every cofactor system (Zabinski and Toney, 2001). Racemic donor and achiral catalyst inventories do not provide an L-alanine bias."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Competing Ni/H2 reduction of pyruvate to lactate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni0-containing surface, including supported Ni0 or the Fe-Ni alloy implementation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 partial pressure", "catalyst": "Ni0-containing surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Occurs alongside rxn_007 or rxn_008 in the same compartment, not in an additional transport stage. Use their pyruvate stock, catalyst loading and 24 h benchmark. Low effective ammonia activity or insufficient imine formation favors direct carbonyl reduction. Quantify lactate together with both alanine enantiomers."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the pyruvate carbonyl gives lactate. The complete net reaction is pyruvic acid + H2 -> lactic acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly observed lactate competition in Ni/H2 and meteoritic-metal experiments. This branch closes the organic and hydrogen balance without assigning an unknown mineral oxidation product. It prevents alanine formation from being mistaken for quantitative selectivity, especially for the alloy implementation."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Competing Fe0-driven reduction of pyruvate to lactate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["accessible Fe0 surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Use the same 5 mM pyruvate and finite accessible Fe0 inventory as rxn_005 or rxn_006. Evaluate pH 7-9.6 and effective ammonia activity; do not import selectivity percentages from green-rust experiments. Fe2+ may subsequently enter rxn_016 if the solution is supersaturated."}, "mechanism": "Two-electron reduction of the keto group competes with imine reduction. The explicit balance is pyruvic acid + Fe0 + two proton equivalents -> lactic acid + Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Direct ketoacid reduction is an established competitor in the metal/mineral experiments summarized by Kitadai et al. (2019) and Barge et al. (2019). The exact branching rate for a particular native-Fe0 surface is not specified by the supplied findings and must be measured. Unlike a proposed FeS/H2S-to-pyrite lactate branch, this reaction has an explicit electron donor and oxidation product and makes no claim about sulfur-mineral endpoints."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Conditional precipitation of released ferrous iron", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_007"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "296-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Apply only to anoxic solutions supersaturated with respect to Fe(OH)2, most relevant to the alkaline pH 9.6 compartments. The component balance consumes two waters and releases two proton equivalents per Fe2+. Carbonate, sulfide and strong ligands can instead control ferrous speciation; this branch is conditional, not compulsory."}, "mechanism": "Ferrous-ion hydrolysis and precipitation: Fe2+ + two waters -> Fe(OH)2 + two proton equivalents. This is a speciation reaction, not an additional source of reducing power.", "reasoning": "Ferrous hydroxide is chemically relevant in the supplied iron-mineral literature (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Barge et al., 2019). Its assignment as the actual spent phase of the Fe0 or FeS_PERM reactions is speculative and is not established by those citations or by Kitadai et al. (2019). The conditional branch is included to avoid implying that all released Fe2+ remains freely dissolved at alkaline pH; no pathway yield depends on this particular precipitate forming."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acetaldehyde Strecker pathway", "description": "Declared acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia stocks form aminopropionitrile, followed by staged aqueous hydrolysis through alaninamide. L-alanine is produced together with D-alanine. Volatile-stock retention, transfer into the acid compartment and hydrolysis kinetics are explicit limitations.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "CO2-dependent pyruvate/cyanide pathway", "description": "Externally supplied pyruvate undergoes the experimentally supported CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia transformation. The reaction is represented as a closed lumped sequence, not as an asserted series of isolated intermediates. L-alanine occurs in the racemic product.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Native-iron reductive-amination pathway", "description": "Pyruvate and ammonia react using finite native Fe0 reducing equivalents. Fe2+ is explicitly produced. This pathway shares its reaction family with P4 but uses a different starting material preparation. Lactate formation competes through rxn_015.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Pre-reduced FeS stored-reductant pathway", "description": "Pyruvate is aminated on a declared FeS_PERM stock containing accessible Fe0. Pretreatment and electron inventory are explicit boundary requirements. No natural electrochemical regeneration or indefinite catalytic turnover is assumed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Supported-Ni/H2 low-ammonia pathway", "description": "A cool pressurized mineral-water compartment supplies H2 for Ni-catalyzed reductive amination at approximately 6 mM total ammonia. This relaxes, but does not eliminate, the concentration problem. The alanine product is racemic and rxn_014 competes.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Fe-Ni alloy/H2 pathway", "description": "An exposed Ni-bearing alloy implements the same hydrogenation family using a mineral or meteoritic material analogue. It is distinguished from P5 by catalyst composition and surface history, not by an independent reaction mechanism. Substantial lactate formation is a documented limitation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Hydroxylamine-oxime reduction pathway", "description": "Pyruvate and a declared hydroxylamine stock form the oxime, which consumes four electrons per alanine during Fe0 reduction. The explicit two-iron-per-alanine redox requirement distinguishes this route from ordinary reductive amination. Hydroxylamine availability is unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Direct glyceraldehyde-thiol pathway", "description": "A glyceraldehyde/ammonium inventory undergoes slow thiol-assisted chemistry to alanine without cyanide or an external reductant in the net equation. The demonstrated low yield, long residence time and external thiol stock prevent treating it as a high-flux source.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-thiol pathway", "description": "Two smaller aldehyde stocks enter thiol-assisted carbon-carbon bond formation and amino-acid chemistry. This is a distinct feedstock entry from P8, with overlapping possible downstream intermediates. L-alanine is produced within a racemate at low reported yield.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Aspartate-to-pyruvate transamination pathway", "description": "A supplied aspartate pool transfers amino nitrogen to pyruvate through pyridoxal-metal chemistry. Oxaloacetate is the explicit coproduct. This redistributes existing amino nitrogen and requires an external organic cofactor; it is not claimed to solve pyridoxal synthesis or alanine homochirality.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_020", "mol_021", "mol_022"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_021", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_022", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_020", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_015"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_016"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_002", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"]}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 463555.6745529175, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T03:46:29.867672", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct ten experimentally motivated pathways that produce L-alanine as a component of racemic alanine. L- and D-alanine are separate molecule nodes; no reaction is represented as enantioselective. The pathways comprise Strecker synthesis, CO2-dependent cyanide chemistry of pyruvate, four reductive-amination implementations, oxime reduction, two thiol-assisted sugar routes, and transamination. Catalyst variants are identified as implementations, not independent mechanisms. This is a complete stoichiometric network at declared feedstock boundaries, not a demonstrated autonomous environmental system. External acetaldehyde, cyanide, pyruvate, sugars, hydroxylamine and aspartate stocks are legitimate boundary conditions; their proposed concentrations and persistence limitations are specified below. In particular, millimolar pyruvate is not claimed to follow from the micromolar CO2/H2 experiments of Preiner et al. (2020), and no unsupported hydrothermal-to-surface concentration step is inserted. All settings are anoxic. Surface reactions occur in shaded or optically screened water pockets unless stated otherwise. Biochemical denotes enzyme-free prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces, including cool pressurized cavities, not living metabolism. Neutral formulas represent analytical component inventories; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Explicit H+, Fe0 and Fe2+ nodes close the metal-reduction balances. Ferrous hydroxide precipitation is a conditional speciation branch, not an asserted measured spent-mineral endpoint. Reported experimental conditions are distinguished from proposed environmental implementation parameters. No unsupported enantioenrichment reaction is inserted: CPL studies show small, irradiation-dependent excesses but do not establish an available terrestrial L-selecting source or a closed photoproduct inventory here (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). Enantiopure or isolated L-alanine therefore remains outside this network's demonstrated scope. Null environment_formed values indicate external stocks or species formed in multiple environments; reaction-level labels resolve the latter. Terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role because it provides no coproduct category.\n\nMOLECULES (23 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (water) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_003: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_007: H+ (proton equivalent) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: Fe (zero-valent iron) [starting]\n  - mol_009: Fe+2 (ferrous ion) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H6N2 (alpha-aminopropionitrile, stereochemistry unspecified) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C3H8N2O (alaninamide, stereochemistry unspecified) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: NH3O (hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_013: C3H5NO3 (pyruvic acid oxime, E/Z unspecified) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_015: C3H6O3 (glyceraldehyde, stereochemistry unspecified) [starting]\n  - mol_016: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_017: C2H4O2 (glycolaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_018: C4H7NO4 (aspartic acid, supplied as a racemic inventory) [starting]\n  - mol_019: C4H4O5 (oxaloacetic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C3H6O3 (lactic acid, stereochemistry unspecified) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_022: C3H7NO2 (D-alanine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_023: FeH2O2 (ferrous hydroxide) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (16 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_004, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_010, mol_001 (Acetaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_010, mol_001 -> mol_011 (Aminonitrile hydration to alaninamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_011, mol_001 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_002 (Alaninamide hydrolysis to racemic alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_005, mol_003, mol_002, mol_014, mol_001 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_002, mol_014 (CO2-dependent cyanide/ammonia conversion of pyruvate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_005, mol_002, mol_008, mol_007 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001, mol_009 (Native Fe0-driven reductive amination of pyruvate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_006: mol_005, mol_002, mol_008, mol_007 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001, mol_009 (Stored-Fe0 reductive amination on pre-reduced FeS) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_007: mol_005, mol_002, mol_006 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001 (Ni0/H2-catalyzed reductive amination at low ammonia concentration) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_008: mol_005, mol_002, mol_006 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001 (H2-fed Fe-Ni alloy reductive amination) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_009: mol_005, mol_012 -> mol_013, mol_001 (Pyruvate oxime formation) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_013, mol_008, mol_007 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001, mol_009 (Fe0 reduction of pyruvate oxime) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_015, mol_002 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001 (Thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_016, mol_017, mol_002 -> mol_021, mol_022, mol_001 (Thiol-assisted alanine formation from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_018, mol_005 -> mol_019, mol_021, mol_022 (Pyridoxal-metal-mediated amino-group transfer from aspartate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_014: mol_005, mol_006 -> mol_020 (Competing Ni/H2 reduction of pyruvate to lactate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_015: mol_005, mol_008, mol_007 -> mol_020, mol_009 (Competing Fe0-driven reduction of pyruvate to lactate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_016: mol_009, mol_001 -> mol_023, mol_007 (Conditional precipitation of released ferrous iron) [Biochemical]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Acetaldehyde Strecker pathway (3 steps)\n  - P2: CO2-dependent pyruvate/cyanide pathway (1 steps)\n  - P3: Native-iron reductive-amination pathway (1 steps)\n  - P4: Pre-reduced FeS stored-reductant pathway (1 steps)\n  - P5: Supported-Ni/H2 low-ammonia pathway (1 steps)\n  - P6: Fe-Ni alloy/H2 pathway (1 steps)\n  - P7: Hydroxylamine-oxime reduction pathway (2 steps)\n  - P8: Direct glyceraldehyde-thiol pathway (1 steps)\n  - P9: Formaldehyde-glycolaldehyde-thiol pathway (1 steps)\n  - P10: Aspartate-to-pyruvate transamination pathway (1 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 16 reactions | 4 balanced as written | 12 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_003 balanceable: 2 C3H8N2O + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_004 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 HCN + NH3 + 3 CO2 + 2 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + NH3 + 3 CO2\n  - rxn_005 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O + 2 Fe+2\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O + 2 Fe+2\n  - rxn_007 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_010 balanceable: 2 C3H5NO3 + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O + 4 Fe+2\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: 2 C3H6O3 + 2 NH3 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: 2 CH2O + 2 C2H4O2 + 2 NH3 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_013 balanceable: 2 C4H7NO4 + 2 C3H4O3 -> 2 C4H4O5 + C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2\n  - rxn_015 balanceable: C3H4O3 + Fe + 2 H+ -> C3H6O3 + Fe+2\n  - rxn_016 balanceable: Fe+2 + 2 H2O -> FeH2O2 + 2 H+\nFormula/structure inconsistencies:\n  - mol_014: InChI could not be parsed by RDKit (RDKit could not parse InChI)\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Alanine: Routes, Evidence, Conditions and Contested Points\n\nRacemic alanine is one of the easiest proteinogenic amino acids to make abiotically. It has been synthesized from several unrelated feedstocks: HCN with acetaldehyde and NH3 (Strecker), pyruvate with NH3 and a reductant (reductive amination), pyruvate with cyanide, NH3 and CO2 (Bucherer\u2013Bergs), and aldehyde/sugar chemistry with NH3. It also arrives in meteorites. No experiment, however, makes the **L** enantiomer selectively. The real bottleneck for \"L-alanine\" is enantioenrichment, plus the problem of joining each route's feedstock synthesis to its amination step under one set of conditions. Which route is \"best\" depends on which framework you accept, and each is contested on yield, concentration, or geochemical plausibility.\n\n## TL;DR\n- **Two families of routes dominate, and both work in the lab.** (1) Aldehyde/cyanide chemistry: Strecker from acetaldehyde + HCN + NH3, starting with Miller's 1953 spark discharge, and today Sutherland's cyanosulfidic network. (2) Keto-acid chemistry: reductive amination of pyruvate on FeS, green rust, electro-reduced FeS, Fe0 or Ni0/H2. Pyruvate amination gives the highest single-step yields (~50\u201370%, \"nearly complete\" in some runs), but needs high NH3, alkaline pH and a pyruvate source that is itself low-yield (\u226410 \u00b5M from CO2 + H2).\n- **Every demonstrated route gives racemic alanine.** The only claimed natural L-excess, ~33% L-alanine in Murchison, was reported by Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature*) on the basis of \"nearly equal, extraterrestrial \u03b415N values measured for each alanine enantiomer\". It was disputed (Pizzarello & Cronin 1998). Later Murchison and Bennu analyses found alanine racemic. Asymmetric photolysis by circularly polarized light gives only percent-level excesses. Getting from racemic to L is unsolved for every framework.\n- **Environment is unresolved.** Alkaline hydrothermal/serpentinizing settings best fit the keto-acid routes, but face the \"hot-vent decomposition\" critique (Miller & Bada 1988). Surface UV/sulfidic or atmospheric-discharge settings fit the HCN routes, but depend on reducing gases or locally concentrated HCN/sulfide. For network design, treat pyruvate\u21c4alanine as a robust, multi-catalyst node. Treat the upstream C3 source and the chirality step as the open problems.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Route summary (evidence-weighted)**\n\n| Route | Feedstocks | Key intermediate(s) | Representative conditions | Best reported alanine outcome | Main critique |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark-discharge / Strecker | CH4, NH3, H2, H2O (or CO2/N2 with buffering) | HCN, acetaldehyde, \u03b1-aminopropionitrile | Electric discharge, then aqueous Strecker | Glycine 2.1% of CH4-carbon; alanine \u22480.54 mol per mol glycine (Miller) | Needs reducing atmosphere; neutral CO2/N2 atmospheres give much less unless nitrite is quenched |\n| Cyanosulfidic (Sutherland) | HCN, H2S/HS\u207b, Cu(I), UV, phosphate | Aldehydes from reductive HCN homologation; aminonitriles | 254 nm UV, sulfidic water | 12 amino acids accessed via Strecker | Local high HCN/sulfide; UV wavelength; stepwise \"one-pot\" claims |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs from \u03b1-ketoacids | Pyruvate, cyanide, NH3, CO2 | Hydantoin / N-carbamoyl amino acid | Aqueous, mild; CO2 essential | \u03b1-Amino acids from \u03b1-ketoacids | Still needs cyanide plus a pyruvate source |\n| Reductive amination on Fe minerals | Pyruvate, NH3, Fe(II)/Fe(III) minerals | Iminopyruvate | FeS/Fe(OH)2 at 50\u201375 \u00b0C; green rust 50\u201370 \u00b0C, alkaline | Up to ~50% (FeS); ~70% (green rust, pH 10, 70 \u00b0C, 72 h) | Requires 0.1\u20131.5 M NH3; lactate competes; stoichiometric mineral reductant |\n| Electro-reduced FeS / Fe0 | Pyruvate, NH3, H2 / electrochemical potential | Iminopyruvate | pH 9.6, 0.5 M NH3, 23\u201325 \u00b0C, 48 h | \"Nearly complete\" conversion | Requires a sustained negative potential in vents |\n| Ni0 / H2 | Pyruvate, NH3, H2 | Iminopyruvate | 22 \u00b0C, pH 7\u20138, 5 bar H2 | 18\u201340% (Ni or Pd/C); 17% at only 6 mM NH3 (Ni) | Native Ni availability; high H2 pressure |\n| Hydroxylamine + Fe0 | Pyruvate, NH2OH, Fe0 | Pyruvate oxime | Aqueous, mild | Alanine among 4 amino acids | NH2OH prebiotic availability |\n| Transamination | Pyruvate + another amino acid, pyridoxal, metal ions | Pyridoxamine / aldimine | Aqueous; Fe(III)/Al(III) cooperativity | Aspartate\u2192alanine works; pyridoxal raises Ni/H2 alanine from 7.6% to 34% | Pyridoxal's own prebiotic origin |\n| Thiol-catalyzed sugar route (Weber) | Glyceraldehyde, or HCHO + glycolaldehyde, NH3, thiol | Methylglyoxal-type dehydration products, hemithioacetal, imine | 40 \u00b0C, pH 5.2, 35 days | 1.8% (HCHO basis); 0.6% after 60 days (glyceraldehyde) | Low yield, slow; mercaptopropionic acid as catalyst |\n| Formose-type parent-body chemistry | HCHO, glycolaldehyde, acetaldehyde, NH3 | Aldimines / aminonitrile-free routes | 60\u2013250 \u00b0C aqueous | \u03b1-, \u03b2-, \u03b3-amino acids including alanine | Asteroidal rather than terrestrial; racemic |\n| Delivery / interstellar ice | CH3OH, NH3, H2O, CO ices + UV; parent-body chemistry | Radicals; Strecker precursors | Cryogenic UV; meteorite parent bodies | Racemic alanine (Bennu, ice residues) | Delivered amounts; survival on impact |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Strecker chemistry (HCN + acetaldehyde + NH3)\n\n**Chemistry.** CH3CHO + NH3 \u2192 imine. Adding HCN gives \u03b1-aminopropionitrile, and hydrolysis (via the amide) gives alanine. Strecker made alanine this way from acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN in 1850, the first laboratory amino acid synthesis.\\[1\\] In Strecker chemistry the imine/aminonitrile and the cyanohydrin are in equilibrium until hydrolysis begins. Where NH3 is low or pH is low, aminonitriles partly convert to cyanohydrins, which hydrolyze to lactate rather than alanine (as noted in the 2021 *Science Advances* paper \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\").\\[2\\]\n\n**Miller\u2013Urey evidence.** Miller (1953, *Science*) sparked CH4/NH3/H2/H2O and obtained glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine and \u03b1-aminobutyric acid.\\[3\\] Miller attributed this to Strecker chemistry: HCN and aldehydes formed in the gas phase reacted in the water.\\[3\\] Bada's retrospective (M\u00e8tode, citing Miller & Urey 1959) gives these yields relative to glycine = 1: alanine \u2248 0.54 mol, \u03b2-alanine \u2248 0.24 mol. From 710 mg of methane carbon, 4.8 mg glycine formed.\\[3\\]\\[4\\] Johnson et al. (2008, *Science*) give glycine at 2.1% of methane carbon for the classic apparatus, versus 1.8% and 0.4% for the other two.\\[5\\] Miller estimated that 50\u201360% of the methane carbon became organic compounds, mostly polymeric material.\\[3\\]\n\n**Variants.**\n- **H2S-rich atmosphere.** Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS*) reanalyzed a 1958 Miller experiment run with CH4, NH3, H2S and CO2. It produced 23 amino acids and 4 amines, with high relative abundances of alkyl amino acids including alanine.\\[6\\]\\[7\\]\n- **Neutral atmosphere.** Cleaves et al. (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) showed that CO2/N2 atmospheres give far more amino acids than previously thought, including alanine, once nitrite/nitrate (which destroy amino acids during workup) are controlled and the solution is buffered. The alanine was racemic within experimental error.\\[8\\]\\[9\\]\n\n**Cyanosulfidic protometabolism (Sutherland school).** Patel et al. (2015, *Nature Chemistry*) showed that UV-driven (254 nm), HS\u207b/Cu-mediated reductive homologation of HCN produces precursors of ribonucleotides, lipids and amino acids. In that network 12 amino acids are reached by Strecker synthesis.\\[2\\]\\[10\\]\\[11\\] Alanine is fairly photostable in these systems. At high pH it photolyzes slowly (~6% products after 2.5 h), and NaSH suppresses its photodegradation. Slow oxidation of alanine to pyruvate was also observed (2021 *Sci. Adv.*).\\[2\\] This links the cyanide and keto-acid worlds.\n\n**Isotopic fingerprint.** Murchison alanine has a \u00b9\u00b3C-rich \u03b1-carbon, consistent with Strecker synthesis from acetaldehyde with a \u00b9\u00b3C-rich carbonyl carbon (Chimiak et al. 2020). Experiments then measured Strecker-specific isotope effects for alanine, e.g. a 55.1\u2030 nitrogen equilibrium isotope effect between NH3 and the aminonitrile (*Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*, 2022).\\[12\\] This is the strongest natural-sample evidence that Strecker chemistry actually made alanine somewhere in the early Solar System.\n\n**Critiques.**\n- The early atmosphere was probably weakly reducing or neutral (CO2/N2), which sharply lowers HCN and aldehyde production.\\[5\\]\n- The cyanosulfidic network needs locally high HCN and sulfide. Ranjan et al. (arXiv 1801.07725) analyzed sulfidic anion concentrations as an extra local constraint.\\[11\\]\n- Strecker chemistry is aldehyde-based. Extant biochemistry makes alanine from pyruvate. This \"disconnect\" is the main congruence critique raised by Pulletikurti et al. (2022).\\[13\\]\n\n### 2. Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from pyruvate (cyanide + NH3 + CO2)\n\nPulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142\u20131150) showed that \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources to give the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids by the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway (hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates).\\[14\\] The same chemistry gives dihydroorotate from oxaloacetate.\\[15\\] CO2 is mechanistically essential. Krishnamurthy: \"If you mix only the keto acid, cyanide and ammonia, it just sits there. As soon as you add carbon dioxide, even trace amounts, the reaction picks up speed.\"\\[16\\] This route is a bridge: it uses a cyanide feedstock but a metabolic (keto-acid) carbon skeleton.\\[13\\] Its weakness is that it still needs both a pyruvate source and cyanide in the same place. A related paper (Yadav et al. 2022, *Nature Chemistry*) showed cyanide acting as a reductant on TCA intermediates, but pyruvate itself was not reduced.\\[17\\]\\[18\\]\n\n### 3. Reductive amination of pyruvate (metabolism-first / hydrothermal frameworks)\n\n**Chemistry.** Pyruvate + NH3 + 2H\u207a + 2e\u207b \u2192 alanine + H2O, via iminopyruvate.\\[19\\] This is the same transformation alanine dehydrogenase catalyzes with NADH.\\[20\\] The universal competing reaction is reduction of pyruvate to lactate. Product selectivity depends on pH, NH3 concentration and the reductant.\n\n**Iron sulfide / ferrous hydroxide (W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user school).**\n- Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695\u20131697) reported that \"amino acids are formed efficiently by reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids under aqueous conditions with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 and with NH3\u2026 at pH values near their pKa\" (pH ~9\u201310 for NH3).\\[21\\]\n- A secondary summary (Milner-White & Russell 2008, *Biology Direct*) quotes alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine yields up to ~50% at 50 and 75 \u00b0C.\\[22\\] The primary table was not verified here.\n- These experiments used large NH3 excesses. Kaur et al. (2024) characterize earlier Fe-based reductive aminations as using \"375 mM to 1.5 M\" ammonia.\\[23\\]\n- FeS acts as a stoichiometric reductant (oxidized toward pyrite/Fe(III)), not as a true catalyst.\\[19\\]\n\n**Mixed-valence iron hydroxides (green rust; Russell/Barge alkaline-vent school).** Barge et al. (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828\u20134833) reacted pyruvate + NH3 with Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxides:\\[24\\]\n- Alanine formed only when the mineral was partially oxidized. The maximum was at 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III).\\[24\\]\n- Alkaline pH was required. At 75% Fe(II) and pH 7, \"no alanine was produced\", and lactate rose to 30\u201350% after 72 h. Lactate is favored at low pH.\n- The highest yield was at pH 10, Fe(II):Fe(III) = 1:1 and 70 \u00b0C: \"\u223c70%\" alanine with \"\u223c30% lactate\", relative to all organics detected in the liquid phase. At 50 \u00b0C \"the same reactions proceeded but more slowly.\"\n- Neither end member worked: \"Purely ferrous hydroxides did not drive reductive amination but instead promoted pyruvate reduction to lactate, and ferric hydroxides did not result in any reaction.\"\n- The authors place this in iron-rich sediments near alkaline vents, where redox and pH gradients would set alanine/lactate selectivity. They state that \"the precise mechanism of pyruvate reductive amination to alanine in these systems was not established.\"\\[24\\]\\[25\\]\n\n**Electro-reduced FeS and native metals (Kitadai/Nakamura).** Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances* 5:eaav7848):\n- FeS was electrolyzed at \u22120.7 V (vs SHE) for 7 days, producing partial Fe0 (\"FeS_PERM\"). The authors argue such potentials arise naturally in H2-rich alkaline vents.\\[26\\]\n- At pH 9.6 (0.5 M NH3), 5 mM pyruvate and \u03b1-ketoglutarate \"reacted nearly completely\" to alanine and glutamate within 48 h at 23\u201325 \u00b0C.\\[26\\]\n- In a competitive one-pot run (four keto acids, 1.25 mM each), all amino acids formed in >50% yield, with alanine and glutamate favored.\\[27\\]\n- Pure Fe0 was less selective: it also made pyruvate and alanine by oxaloacetate decarboxylation.\\[27\\]\n\n**Ni0/H2 (Moran/Martin school).** Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem* 10:1528\u20131540):\n- Screening conditions: 30 mM pyruvate, 5 bar H2, 22 \u00b0C, pH 7\u20138, 24 h. Ni/SiO2\u2013Al2O3 or Pd/C gave 18\u201340% alanine with 5\u201315% lactate.\\[23\\]\n- At only 6 mM NH3, alanine reached up to 17% with Ni and 44% with Pd (relative to ammonia). This substantially relaxes the ammonia-concentration critique of the Fe-based work.\\[23\\]\n- The Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni) gave 24% alanine and 43% lactate.\\[23\\]\n- The system also runs a reverse-Krebs-cycle analog, making keto acids and amino acids in one pot.\\[28\\]\n- Follow-up work without H2 (Ni at 100 \u00b0C, pH 11, 18 h) aminated 6.4% of pyruvate to alanine.\\[29\\]\n\n**Hydroxylamine + Fe0.** Muchowska, Varma & Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104\u2013107) showed an Fe\u00b2\u207a-promoted network in which pyruvate and glyoxylate build 9 of 11 TCA-cycle intermediates. Adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 gave four biological amino acids, including alanine, by oxime reduction \"in a manner that parallels biosynthesis\".\\[30\\] The prebiotic source of NH2OH is the weak link.\\[26\\]\n\n**Where does the pyruvate come from?** This is the upstream bottleneck for every keto-acid route.\n- Cody et al. (2000, *Science*) made pyruvate from formic acid with nonyl thiol and FeS at 250 \u00b0C and 200 MPa. W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user himself noted the paradox: pyruvate is thermally fragile (it decomposes near 165 \u00b0C).\\[31\\]\n- Preiner et al. (2020, *Nature Ecology & Evolution* 4:534\u2013542) showed greigite (Fe3S4), magnetite (Fe3O4) and awaruite (Ni3Fe) fixing CO2 with H2 at 100 \u00b0C under alkaline conditions. Their abstract reports \"formate (up to 200 mM), acetate (up to 100 \u00b5M), pyruvate (up to 10 \u00b5M), methanol (up to 100 \u00b5M) and methane\". Higher figures quoted in a 2023 *Frontiers in Microbiology* review (560 \u00b5M acetate, 0.3 M formate) do not match this primary abstract.\n- Huber, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user and co-workers made pyruvate from acetylene + CO on NiS above 100 \u00b0C (2020, *Life*/PMC).\\[32\\]\n\nA tens-of-percent amination step fed by micromolar pyruvate gives micromolar alanine at best. Concentrating mechanisms (pores, thermophoresis, mineral adsorption) are invoked but not demonstrated for this specific pair.\n\n### 4. Transamination (pyridoxal and metal ions)\n\nPyridoxal (PL) transaminates amino acids without enzymes, and metal ions strongly modulate this.\\[33\\] Dherbassy, Mayer, Muchowska & Moran (2023, *JACS* 145:13357\u201313370) found cooperative metal\u2013pyridoxal catalysis, strongest with Fe(III) and Al(III) (roughly 180-fold over Fe(III) alone and 90-fold over pyridoxal alone, as quoted in a PMC review). They also found that the mechanism converges on the biological one.\\[34\\] Amino-group transfer from aspartate to pyruvate (giving alanine) proceeds readily, but alanine\u2192aspartate is poor (*Life* 2023, \"Prebiotic Synthesis of Aspartate Using Life's Metabolism as a Guide\").\\[35\\] In Ni/H2 systems (FEBS J), adding pyridoxal raised pyruvate\u2192alanine conversion from 7.6% to 34% at pH 11 and 80 \u00b0C, and up to 48% at 80\u2013100 \u00b0C. Pyridoxamine acts as the nitrogen shuttle and favors amination over lactate formation.\\[36\\] Counterpoint: Zabinski & Toney (2001, *JACS*) found that metal ions inhibit some PLP-catalyzed transaminations, so metal effects depend on the system.\\[37\\]\n\n### 5. Sugar/aldehyde routes without cyanide (Weber's \"triose model\")\n\nWeber (1985, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) made alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium at ambient temperature. A thiol (3-mercaptopropionic acid) made the reaction about six times faster: 0.6% vs 0.1% after 60 days. The proposed path runs through dehydration of glyceraldehyde to a methylglyoxal-type species, a hemithioacetal and an imine, with lactate as a parallel product.\\[38\\] Weber (1998, *OLEB* 28:259\u2013270) then showed that formaldehyde + glycolaldehyde (formose substrates) + NH3 + thiol at pH 5.2 and 40 \u00b0C for 35 days gave 1.8% alanine (on formaldehyde), and essentially none without thiol.\\[39\\] Attractions: no HCN, a link to formose sugars, and thioester (activated) intermediates. Critiques: low yield, month-long timescales, and an organic thiol catalyst whose own prebiotic source must be explained.\n\n### 6. Parent-body and interstellar synthesis (delivery framework)\n\n- **Parent-body aqueous chemistry.** Kebukawa et al. (2017, *Science Advances*) heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and ammonia (90\u2013250 \u00b0C) and made \u03b1-, \u03b2- and \u03b3-amino acids together with insoluble organic matter.\\[40\\] Koga & Naraoka (2017, *Scientific Reports*) made glycine, alanine and hydroxy amino acids from formaldehyde/acetaldehyde/NH3 at 60 \u00b0C and pH 11.5.\\[41\\] In their Murchison sample, \"the second most abundant amino acid was alanine (1.7 ppm) with a relative enrichment of L-alanine\". That L-enrichment conflicts with the racemic results discussed below.\n- **Interstellar ice photolysis.** UV-irradiated ice analogues give racemic amino acids, including alanine (Bernstein et al. 2002 and Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, *Nature* 416).\n- **Returned samples.** Bennu samples (Glavin, Dworkin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199\u2013210) contain 33 amino acids, 14 of them proteinogenic. The total is ~70 nmol/g, ~3.5\u00d7 less than Murchison, and alanine is racemic.\\[42\\]\\[43\\] The authors interpret the high glycine and racemic \u03b1-amino acids as consistent with HCN-polymerization/Strecker-type chemistry in an ammonia-rich parent body.\\[42\\]\n\nDelivery supplies alanine without terrestrial geochemical constraints. It is racemic, though, and quantitative impact survival and delivery fluxes are uncertain.\n\n### 7. Thermodynamics and stability (hydrothermal frameworks)\n\nAmend & Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659\u20131662) calculated that net alanine synthesis from CO2, NH4\u207a and H2 in a 100 \u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution releases 12.12 kJ/mol. Synthesis is exergonic there but endergonic in cold oxidized surface seawater.\\[44\\] This supports the idea that H2-rich hydrothermal mixing zones thermodynamically favor alanine. The kinetic counterpoint: Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009, *OLEB*) found that at 175 \u00b0C glycine and alanine peak after ~75 h and then decompose by deamination/decarboxylation.\\[45\\] Miller & Bada (1988, *Nature* 334:609\u2013611) argued that vent temperatures \"would not allow synthesis of organic compounds, but would decompose them, unless the exposure time at vent temperatures was short.\"\\[46\\] Note that the most productive keto-acid experiments (Barge, Kitadai, Kaur) run at 22\u201370 \u00b0C, well below black-smoker temperatures. That partly answers the critique for off-axis alkaline systems, but not for high-temperature (>150 \u00b0C) pyruvate formation steps like Cody et al.\n\n### 8. The \"L\" problem\n\nAll routes above give racemic alanine. The evidence on natural enantioenrichment:\n- **Murchison.** Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature* 389:265\u2013268) reported an L-alanine excess ([L \u2212 D]/[L + D]) of ~33%. They argued it was extraterrestrial on the basis of \"nearly equal, extraterrestrial \u03b415N values measured for each alanine enantiomer\". Pizzarello & Cronin (1998, *Nature* 394:236) found interior Murchison alanine racemic. They attributed the excess to exterior contamination or incomplete chromatographic resolution.\\[47\\] Glavin et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) found Murchison alanine \"both racemic (D \u2248 L) and heavily enriched in 13C, indicating no measurable terrestrial alanine contamination\" (16.5 nmol of racemic alanine in a 1 g extract). In the same samples glutamic acid showed ~15\u201339% L-excess.\n- **Bennu.** Alanine is racemic (2025).\\[48\\]\\[49\\]\n- **Circularly polarized light.** Asymmetric photolysis of racemic alanine films (Meinert et al. 2014, *Angewandte Chemie*) and CPL irradiation of ice analogues (Modica et al. 2014, *ApJ* 788:79; up to |1.34|% ee) induce only small, helicity- and wavelength-dependent excesses.\\[50\\]\\[51\\]\\[52\\]\\[53\\]\n\nSo no published route produces L-alanine selectively. An amplification mechanism (crystallization, autocatalysis, chiral templating, or later selection by chiral catalysts) has to be added to any route.\\[54\\] This is common to all frameworks and is not specific to alanine.\n\n## Catalysts and surfaces: what is actually shown\n\n- **No catalyst needed:** the Strecker steps (imine formation, cyanide addition, nitrile hydrolysis under acid or base) and Bucherer\u2013Bergs (needs CO2/bicarbonate as reagent, not a mineral catalyst). Spark discharge supplies the energy for HCN and aldehyde formation.\n- **Mineral or metal reductants/catalysts for pyruvate \u2192 alanine:**\n  - freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)2 (stoichiometric)\n  - mixed-valence green rust (Fe(II):Fe(III) \u2248 1:1)\n  - electro-reduced FeS / Fe0\n  - native Fe0 with NH2OH\n  - Ni0 or Pd with H2 (catalytic in metal, H2 as reductant)\n  - Ni-bearing meteorite metal\n- **Upstream C\u2013C bond formation (CO2 \u2192 pyruvate):** greigite, magnetite, awaruite (with H2); FeS with thiols at high T/P; NiS with CO/acetylene.\n- **Organocatalysts/cofactors:** thiols (Weber); pyridoxal with Fe(III)/Al(III) (transamination); Cu(I)/HS\u207b photoredox (cyanosulfidic).\n\n## Recommendations for synthesis-network design\n\n1. **Model pyruvate \u21c4 alanine as a reversible, multi-catalyst hub.** It is the single best-supported step across frameworks: Fe minerals, Ni/H2, NH2OH/Fe0, pyridoxal transamination, and Bucherer\u2013Bergs with cyanide. Always include lactate as the competing sink. Make selectivity a function of pH (alkaline favors alanine), NH3 activity and reductant redox state.\n2. **Treat the C3 source as the rate-limiting, framework-defining choice.** Use either acetaldehyde + HCN (Strecker; needs a reducing atmosphere or local HCN/sulfide) or CO2/H2 \u2192 pyruvate (\u00b5M yields on Fe/Ni minerals). Do not assume high pyruvate concentrations without an explicit concentrating mechanism.\n3. **Use realistic NH3.** Fe-mineral yields come from 0.1\u20131.5 M NH3. Only the Ni/H2 (Kaur 2024) and pyridoxal-shuttle data show useful yields near 5\u20136 mM. Flag any pathway that needs molar NH3.\n4. **Keep amination steps \u2264~100 \u00b0C.** The best amination data are at 22\u201375 \u00b0C. Above ~150 \u00b0C, alanine decomposes on timescales of days.\n5. **Add an explicit, separate enantioenrichment module.** No feedstock route provides it, and claims of natural L-alanine excess are not reliable.\n\n## Caveats and contested points\n\n- **Hydrothermal vs surface.** Contested. Vent chemistry has thermodynamic support (Amend & Shock) and mild-temperature experimental support (Barge, Kitadai, Kaur). It is still criticized for dilution, decomposition at high T, and reliance on lab-prepared minerals, electrochemical potentials or 5 bar H2. Surface cyanide chemistry is criticized for needing reducing atmospheres or local HCN/sulfide/UV, and for incongruence with keto-acid biochemistry.\n- **Yields not verified against primary tables.** The Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) ~50% figure is from a secondary review. Kitadai's alanine is described as \"nearly complete\" (exact percentage in their Table S1). Miller's alanine figure is given relative to glycine; the commonly quoted absolute value (~1.7% of carbon) was not confirmed from the 1955 *JACS* table.\n- **Yield definitions differ** (percent of keto acid, of NH3, of carbon), so cross-route comparisons in the table are indicative only.\n- **The NASA/JPL press release** on Barge et al. described the chemistry as \"reproducing origins of life on the ocean floor\"; that framing outruns the data, which show one reaction step.\\[55\\]\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [The 1953 Stanley L. Miller Experiment: Fifty Years of Prebiotic ...](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1023/A:1024807125069.pdf)\n2. [A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.abh3981)\n3. [One of the foremost experiments of the 20th century - Revista M\u00e8tode](https://metode.org/issues/monographs/one-of-the-foremost-experiments-of-the-20th-century.html)\n4. [M\u00e8tode Science Studies Journal ISSN: 2174-3487 metodessj@uv.es](https://www.redalyc.org/pdf/5117/511754471026.pdf)\n5. <https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Johnsonetal2008.pdf>\n6. [Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 ...](https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/691/analytical/PDF/Parker2011.pdf)\n7. [PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY Enhanced Synthesis of Alkyl Amino Acids in Miller\u2019s](https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Parker2011b.pdf)\n8. [PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY A Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral](https://www.bio.miami.edu/dana/dox/Cleaves2008.pdf)\n9. [The first peptides: the evolutionary transition between prebiotic amino acids and early proteins](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.2832)\n10. [Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism.](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Common-origins-of-RNA,-protein-and-lipid-precursors-Patel-Percivalle/3b482858daca31713acd680830b446c96e0ee8d5)\n11. [Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth