cker and hydrolysis references to the corresponding reaction entries.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.87\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.66,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.93,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support CO2-dependent conversion of alpha-ketoacids to amino acids using cyanide and ammonia. The deterministic check establishes that this reaction is balanceable; it is not classified as a conservation violation. Nevertheless, this lumped entry leaves the operative intermediates, final hydrolysis conditions and carbon/nitrogen accounting insufficiently specified. Listing ammonia and CO2 on both sides does not establish their mechanistic or net stoichiometric roles.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Provide explicit experimentally justified coefficients and distinguish required initial CO2 from any CO2 evolved during the transformation.\",\n        \"Identify the hydantoin/N-carbamoyl sequence described in the supplied research, either through intermediate nodes or a documented lumped mechanism.\",\n        \"Specify pH, temperature, reagent concentrations and the conditions that release free alanine rather than merely an alanine precursor.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.93,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.96,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.66,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.97,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"Native-iron reductive amination is experimentally motivated by Kitadai et al. (2019). Explicit Fe0 consumption 