sible Fe0 separately from the supporting sulfide matrix.\",\n        \"Document the experimental benchmark of approximately pH 9.6, 0.5 M ammonia, 23\u201325 degrees C and 48 hours, or justify alternative conditions.\",\n        \"Distinguish the laboratory preparation history from any proposed environmental preparation; do not imply regeneration unless it is explicitly modeled.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.96\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.95,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.97,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.98,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.89,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) provide strong experimental support for supported-Ni/H2 reductive amination at near-neutral pH and low ammonia concentration. The explicit H2 reductant and competing lactate branch are appropriate. Cool pressurized cavities are compatible in principle with the experimental temperature and hydrogen pressure, but the network does not specify hydrogen partial pressure, catalyst loading or gas retention. This is the strongest implementation as documented, not because of its feedstock identity or network position.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the supported-Ni material, loading, approximately 22 degrees C temperature, pH 7\u20138, hydrogen partial pressure and reaction duration.\",\n        \"Explain how the cavity maintains the required H2 supply; hydrostatic pressure alone does not establish a 5 bar H2 partial pressure.\",\n        \"Preserve the reported yield denominator: the approximately 17% low-ammonia Ni result is relative to ammonia, not automatically to pyruvate.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.96\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.95,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Ni-bearing meteorite experiments summarized from Kaur et al. (2024) support this implementation. The network correctly treats it as a catalyst variant of reductive amination rather than a separate mechanism. However, evidence for the tested Aletai material does not establish equivalent activity for an unspecified Fe-Ni alloy. Alloy composition, surface state and hydrogen conditions remain unresolved. Including lactate is particularly important because it was a substantial product in the cited meteorite experiment.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify alloy composition, mineral phases, surface preparation and loading, and identify whether the material matches the tested meteorite or represents an extrapolation.\",\n        \"Provide H2 partial pressure, pH, temperature and residence time, with evidence for any departure from the reported experiment.\",\n        \"Use alloy-specific alanine/lactate selectivity rather than transferring supported-Ni performance to the alloy.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.95,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.96,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.98,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.88,\n      \"feedback\": \"Oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction is chemically coherent and supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). The reduction correctly supplies four electrons per oxime through two Fe0 equivalents per alanine. External hydroxylamine is an acceptable declared feedstock, so its upstream synthesis is not required here. The remaining uncertainty concerns reaction conditions, reagent lifetime and whether oxime formation is staged before metal addition.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify pH, temperature, concentrations and durations for both oxime formation and reduction, including the order of reagent addition.\",\n        \"Document hydroxylamine persistence over the chosen residence time and distinguish productive oxime reduction from competing reagent consumption.\",\n        \"Quantify Fe0 loading and retain ferrous hydroxide precipitation only as a saturation-dependent branch.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.91,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.66,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n      \"feedback\": \"Weber (1985) supports thiol-assisted alanine formation from glyceraldehyde and ammonium. The net equation is balanceable without an additional external reductant, consistent with internal redox rearrangement of the sugar substrate. Low yield does not invalidate production of L-alanine within the racemate. However, the catalyst identity and loading, reaction conditions and substrate persistence over the approximately 60-day experimental timescale are not supplied.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Identify the thiol, including whether it is the experimentally tested 3-mercaptopropionic acid, and declare its supplied inventory and loading.\",\n        \"Specify the glyceraldehyde experiment's actual pH, temperature, concentrations and duration rather than borrowing conditions from the separate formaldehyde/glycolaldehyde experiment.\",\n        \"Document competing lactate formation and sugar degradation when describing expected product inventories.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.92\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.91,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.69,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Weber (1998) directly supports thiol-dependent alanine formation from formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and ammonia. The lumped stoichiometry is acceptable, although it does not describe the intervening carbon-carbon bond formation and rearrangements. The reported pH 5.2, 40 degrees C and 35-day experiment is compatible with a protected surface pool in principle. Those conditions and the required catalyst inventory are not explicitly assigned to the submitted reaction.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify thiol identity and loading, feedstock concentrations, pH, temperature and residence time.\",\n        \"Explain retention of volatile formaldehyde and persistence of reactive aldehydes throughout the proposed reaction interval.\",\n        \"Retain the reported approximately 1.8% yield on a formaldehyde basis and identify the intermediate sequence in the reaction reasoning.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.62,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.96,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Aspartate-to-pyruvate amino-group transfer is balanced and supported by the nonenzymatic transamination literature, including Dherbassy et al. (2023). Racemic aspartate and an achiral catalytic system are 